      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ILLUSTRATED FASHION S.</head>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED FASHION S.</p>
        <p>Winter now reigns supreme, and we turn gratefully to furs and thick garments. The fashions for this season of the year are by now fully declared, and may he aptly described as "furry." Cloth gowns have rough, hairy sur- faces, and are liberally trimmed with fur, while the smartest hats and toques are composed of lace and fur cunningly intermingled. The smartest winter gowns are the most simple, and are devoid of any fussiness of detail: and to those who hesitate as to which of the latest modes to follow I should counsel to restrict their inclinations, and allow their toilettes to be distinguished by a quiet sim- plicity rather than any ultra-fashionableness. Tartans are coming into favour, but are, of course, rather marked, and if a dress has to wear more than one season, a plain material should be selected. Nearly all coats have some</p>
        <p>pretenecions to a basque, some consisting merely of small coat toile, while othery extend quite half-way down the skirt. A new style of coat for tailor-made gowns is a sort of Newmarket, with a basque ornamented with large, Eat gold buttons.</p>
        <p>The pretty coat depicted is of blAck velvet, and boasts a short basgte. The wide collar is edged with black, -curled ostrich feather trim- ming, while a line of silk braid is appliqukl as a finish. The cuffs of the coat are loose, and bordered with the feather trimming, while an ornamental belt confines the waist. A smart little coat like this can be worn with any sort of skirt and always looks dressy and stylish, besides being warm and cosy, though not so heavy as fur. Indeed, one of the seeding tailors has evinced a strong preference for the •velvet coat for winter wear over and above all others.</p>
        <p>Although the Lasque is one of this season's novelties and much in evidence, the long-lived bolero is still well worn, for there is a certain style of figure to which it is eminently becoming Indeed, it is, perhaps, more in favour with the Parisienne than any other, and is worn either closely buttoned up in front, cut in the simplest and severest lines, or opening in front to dis- play a dainty vest of kid or light-coloured cloth, beautifully embroidered. The short skirt, devoid of all pretensions to a train, is also much in favour with our sisters across the Channel, and is really much more sensible for outdoor wear during the worst of the winter.</p>
