      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>AN EVENING TOILETTE.</head>
        <p>AN EVENING TOILETTE.</p>
        <p>The dainty evening toilette sketched is verj simple, being made of cream nun's veiling trimmed with ivory tinted lace. The front of the decolletage ,is draped with a flounce of lace. while a &amp;nu and long depending ends grace the left side. The sleeves are very loose and are semi-transparent, finishing in a lace cuff. The skirt, which is slightly trained (as, indeed, should be all those intended for house wear), is covered with countless tiny tucks, interspersed with bands of lace insertion.</p>
        <p>This gown could be expressed in almost any material in this style with success. A. thin black gauze or chiffon, with creamy lace of a fine quality, would make a charming effect, Black is generally becoming, and stands any amount of wear, although chiffon is not a material I can conscientiously recommend fot hard wear, and .a good strong net will stand much more knocking about. _ _</p>
        <p>In the world of millinery, plumage of birds plays a very prominent part, in spite of the loud protests of many conscientious-minded persons. It does seem cruel, one must confess, that so many innocent little lives should be sacrificed for our outward adornment, but at the same time there are few women who have ever looked much into the matter who believe that the wings and entire birds even are verily</p>
        <p>er I/P -"- t 4404,r,:4 .••••-72.iti • it* - • •••,c/0.0:,•- . • r 4ett V.' \NNE—ipr. , e,O. , , .A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FASHIONABLE TOQUE.</head>
        <p>A FASHIONABLE TOQUE.</p>
        <p>the little songsters of the grove whose fate we deplore. A little attention will soon convince us to the contrary, for the domestic sparrow and the homely barnyard fowl play the leading part in the wonderfully tinted feather monstrosities which appear on many hats. The toque sketched, however, is indebted principally to fur for its embellishment, as it is made entirely from sable, and is only trimmed with a twist of white tulle which concludes in a chou at one side.</p>
        <p>The upstanding aigrette gives style, while a buckle of an ornamental character catches the twist oC tulle. A slight tilt is given to the toque by a bandeau, cone :led by a bunch of shaded parma violets and a little foliage. Fur is much used for trimming all kinds of hats, and the picturesque variety of hat is some- times trimmed with a whole sable. the head pointing, to the front and the tail and paws hanging down behind. Large round flat toques are very fashionable, and are worn well forward, shading the forehead. The prettiest toques are generally made of draped zebeline or velvet, while the brim is ornamented with rich embroidery. The coiffure with this style of toque generally is worn dressed high, although for evening wear the low coiffure is preferable. Somehow, dressing the hair high looks more chic with the flat style of toque. In either case, side combs and tOrtoiseshell clasps are necessary adjuncts.</p>
        <p>COQUETTE,</p>
        <p>[Au, RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>A FLOt 71,1103,T THE EXAMINER FRI!) \ NOVEMBER 21 1902</p>
        <p>imam, naa eviaentiy been purcnasea as a hand establishment establishment and were both frayed and musty as well as badly fitting, and being originally of the dress-suit order gave him the appearance of a broken-down waiter at a poor restaurant. He was not a wholesome-looking object while he wolfed his steak as a dog might devour its meal; the steak was badly cut and indifferently cooked, and what with the intense heat of the atmosphere, and the perspiration running from his forehead and cheeks in dirty rivulets, his dirty hands helping the fork, ignorant, low-bred, square-pointed fingers, with the unclipped nails in deepest mourning, an,d, added to this, that vacuous mourn, with the slobbering lips taking it in with such evident unction, and the solitary, unwashed tooth emphasizing that moist unctiou, rendered this hypnotic disciple of the Borderland lore more gruesome than pleasant. He was exactly the kind of man who might like to see pigs killed in his boyhood, and who would develop either into a sneak or a spy in his manhood, original or energetic he never could be. As he sat, he scratched his head sometimes with his dirty nail, sometimes with his greasy fork. He also fumbled suggestively about his half-opened waistcoat as one is used to see tramps do when they fancy themselves unobserved. Altogether, this Sydney interpreter of " Border land " love gave one the decided impression of that drawing in Punch. "Two years ago I used your soap, since then I have used no other." Harry Furness's model was attenuated and unkempt ; but fancy a professor of mystic lore in a badly-fitting waiter's dress suit, the knees baggy and the vest overtight, and the angry buttons threatening to sever; burly as a friar of old, and soft as a retired bantam boxer,' with a vacuous countenance, and crow-like eyes, always on the giggle, and you have the professor, or ancient wizarsi, up to date. Supplement this with the awful heat of a Sydney mid-summer day, an under-done steak cut in Colonial fashion, and cooked ditto, the natural perspiration and fumes of an unwashable tramp dressed up in an old dress suit, and you may realize the picture of this high priest of Om temple of mystery without further description. He had just finkhed his gorge and imbibed his last drop ot Colonial "..ocanky," when the hand- maid popped in her frowSled head and shedding a tiny shower of pearl powder, which after all sweetened the apartment a little, announced in brassy tones :</p>
        <p>"Are ye done, perfesser, for there's a laydy awaiting ter consult 3-er." The professor bundled his plates and beer-mug to a side tabie, where he covered them with an old newspaper ; then, dusting the crumbs aside with his hand, and wiping his hands again on the tail of his coat, he produced a greasy pack of cards and said with calm dignity : " Yes Matilder, show the party in." The party entered, a young woman of about twenty-five, fashionably dressed and according to the season, in vitgin white, with a Donna-like bunch of snowy ostrich feathers in her hat, and a cloud about 11:ir fs c:&gt;, She was slim-built and graceful in figure, and the professor, who loved the fair sex, ttok notice that she had a wealth of golden brown hair under the drooping feathers. "Sit down my dear," he said, with a smirk on his sweaty and greasy red face, and the party sat and silently looked at him through her c/oud. "Is it your fortune ye want to know? By the cards or by the hand? Or are you in any other trouble that a man of my experience can help you? Girls will be girls, you know my dear; therefore you may safely give me your confidence, for it won't be abused."</p>
        <p>He bared his lonely tooth and regarded her with a smile, while his crow-like vacant eyes tried to get behind the cloud.</p>
        <p>The visitor laughed softly, yet enjoyably, then after a pause she replied, removing her white gloves at the same time. "No, old man, I don't want any confidence business, I only want you to tell me my fortune by my hands and by the cards." "It's unlucky they say to do both at one sitting, yet, if you turn round and go to the door and come back again, that will break the charm. Which will you have first?" " My hand, professor, tell me what I'm doing, and what I'm going to do, and then I can ask yon other questions when we come to the cards." She held out her two hands, palms upward to him; while he took a magnifying glass, and after giving himself a preliminary hitch and scratch, he stooped over them to examine. Shapely hands they were, with tapering long fingers and fleshy palms which had hard and well defined lines running over them. _ _</p>
        <p>- "You are married," the professor said after a pause. "Well, what of that?" "No more than you think of it yourself, my dear. You have had some troubles in the past, and had some adventures; but your marriage has never been a happy one, there are no children, and you'd like to be free of your bond." " Yes, and shall I?" "I see a death here, and a little trouble, but your line of life is clear." "Shall I be married again ? " "Yes, and have three children, with much pros- perity in the future." "That'll do, when will it come off?"</p>
        <p>"How old are you" "Twenty six." "You'll be married again, before you are twenty eight." Budgerrie for you, old man, now let's try the cards."</p>
        <p>She rose uprightly arid went to the door, returning in a moment while the professor arranged his cards.</p>
        <p>"There's an old fellow waiting for his fortune at the door, professor, will you take him or me first she asked as she returned.</p>
        <p>"Ladies first, of course," answered the professor gallantly again, baring his solitary tooth. "Drive ahead, then, for I am in a hurry," said the young woman curtly. "Shuffle and cut." - -</p>
        <p>After she had done so, he began to read : "There is a dark man here close beside you, your husband I should say, but you turn your back on him—you take hearts, do you not?" "Yes," replied the young woman. "There's a diamond man facing you, that is the one you fancy." " Go on."</p>
        <p>"You'll get that diamond man, but the club man will cause you some trouble, there is a death here."</p>
        <p>"To the club man?" eagerly. "I don't know yet—shuffle again and I'll tell you.,, Thrice lid the cloud-veiled woman, with the strong whn,. hands shuffle the cards, and then he answered hr curiosity and desires. "Yes the club man will die, and the diamond man is the winner."</p>
        <p>"Thank heaven for that," cried the woman fervently as she paid him his fee and went forth, brushing by his whitehaired customer who was waiting his turn. She did not look at the whitehaired customer and she was too closely veiled for any one to see her features, yet he glanced after her through his blue glasses in a curious way as she went out of the doorway, then he sought the professor. A tall and singularly powerful looking man he was, this second visitor, dressed in thin grey, smooth faced and with a shock of white hair that fell about his shoulders after the style of the Ex-premier of New South Wales; indeed, for a moment the professor thought that this cele- brated politician must have shaved and mounted blue glasses as a sort of disguise, but he soon recovered hinisself, with this assurance gained from knowledge that this eminent man would .never sink his personality, no matter what position he chose to take up, therefore he became composed and ready for his visitor. " I want my future read," said the stranger. "By the palm, or by the cards?" "The hand will do." He stretched a sinewy hand out to the professor's magnifying glass. "You've experienced troubles in the past." Yes." "But you have a singularly open and confiding as well as a generous nature. I may say that you are</p>
        <p>a man who has been Imposed upon by raise 11-Asraut, and may be again if you are not particularly care- ful—you-- " " What a confounded old fraud you are to be sure, Jeremiah Judge." The stranger as he uttered these words, his left hand still lying under the professor's magnify- ing glass, removed with his right hand the blue spectacles and snowy wig, and revealed a hard faced but handsome young man, with short cropped black hair and glittering black eyes. "Fancy me being sucked in by any cove." The Professor fell back in his chair confounded, his crow-blue eyes almost starting out of his head as he uttered the feeble cry : " Jack Milton, by George."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER 11. A CONFIDENTIAL CONVERSATION.</p>
        <p>"Yes, you genteel old humbug, I've come back once more to do some busißess in Sydney." , As he spoke, Jack Milton, as he had just been called, leaned back in his chair and regarded the phenmatologist with a benevolent, yet contemp- tuous air of patronage. "Still trying your best in your small way to alo the public, I see, Jeremiah, and doing it shabby at that, per usual." The phsychometrist got upon his shambling legs, with a gushing air ot welcome illuminating his streaming face, and parting his vacuous mouth. "My dear, tear boy, wno-cr nave expected to see you here, I thought your time wasn't due for another twelvemonth, how have you done it?" "Oh, I behaved myself and kidded the sky-clark, therefore got my ticket." " What was the little item?"</p>
        <p>"A mere trifle in the past, something I did in Melbourne long ago, and which I had forgotten or I should not have gone there quite so openly, but the 'Pecks' remembered me and laid me up by the heels for a spell; however, it wasn't an unmixed evil, for being in lavender gave me the repose that 1 required, and put the Sydney sentries quite off my track." "Good—very good," chuckled the Professor. rubbing his hands together and gazing admiringly on the powerful figure before him, "You'll find hosts of friends who are glad to see Jack Milton back amongst them again, and none more so than your humble servant. While the old humbug was speaking, the young max carefully replaced his white wig and blue spectacles, and became once more a kind of 'bene- volent replica of a clean shaven Sir Henry Parkes. "I have made an appointment with my lawyer, to meet me here, Jeremiah—or as you call your- self now, Professor Mortikali, I thought it safer than at his own office." "Much safer and more secluded."</p>
        <p>"So I thought." "But how did you find me out Jack, eh ?" "Well, you are not exactly the kind of coon to give an agent much trouble, if he wanted to get on your tracks • I knew you'd be up to some busi- ness of this kind where spirits or mesmerism had a show in it."</p>
        <p>"Call it hypnotism Jack, or psychometry." "Yes, that's exactly how I spotted you, old boy, I looked out all the sign-boards, as I came along, for the most jaw-cracking words. I knew your weakness for that sort of thing. Palmist or Futurist would not be good enough for you, there- fore when I came to Professor Mortikali : Psycho- metrist, Plienmatologist, &amp;c., I felt sure I had run my fox to earth, and I wasn't far out." "No, Jack, you'd make a first-rate detective, if you wasn't better than that, a first-class "-- " Crib-cracker, eh ! It takes a thief to catch a thief, but I don't belong to that race who can utilise their experience to snare their own kind. I have always been and always shall be grit to my pals—ls this much of a tack? " He looked about him with a little disgust, and at the shabby professor with a humorous air of pity. "Fairly," observed the psychometrist. "1 holds my own, this is the slack time of the day, but at night they comes up wonderful, considering." "You haven't got a fashionable class of custo- mers I can see, or you'd be better togged up. Never mind, I'll put you up to something good before many days are over our heads." "No, Jack, no ! I'd rather not," cried the professor nervously. "I don't mind helping you when the game is safe, but you are so reckless, my dear boy, and don't at all consider personal safety. Now you see, I'm doing small things, and the police don't tou4 me at 'ern, but one never knows where he'd land, if this business spread out into many more new branches." " Bah ! the business I'm going to set you up in is strictly in your own line, psychometry and the other P.H's. You're too cramped here. You want bigger and more fashionable premises and to get yourself togged up more like an orthodox medical professor, and less like a broken down waiter, and I've just spotted the shop that'll suit you to a nicety. Do you know that place where 'Brisco' the jewellers used to be, in George Street." "With the bank on one side and the pawn- brokers on the other?" _ _. -</p>
        <p>_ "Exactly, would these diggings suit you to open as branch establishment of the Faith Healing and Fortune Telling fake ? " "What d'ye mean Jack, Milton? d'ye know them two premises will cost in rent and taxes near to a thousand a year?" " Well !"</p>
        <p>"And the fixing of them up properly, with furniture, carpets and sofas, &amp;c., &amp;c., will come to nigh five hundred quid." "You've hit it pretty nearly, I should say, to do it properly," replied Jack, calmly. "You'll want some attractive looking girls with a little more, style than this one here, and some respect- able toggery for yourself. Yes, you'll need all that money, and more, perhaps, till business comes in." "Yes, and who's going to pay for all this autlay ?" "I will, my Sage Psychometrist, I think there are vast possibilities in this business of yours, if properly worked in this city, and as I happen to have the spondulonx, or will have, if my lawyer, who'll be here presently, isn't a fool as well as a rogue; I'm going to make a boom in the prediction business. We'll take in the national sports and give the juggings' tips from spirit-land ; we'll pitch our placards and bills about like snow flakes and make the pressmen our serfs by advertising. But you must try to acquire the art of washing yourself and changing your linen at least three times a week while the hot weather lasts or it'll be all bunkum. Do' you savy, my odoriferous psychometrist, don't you see what I mean? Plenty of water, soap, fresh linen' and a trifle of Jockey Club or Cherry Blossom, for the sake of business, also a nail brush and a little attention to the nails, at present in deepest mourning for your rubbishy sins which arn't worth so mach' respect, lemons also are first-class articles for cleanin the hands and nails., and the Sydney waterworks are lavish with their supply." "I ain't at all averse to washing in the warm weather," observed the Professor with an injured air. "I likewise likes a frequent change of shirts and collars, and feel kindly drawn to clerical neck. clothes if I has the articles to work on." "You'll have them, meantime I want you to keep out your customers, or read their fortunes at your outside counter this afternoon, while my man of business is engaged with me, and we'll arrange all that afterwards. By Jove you'd be a great man professor, if you had a fair Chance, and I'm going to give you that chance." "Will ye have anything to drink now?" said the Professor respectfully, for Jack Milton had spoken to him in the lordly manner of one who has means at command, and the sneak reverenced him accordingly. • "Not at present, I have a lawyer to talk to and I bet he won't imbibe until he has finished this interview, and neither will I. I say, do you know anything about my wife ?" "I don't know exactly where she lives, of course, but I have seen her."</p>
        <p>"Lately ! " "-Yes, just recently, I may say." "Yes—yes, and is she looking well—my Rosa, my darling." A wonderful change took place in the disguised house-breaker as he asked the questions; he was no longer cynical nor supercilous, but eager and boyish, while by contrast the Professor seemed to become ill at ease and constrained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PROFESSOR OF TI-I E BLACK ART,</head>
        <p>A PROFESSOR OF TI-I E BLACK ART,</p>
        <p>HUME NISBET,</p>
        <p>(Author of" Bail Up," dm)</p>
        <p>PART I.</p>
        <p>GRAFTER I. THE DEN OF THE MODERN WIZARD.</p>
        <p>Professor Mortikali sat in his inner sanctum waiting for customers. It was a hot day, during the early portion of the month of January, 1895, and although the Profes- sor had all his blinds drawn down, and occupied the coolest corner of the Arcade, still he could not shut out those intense waves of Sydney heat that swept in between the crevices of the doors and windows; although he managed to shut out a good deal of the intense light. The professor liked shadow for he was a modern wizard, and his business did not require much light; indeed, the less light that was thrown upon it the better it was conducted; therefore it was not often that the green venetian blinds were drawn up. The professor was at present resting upon his oars, for it was the slack hour of the day; the hour when no one, unless absolutely forced to come out, would care to face the terrible sun-glare of mid- day. The professor was of that peculiar craft which flourished so much during the earlier centuries, and has more or less flourished ever since under various disguises. He belonged to the tribe of the witch of Endor, that profession of seers and foretellers whom King Saul tried to put down in his vigorous and virtuous years, and afterwards meekly consulted in his decline; the same craft which that modern Solomon, King James the First of Englana, so rigorously hunted to death, and which might have died naturally but for the efforts of the Pschycological Society, and the able editor of "Border Land" and discoverer of the fourth dimension.</p>
        <p>It is not a profitable profession in merry old England, where the police imitate the tactics of Kings Saul and James, and take as much delight in making raids as lively terriers do in hunting rabbits; but in progressive New South Wales, where convict laws still hold sway, and men are hanged for attempted murder, while judges dictatt to jurymen, as the celebrated Jeffreys used to do ; where even the judges themselves consult the witches, fortune-telling and witchcraft thrives wonderfully, even in the midst of the universal depression which has of late fallen upon the solonies.</p>
        <p>In the Sydney of to-day you may see, amongst other closed places of business, the shutters up on many public-houses and bars. The reason of this is that they have been raided by the police, because the proprietors have been selling poison, undiluted, lto their customers, and although the colonial stomach can stand a good deal in the way of vitriol and blue stone, yet when the landlord finally omits to give his customers even the flavour of brandy, rum, or whiskey, then his fate as a landlord is decided. They are a proud and conservative race, the New South Welshmen; they cannot stand the slightest approach to a joke about theiplcountry. You may abuse England or London as much as you are disposed (to an Englishman) and he will only laugh unctuously, but you must not take the same liberty with a New South Welshman. His har- bour is the most beautiful harbour in the world. If you wimit this, yet suggest that the buildings might be just a little more classic, he will grow pale with passion and cut you dead. If you ven- ture to hint that morality in its msthetic quality is not so strictly observed amongst the politicians and tradesmen as it might be, he will consider it time to regard you as a dangerous person and a fit sub- ject for the martial law, that hangs first and tries afterwards, and is still in such active force in that sun-laved colony. They aim to be very high toned in Sydney, and imitate as nearly as they can the manners of fashionable London. Therefore, if an accomplished swindler or thief comes amongst them and knows his business thoroughly, he is nearly sure to be suc- useful ; for the veneer being all that they aim at Ihemselves, so are they satisfied with it in their visitors. In Victoria and Queensland the inhabi- tants are less conservative and much clearer- headed. Professor Mortikali was a wonderful man in his way, and pursued various branches as a livelihood. On his brass plate was written, "Professor Morti- kali; Psychometrist, Phenmatologist, Futurist, and Migneto-Electric Healer." He called his establishment the Egyptiae Mystic Hall and Health Sanatorium, and had on his bills a wide range of subjects, from character delineating by phrenology and physiognomy, fortune-telling by cards, palmistry or astrology, with the art of healing all diseases by hypnotic treatment. There are hundreds in the same line of business throughout Sydney, and all apparently flourishing more or less, despite the dull times. In the Arcade were three rivals, while along the principal streets every fifth or sixth shop is a futurist or an astrologer, with all the paraphernalia exhibited in the windows which one expects to witness in the windows of a wizard.</p>
        <p>In the newspapers also of this enlightened and conservative country, along with singular gems of poetry, in the form of memoriam verses, you may read any day, advertisements like the following: " Wanted, a loving, clairvoyant, test lady, with means preferred, as life partner by Magnetic Healer, &amp;c.," and yet no one laughs either at the verses or the advertisements, they have become so accustomed to both. But they get terribly vicious if any stranger attempts to criticise their politicians or their professionals. Professor Mortikali had his front window artfully set out. A plaster cast of the hand of Deeming, the murderer, with casts and photographs of actors, clergymen, and other distinct types were displayed. A phrenological head stood on a stucco pillar, and the complete skeleton of a baby dangled in a glass case, specimens of snakes and tape worms were coiled in bottles, or dried and stuffed and grouped about, while the invariable alli- gator which Hogarth has accustomed us to in his pictures of the quack was likewise displayed. A curtain, half-drawn back from the doorway, revealed the image of a gipsy holding out her hand for silver, while at a little counter sat an attractive-looking girl, costumed in black velvet, scarlet facings, and glittering metallic discs, such as we were used to gaze on with wonder at " fairs " in our youthful days. There was no attempt at advancement or dressing up-to-date in this estab- lishment. The old-fashioned arrangements appeared to answer the purpose perfectly, the same amount of rouge and powder on the face of this attractive handmaid to the wizard, the generous display of snowy neck, and the usual surroundings of curtains, herbs and stuffed monstrosities, they I were all there, while inside waited the futurist and faith healer.</p>
        <p>At this precise hour of midday the professor and his handmaid were both similarly occupied in dis- cussing their lunch. Her's was a light refection, as befitted a Sydney nymph of her light and powdery nature, a couple of tarts and an ice-cream, which she had procured at the next door. The ice she had finished first, before it became quite liquid, and now she was leisurely discussing the fruit and pastry. Inside his sanctum the professor was having a heavier meal, as befitted the nerve tear and wear of his occupation; an under-done beef steak with a chunk of bread and a tankard of Colonial beer, vulgarly called swanky. The professor was a man well advanced in years, of an ordinary height, and inclined to run to stout- ness. He wore his iron-grey tresses long, so that they fell over his back in a lank and straggly fashion, his beard and moustache also were grey and mangy, while his rubicund complexion, common-place features, and small eyes reminded one somehow of a mendicant friar of the middle ages.</p>
        <p>_ His small eyes were crow-like and rather vacant looking in their expression, and what linen he dis- played was of the dirtiest and most rumpled des- • bis garments also, although at one date</p>
        <p>The Most Nutritious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EPPS'S</head>
