      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Among the many duties expected of Royalty, that of being photographed can hardly be the least trying. The German Emperor has net, however, that dislike of the camera which the Czar of Roesia has, and during his Majesty 're visit to Sandringham some excellent photo; of Royal group. have been taken by a well-known photo- grapher. Several members of our own Royal Family are expert photographers, and the camera is •Iso the pet hobby of the German Empress. She seldom goes anywhere without it, and her collection compriees every kind of scene, and includes pertraite of her husband, of her children, aud other relatives. Setaae ominous reporte are current respecting the health of several members of the Russian imperial family. The Emperor timeelf is not free from weak- ness of the lungs, which is hereditary in his family, and the Empress Is stat.i to be suffering from acute melan- cholia. Still more definite reports refer to the precarious state of health of the Grand Duke Michael, the heir pt.,- eumptive and only surviving brother of the Czar. Re too is said to be affected with tuberele disease, which has becementore marked since his last attack of infleecza at Cepenharren. The Grand Duke holds more progres- sive ideas than does his Royal brother. In the event of his death, the Grand Duke Vladimir, who belongs to the older Russian school ef thought, would become heir pre- Intmptive. Lord and Lady Lonsdale intend to be present at the Delhi Durbar seed arranged to leave for India after the German Emperor's visit to Lowther Castle. The Countess is the tall stately daughter ef the tenth Marquis of Huntly, and has been in India before. There is not the bunting in that country that is to be had at home, but there is plenty of sport of different kinds, in which Lord and Lady Lansdale will no doubt take a share. Those people whe are going out to friends will be all right, but those who are trusting to find their own accommodation at Delhi will discover that the Icdian town is rather an expensive one. All over the East the modern craze for " globe trotting" ban greatly put up the cost of hotel living, but at Delhi the pro- prietors are already ebarging eight and ten times the ordinary prices. I Possibly the weaker sex will at some future time come in for its fair share in the distribution or honours,though as yet it is seldom that snob marks of favour are bestowed upon a woman. That, however, only makes the honour more prized, and Mies M C Smith of the Savings Bank Department, is to be congratulated on being the first woman to be admitted to the Imperial Service Order by his Majesty. Miss Smith has been nearly thirty years in the service Of the State, and she was one of a small staff at the Savings Bank Berartment who may be said to have been the pioneers of the em- ployment of women in the Post Office. The experiment .proved such a success that the late Mr Fawcett began at once to open the doors to women clerks, and the number employed has circa then steadily increased without any set back to the movement. Miss Smith who was first one of twenty, has now about nine hundred women clerks %leder her coetrol at the Savings Bank Departmeut in London. A popular Christmas gift i 3 some article of silver, whether for the table, or some little novelty, and swing to the great fall in the price of F Ryer, such purchases ought to be considerably cheaper than in former yeare. Such advantage however, is not always apparent in the , priors marked up in the jewellers' and silversmiths.' I These shop keepers do their best to foster the old- fashioned notion that silver is a very Precious metal Indeed, and this ides is so ingrained in the publics mind that few people realise that for a comparatively few pounds, it is possible to buy almost as much of the solid metal as one could carry. Yet such articles as silver , jugs, cigar cases, pocket flasks, etre, are frequently I marked tip in the big silversmiths' at prices which are 1 pile out of proportion to their value as regards both workmanship and weight of metal. Shopkeepere Rater- ' ally get what they can, but if purchasers were better informed, they too might obtain more benefit from the fact that silver is not now worth half what it was years ago. In colours, ree medium tints are accepted, either the palest or brightest must be worn, and among the latter, red is in great faveur. Unless, however, one can afford many toilettes, it is a mistake to indulge in bright colours. Bleak, or the soft pastel tints, are much More serviceable Rue render the wearer more secure from the eery unpleasant process of being known by her clothes, Beth black and the pest&amp; shades permit too of so many colour schemes that they make distiectly for economy- The new barbaric embroideries, which eae now bead to match or tone with almest any material, are exceed- ingly fashionable, To the home dressmaker they will I ereve a blessing indeed, and the renovator will find , I them of the greatest service. In price they are moderate. I though for the very elaborate varieties, aid these made specialty to ordeir,cne can run to somewhat high fieures. This latter precedere is not a wise one. The Russian and Sertyrne embroideries will surely appeal greatly to the tt masers," and when prices permit they will be far too popular to please the really well-dressed woman. Women, generally speaking, give each other much more sensible Ctristrnas presents than were in vogue some years ago. Drapers are Arming just now, dress lengths et the most fascinating fabrics, this form of gift haring been they say, a very popular one last year. To the girl who has but a small dress allowance, aed is able to build her own gowns, such a present is sure to be acceptable, and the idea should recommend iteelf to these among us who have such a friend,to whom it is poesible en melee such a gift. A fan too, ranks as a 'ere popular Christmas present. The newest are of a feathered variety and wonderfully inexpensive. The ostrich feather fan is never really out of fashion, but three wearing the tinter of the pheasant's breast, or the peacock's, back, or again, the snow white livery of the I Swan, are in greater favor. The beet veiling, are of a somewhat open mesh spotted largely with eheeille, and lined with chiffon. When the keen winds urine, we Shall be glad of the protection they ffc•rd,but we should do, well to disperse with the spots, which all oculists are agreed are so had for one eves. Another novelty in the millinery world is flowers built of silk ribbons, which look very soft and quaint, and wear so very much better that the ordinary artificial flower. Everything possible in the way et softening the effect of headgear is being done at the moment by leading milliners, and the result is such that never perhaps have bats and toques been mere ! generally becoming than they are this winter MADGE.</p>
        <p>A family of St Lo, Fiance, named LE bautiern,conNint- log of husband, wife, and three children, were found dead in bed on Wednesday in their rottsgr. A note left by the husband stated that owing to their hopeless poverty they decided to suffocate themselves.</p>
        <p>The " Cambrian licws " says : The Cambrian Rail' araT s Company have made a nevi departure in arrang- ing to isPere a filst-class free pars fel- Eve years in favour of pPteOlid bo undertako to build hon9es ot £5O per annum rectal at the various Cambrian zoast att rlng plates.</p>
      </div>
