      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>No ruling monarch in the world could be more popular With.the British nation as a whole than is the King of Portugal, who is visiting King Edward at Windsor this week, and the• only drawback to his visit to England is that there is no opportunity for vouchsafing him a public welcome, as: it is entirely a' private one. Should he come to London at all, which is doubtful, tit:6 visit to the metropolis will belkept scrupulously private and will simply be. a shopping expedition. This is a disappoint- ment to the nation, : s -Britons are slow to forget their friends, and of Kinsc,Carlos it will ever be remembered that during thee recent ter, when no one had a good word to say for us, and we were at the height of our Unpopularity abroad, he enter- tained the British r meal officers at Lisbon, and, in proposing Queen Victoria's health, spoke most warmly, sa, ing, "We are anxious that the ties which bind this. Fmall btu glorious nation (Portugal) to our great and glorious ally shall be indissolubly cemented."- A little thing, perhaps, ,hut it will lever be forgotten in England. And this, it may obe add, is only one of the many occasions upon</p>
        <p>which he has made warm reference to thi;,. country in his public speeches. In addition to being a close personal friend of the King, he is also distantly related to our P.nyal -17 . bring grandnephew of Queen Victoria's mother. By the late Queen he was always seoken of as "my dear nephew," speaking more affectionately than strictly colorectly. King Carlos is thirty-nine years of acre—,ell, fair and decidedly good-looking, though inclined to be fat, weighing over sixteen atone. He is, neverthe- less extremely active and full of vigour, excelling in all sports, especially as a shot, being one of the finest in the world. Like our late visitor, the Kaiser, he is marvellously versatile, and would have made his mark as an artist if he had not been a king. At the recent Paris Exhibition he was awarded a medal for painting, whica is his favourite hobby.. He is also a sculptor of no mean merit, and has published one of the most interesting works on matters maritime ever issued. He is, above all, an excellent talker, and has perfect command of no fewer than seven languages. Since he ascended the throne, thirteen years ago, all has not been plain sailing in Portugal, and his cares have been disproportionate to the size of his kingdom. In 1892. even after having voluntarily reduced his income on account of Portugal's - financial straits, his life was attempted as he was leaving Lisbon. But things have changed since then, and he is now extremely popular. Queen Amelie, his consort, was brought up in England, at Twickenham, where her father, the Comte de Paris, spent his exile. She, too, is most favourably disposed towards Great Britain. She has done a lot of good in the country of her marriage, and is worshipped by the Portuguese. During the King's absence she is atting as regent.' In at emlance on King Carlos at Windsor is M. de Several, the Portuguese Minister, who is a great personal friend of both monarchs, and is constantly about with King Edward. Captain His Serene Highness Prince Louis Alexander of Battenberg, G.C.8., G.C.V.O.' A.D.C., R.N., has been appointed Director of Naval Intelligence in succession to Rear-admiral Reginald N. Constance, Prince Louis of Battenburg was born at Gratz, in Austria, rma May 24th. 1854, and is the eldest son of Prince Alexander of Hesse and grandson of Louis 11., Grand Duke of Hesse. He became a naturalised British subject and entered the Navy as cadet in 1868. Six years later, he was promoted to asub-lieutenancy and did con- spicuous service in the Egyptian War, being awarded the medal and Khe- dive's Sair and promoted to the position of corn- mande r. Ile married, in 1884, his cousin Prin. ,cess Victoria the daughter of Louis IV., Grand Duke of Hesse, K.G., and of the lat e Princess NEW DIRECTOR OF NAVAL Alice. In 1891 INTELLIGETCE. he was made cap- tain and was Naval Adviser to the Inspector- generalof Fortifications in 1893-4. In June 1899, he was appointed As,istant-director of Naval Intelli- gence. He owns Helligenberg Castle and the estate of ,Hesse in Germany, and his heir is Prince George, who is now ten years of age. The history of the British E npire seems to be made up of a constant series of little wars with occasional big ones, sometimes singly, and at others two or three together. The latest of these is the expedition against some sections of the Darwesh Khel Waziris, which started on Monday. The objective of this attack is Kabulkehl, the stronghold of the Waziris, with a view to destroy- ing its fortified towers and capturing the large number of outlaws whom it has been harbouring between their frequent raids on British territory. It is expected that a salutary lesson will be taught to the robbers who infest the frontier, as the force, though small, is thoroughly well equipped. It</p>
        <p>LOUIS</p>
        <p>BATTENBERG,</p>
        <p>_:.- --</p>
        <p>COL. EGERTON, A TROOPER OF PIINSAR COMMANDER OF CAVALRY OF WHICH THE THE WAZIRI EnEDITION IS LARGELY EXPEDITION. COMPOSED. crnsists of four flying surprise columns of eight hundred men each, and it is hoped that the whole affair will last about a week. The expedition is under the command of Colonel Egerton, with Colonel H. /c. Mcßae, starting from Idar, Colonel J. Pollock, from Thal, Colonel O. Radford, from Bargaraitha, and Colonel V. Tonnochy, from Gumathi, as. column leaders Colonel Egerton himself accompanying Colonel' Pollock flora Thal in supreme command. The troom have been drawn from the First, Third and Fifth Punjab Cavalry, the First, Third, and Fourth Sikhs, and, the Second, Fourth, Fifth, and Twenty-nun:L(4 Punjabis, with parties of' sappers and Kurram and Waziristan Militia, with ten mountain guns. All the officers selected for commands in this frontier expedition below, to' the Indian Stall Corps. Colonel C. L. Egerton, C. 8., D. 5.0., Aide- de-Camp to the King, has lately be in command at Abbottabad, with the local rank oE major. general, previous to which he had been serving on the Pun jab Frontier Staff. He took part in ths Afghan War and the march to Kandahar ; also in I the two Hamra expeditions; was severely mounded in one of the Mirauzi eneditiosa</p>
        <p>1:, _......4 . ~.._.), .....„ , ~.,,,.. ... • f i j"----- • „7:4,. ,--, ••••1 I ""; ',..,. •io 1 . .2.• ...--..--N.F------) r ' , ...it CP ..ste clasiOtt . _</p>
        <p>THZ RING AND QUEEN OF PORTUGAL.</p>
        <p>T Evj 47n,11 I A, H.S.H. PRINCE OP •</p>
        <p>1 , , 1 • 0 . --- . , , - I I i I - i • •--...), 7:l;ir 41, fl I .1.1 1 ~--T 10 "="r."...-,4,41:fir------.7- .... :* ~_•±...,..... 1 • \''' ; ‘ll*4( 't,,, 11 '. '•, . ' 1 ___-,_---- % li*,,,,`k • ; ~!-:y , , , 70 , 0 ' '"'• ,' •;‘ ~, i t r , .., .., ~.., r . ty A • . -'- ___ _ a --=--</p>
        <p>• /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <p>commanded a column in the Waziristan expedition in 1894; the Indian contingent at Suakim in 1896 ; and a brigade in Tochi in 1897. Colonel H. N. Mcßae, C. 8., Aide-de-Camp to the King, one of the column commanders, was also in the Afghan war, and later has sewed in the Zhob Valley Expedition, the 1888 Razara Expedition, in the defence of Malakand, and other frontier cam- paigns, including the Mahsud Waziri Expedition of last winter, in which Major-General Egerton also served.- Colonel J. A. H. Pollock, another column commander, had 'his first war service in the JoWaki Expedition of 1877, and has since taken part in the Afghan war, in several frontier cam- paigns, and in the China Expedition of 1900. Colonel 0. C. •Radford, C.1.E., the third column commander, also began campaigning in the Jowaki force, followed by the Afghan and several frontier campaigns, and was wounded in the Hazara Expedition of 1888, and. like Colonel Pollock, he was in the last China campaign. Colonel V. C. Tonnochy, C. 8., after service on ' the frontier and in Burma, was severely wounded in the storming of the Malakand Pass; afterwards • served in the Tochi and Tirah campaigns, and, lastly, in the Mahsud Waziri affairs. For a long time, Newport has had under con- sideration many schemes for dealing with the congested state of traffic on the bridge over the Usk. The proposals have included a tunnel under the river, a high level bridge, and a swing bridge; but each of them was found impracticable and it is difficult to say how the problem would have been solved had not M. F. Arnodin, a clever French engineer, come forward with a remarkable idea. Arnodin has invented and patented a bridge which he calls the Transporter, and which Newport has decided to adopt. The novel feature of the Transporter is that instead of the pedestrians</p>
        <p>TEE TRANSPORTER BRIDGE W TuE USK AT NEWPORT.</p>
        <p>walking across hnd vehicles driving over the I,ridge, the traffic is taken into a huge • oar sus- pended horn the top and -swung to he Other side by electricity. The accompanying illustration shows how this l'emarkable structure will</p>
        <p>appear when finished. The car will accom- modate six vehicles and a couple of hundred pexies.triam. The span will be 645 -feet, and there will be a clear opening of 592 feet, so that shipping will -not be interfered with. The towers are being built into solid blocks of masonry, each weighing 2,000 tons. The bridge, which is estimated to cost £90,000, will be set up at a point where the ri'ver is 700 ft. wide. It is estimated that the bridge will be completed in a year:</p>
        <p>Interest seems rather to increase than to diminish in the- Southwark mystery, which is furnishing one of .the biggest criminal sensations of modern times. It will be remembered that George Chapman, the landlord of the Crown public-house in the Borough, was arrested on the day of the recent Royal Progress on the charge of having murdered Maude Marsh, one of his bar- maids, and his reputed wife, by the administratioA of poison, arsenic being the drug suggested by the Treasury. Since then, Dr. Waldo, the South- wark coroner, has held two or three sittings on the body, and Chapman has been up before Mr. Cecil Chapman, the magistrate at Southwark, an equal number of times, but in neither case has considerable progress been made pending the result of the reports of the analysts, who performed the post-mortem on Miss Marsh. This is expected</p>
        <p>HO IS CHARGED WITM THE MURDER OF MAIIDE MARSH.</p>
        <p>this week, and would help both the coroner's jury and the magistrate to arrive at a decision one way or the other with regard to the prisoner. Formal as have been the sittings, nevertheless, a certain amount of interesting and sensational evidence has been unearthed",about Chapman's past and his relations with Miss Marsh, whose parents were fully under the belief that she was legally Mrs. Chapman and had been married to the prisoner 'at the Roman Catholic church in Bishopsgate. Their evidence was particularly pathetic, especially as to their last visits to their daughter, when she was on her death bed, and they will be amongst the principal witnesses for the prosecution if the case is sent to trial. But until the analyst's reports have been furnished, there is, of course, little or no case beyond suspicion against the prisoner, who maintains a calm demeanour and appears dressed wit a scrupulous neatness. Inspectors Godley and Kemp have been in charge of the case and their evidence also will be important. The court at each sitting has been crowded as full as the authorities allowed, and large numbers of people have collected in the road outside, showing the local interest evinced in the matter.</p>
        <p>What is perhaps an unprecedented feature in any London entertainment is the latest addition to the Hippodrome programme. It is a repro- duction of the awful eruption 'of Mont Pelee and the doom of St Pierre. The production on a stage of such a gigantic phenomenon of nature must 1:4 attended with considerable difficulties, and one apt to imagine a truthful representation of ucli a disaster at the best a fair display of squibs, red fir, and collapsible • scenery. But the manage- ment of the Hippodrome have merited a more kindly anticipation. The mountain spurts huge volumes of steam, a great fissure appears at the summit, from which is emitted vast quan- tities of very real looking fire and lava, and by aid</p>
        <p>PTION OF MONT PELEE AT THE LONDON HIPPODROME. Of electricity is splendid represmtation of a storm is effected, which playing overhead, greatly im- proves the spectacle. The %houses at the base of the mountain take fire and collapse, the idea of total destruction being very well carried out. The Hippodrome has been fortunate in securing a number' of men, women and children who were inhabitants of St. Pierre before the eruption, and who actually witnessed the catastrophe and only with :difficulty escaped themselves. Their experiences have not, apparently, affected the gaiety of these survivors, who seem to take genuine pleasure in the performance of a native wedding which precedes the eruption.</p>
        <p>"You catch cold," said the doctor, as he settled him- self in his chair," by infection from the breath of some- ' one else who has one, generally. " In a railway carriage with a mac who is sneezing and snuffling, insiet on having the window once a little, or you will stand a good chance of catching that cola yourself. More colds are caught through stuffiaess than ! drauehts. " Don't neglect a cold. It may go on to influenza ; or it may set up a cough that will tear a little wound in year lung. Then you will split blood, and the wound will enlarge until you are in the first stage of Consump- tion. Yoar throat will be husky, your chest sore ; you will get thin and begin to perspire more than you ought. All through a cold! " Why does one man catch cold more easily than another ? Because some people (especially women) haven't half as much blood in their veins as they should have. What they have is poor and thin. That lays you open to dim.° of all sorts ; it is a disease in itself Anemia, the Royal College of Physicians cells it. 'Try ,Dr Williams' pink pills for pele people. Best thing I I know for it. We doctors don't like advertised medi- cines, as a rule, but these pill 3 are not a peteat medieine ; they are a discovery hy one of our own pro- fession, a graduate of my own University—Edinburgh. They mean more blood in the veins, more vit silty, more nervous and spinal power. A safeguard as well as a sure. Dan't purge yourself ; these pills are a tonic, and will do you all the good purgatives are supposed to, without the discomfort." The old physioian's candour does him honour. A sold, the effect of which extended over ten years, is described, almost in the doctor's own words, by , the victim of it, Mr Wright, Easthorpe-street, Ruddington, Notts, who narrowly escaped a Consumptive's grave. " Ten y 'are ago," said he, " I took cold, as people are apt to do, and in coughing broke a blood-vessel. It was the first indication that I was consumptive. For twelve weeks I lay, fed like a little child. "At the time I lay in bed there were four other people in the village similatly affected. lam the only one of the five alive now ! Bow did I escape ? We had read alocut Dr Williams' pink pills, and I tried them. From the first box they did me good, and I have not been under a doctor since. From being a total wreck, I have regained a great deal of my former strength. I first noticed that the wasting away stopped, and that my cough became less troublesome. I began to put on flesh, an indication that I was on the road to better health. My progress bee been slow, which is only natural, as I was at death's door; but it has also been sure. The wander of those who know me is that lam alive. "I am a better man now," continued Mr Wright, " than I have been for tee years, and these pink pills are the only thing that have helped me." Not eoly chest complaints, but rheumatism, paralysis, St Vitus' Dance, kidney diseases, and the ailments so painful to women have been enred by the same remedy. Dr Williams' name on the wrapper is s safeguard against substitutes. The pills are a tonic, not a purgative— bear in mind the old physician's warning against purgatives.</p>
        <p>-, ..1. C 1 ' ../- 1...,., , r ' --* • W ~ r/o , / ;,Iyir9fp• z., GEORGE CHAPMAN, W</p>
      </div>
