      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>LANJISHIRE v. YORKSHIRE.</p>
        <p>There was It large attendance on Saturday on the Salford ground at Weaste to witness the Lancashire and Yorkshire match under Northern Union rules. The result —a win to Lancashire—practically assures them of the county champion- ship for this season. The number actually paying was 13,236, and there were probably 14,500 present. The gate receipts totalled £432. The match was far from a brilliant one, and the accidents were so numerous as probably to create a record for a county match. Lancashire won easily, but their display was not of a very high class, and that of Yorkshire was lamentably weak. The game opened in a dreary manner, and Lancashire had a monopoly of the play for some time. They made several attacks on the Yorkshire lines, and ought to , have scored in the first few minutes. Valentine and Wilson however. opened 'out very badly to their wings, and the reault was that Hogg and Fish got few opportunities. Taylor, the visiting fall back, was given plenty to do, and he seldom m de a mistake. His tall kicking relieved the pressure for his side, and Ciracts and Marsden, by quick dashes from the scrummage made much ground. W Davies was also consoicuous tor his side, causing Smith, the Lancashire full back, to keep a careful look out, and it was some time befere the house team again became aggressive. Ulti- mately Sam James started a passing movement amongst the backs, and this ended in Valentine sending to Fish, who raced over near the corner. W James was unsuccessful with the place kick. Upon resuming, Marsden put in a brilliant I run for Yorkshire. He was pulled up by Valentine before he became dangerous, arid from this point to the interval nothing very eventful happened. Lancashire crossed over leading by three points to nil. The second stage did not open very brightly. Lancashire strightway began to attaik, and it was soon seen that the Yorkshire chance of victory was a very slight one. The players went about their work in a very half-hearted manner, and Lancashire had little diffi- culty in penning them. Hushes by the horns backs took them in front of the Yorkshire lines, and Valentine picked up and dropped a really clever goal. This was followed by pretty combination amongst the Lancashire backs, and Wilson and Hogg were mainly responsible for another severe attack. The first-named player was often dangerous, but he was well marked by T V Davies. For some minutes the Yorkshire position was dangerous, and after threatening movements amongst the Lancashire bacias Willie jaines gave Fish is good opening. The Warrington man ran finely to within a few yards a the Yorksoireline, and then passed opportunely to Valentine, who scored in an easy position. Willie James easily placed a goal. The game W/to now at its best. The Lancashire men never weakened in their attacks, and Taylor was kept continually on the move. Grace and Marsden very often fed their three-quaners judiciously, but that line was particularly weak and lacked the power of combination. Before the finish Valentine was carried off hurt, but his Absence made no appreciable differ- ence. One more concerted movement came from the Lanca- shire backs, and Hogg, following up well, intercepted a pass meant for Hagen and obtained another try for Lancashire, Ike point was not improved, and Lancashire were winners by two goals and three tries (13 points) to nothing. The teams were t— _</p>
        <p>Lancashtre: D -Smith, Seaford, full back A Hogg and R Wilson (Broughton Rangers), R Valentine (Swinton), and J Fish (Warrington), three-quarter backs; W James and James (Broughton Rangers),half backs; r Tunney, J Rhapps, and G Heath (Salford), J Ferguson and (ii Frater (Oldham), G Boardman (Leigh), J Hyena (Swinton), and Vir Oran:: (Broughton Rangers), forwards. Yorkshire : H Taylor (Hull), full back ; W ' Davies (Batley), P D Davies (Leeds), W J Eaves (Bradford), and W Bennett (Wakefield Trinity), three quarter backs ; H Marsden (Bradford) and (I Grace (Leeds), half backs ; T Broalley CBradford) (captain), A. Starks (Hull Kingston Hovers). I Bartle and .1 Riley (Halifax), A. Laidlow (Bradford), 0 Crampton (Leeds), and r Driver and F Littiewo (Huddersfield), torwards. Referee, Mr J Kidd (Hilloin).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. First Division. Vr ARRINGTON v. BROUOHTON 'ANGERS.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. First Division. Vr ARRINGTON v. BROUOHTON 'ANGERS.</p>
        <p>Warrington journeyed to Wheater's Field on Saturday to do battle againsi, last season's champions,Broughton dangers. 'they were without two such capable exponents as Fish and Dickenaou, while the Rangers had to find substitutes for Wilson, Hogg, b James, W James, Orem and Harry, the first five all doing duty for Lancashire. along with Fish. Both teams were, therefore, disorganised and took the field betore only 2,703 spectators in the following order : Broughton liangers.l4ielding, bask; Eckersall, Barrett, Widdeson and Hampson, three-quarter backs ; Driver and Findlow, half backs ; Whitehead, Whaskill, Boetham, Scott, WarWiek, orarretty. Hardisty and Locke, forwards. Warrington—Kni,,,ht, back ; Edmunds, Isherwood Duck- worth,and Harris, three-quarter basks; Burgess and Hocken- ball, half baoks ; Boardman, Morrison, Roberts, Forster, W Swift, Edmondson, J Swift, and Taylor, forwards. Referee, Mr RII Smirk, Wigan. Touch judges, Messrs P Taylor, Oldham, and W Lancaster, Rochdale. Warrington were the first to attack, and Hockenhall was brought down close to the Rangers' line. Findiow relieved and hickersall further improved matters by intercepting a pass and getting clear away, but in attempting to dodge Knight he fell The /Rangers then attacked strongly, and liampson almost dropped a goal. At half time there was no score. The second half was mostly in favour of the Rangers, but the tackling of the Warrington three-quarters prevented their line from being crossed. Yielding ruade several attempts to place a penalty goal, and Hampson also had a kick or but but neither met with suasesa. Holleman should have scored but missed a pass a yard from the line, Towards the finish of the game Warrington had two capital openings to score as the result of anus break away by Hoesenhall, but both Harris and Duck worth failed to take advantage of them. and I the result was a pelatless draw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>WIDNHS T WIGAN.</head>
        <p>WIDNHS T WIGAN.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, before 4000 spectators. Bsth tennis turned out as selected. In the first halt neither side scored, although the home team had the best of the game. In the second half Hothwell dropped a goal far Wigan. Final Wigan, 1 goal (1 points); Widnes, nil.</p>
        <p>Lkl4ll v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Leigh, before b,OOO spectators. Mach side had two absentees. la the litst half itun3ora, with the wiacLattaeked, and erica loot a try by failing to pass. Runcorn missed two easy chances at goal. In the second half Ilelyneen,the Leigh halt, reUred hurt. Consequently Leigh played 14 men in the last quarter, and pressed continuously, but could not score. Runcorn defending tenaciously. Filial .—Leigh nil,Runcern nil.</p>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD V HULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>At ?sawn, in line but dull weather, before ONO aunts. tors. The teams were well represauto4, add a fast ana ex- citing encounter Was witnessed. In the first half, tries were scored for the respective sides by aura= and Deere, the teams crossing over with the score aged. Tie second half was very keenly oontested.Bntoliffe scoring for Hudders- field. Final :—Huddersfield, I. goal, two tries (8 points) ; Revers 1 try (3 points),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>HULL V ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>HULL V ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At Hull before about 7,0 i 3. spectators. The game was somewhat desultory, the teams being fairly equal. When in a good position for the visitors, Hull were penalised. and Mercer mired an easy goal. No other score resulted in the first half. On the restart, Hull had slightly the advantage of the wind, but this did not benefit them. A. scrimmage near the home posts resulted in a dropped goal by Hall, and Parry scored a try, but the kick failed. Final s—St lielans. 4 points ; Hull, 3 points,</p>
        <p>THE TALK 01- THE VILLAGE. ---- What Bile Beans did for a Miner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HALIFAX v BATLEY.</head>
        <p>HALIFAX v BATLEY.</p>
        <p>44, I hear they want more</p>
        <p>V</p>
        <p>At Halifax. in dull weather, and before 7,600 spectators. Bigg and Hadwen were substituted by Harley and Riley for Hallfax,whilst Davis was playing for the county. Joe Riley scored for Halifax, and Little converted, He alas kicked two penalty goals. naidgley scared a try for Batley. Just before half-time Morley had his left arm broken through being kicked, a very serious less for Halifax. Final : Halifax, 3 goals 2 tries (12 points) ; Batley 2 tries (6 points).</p>
        <p>OLDHAM V BRTGRoUSE RANGERS. At Oldham, before 6,000 spectators. The home teai . did most of the attacking in the first half, and a bout of passing by the Oldham three-quarter line resulted in Gardner cross- ing the Brighouse line, but Wormald tailed at goal. From penalty kick at mid-field, Dick Thomas landed a goal,making Oldham 5 points. In the second half the Rangers did most of the attacking, but no score was registered. Final. Oldham, 5 points ; Brighouse ka)gers,</p>
