      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>A VICTORY FOR WARRINGTON "A."</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers "A "were tbe visitors to the Wilders- pool enclosure on Saturday, and they returned defeated to the extent of six points to all. Warrington won the toes,and soon after the kick-off the visitors became aggrestve, but the house backs were on the alert. Play was soon taken to the visitors' quarters and Allen opened the scoring for Warring- ton. The kick at goal was abortive. Watkins, Brooks and Jones combined well, but the latter passed wildly when a try seemed certain. Brooks kicked well and following up tackled his man, thus gaining considerable ground. Bailey made a capital wing, but was grassed near the line. Half- time : Warrington, one try ; Broughton Rangers, nil. On resuming the visitors played up briskly and Wai•rington were forced to defend. Ultimately Jones transferred play to half way. The Rangers' captain now secured, and after running half the length of the field was pulled up by Broeks two yards from the line. One of the Broughton men was injured, and had to retire for the rest of the game The borne team forced the play considerably, but tasked finish. Fine passing by the visitors nained much ground, but Fillingbarn retaliated with a strong kick. Just on tin, e Brooke gathered the ball at centre, and evading all his opponents, scored a fine try, no goal resulting. Final : Warrington two tries (six points); Broughton Rangers, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>P.S.A. MOVEMENT. Annual Tea Meeting in Wycliffe Memorial Buildings.</head>
        <p>THE P.S.A. MOVEMENT. Annual Tea Meeting in Wycliffe Memorial Buildings.</p>
        <p>Tha annua tea meeting in connection with the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society was held on Monday evening and wag very sueoessful. About 300 persons partook of an excellent tea, and subsequently a crowded= meeting was held in the Assembly Room of the Memorial Build- ings. The Rev John Yonge presided in the absence of Mr F Monks, and he was accompanied on the platform by the Rev E C Tinker,of Wigan (president of the Lanca- shire and Northern Counties P.S.A. Federation), Mr Barnes (secretary of the Wigan Society), Mr Farrar and Mr Downes (secretaries of the Wycliffe Society), Mr R Atkinson, Mr Joseph Owen,Mr R Mills and Mr W Bee- wick.</p>
        <p>The Chartism; explained that the absence of Mr Monks was due to a clashing of engagements, and they had let Mr Monks off on the present occasion on the distinct understanding that he came on some future occasion. Prooeeding to address the meeting, the Chairman said he was glad to know that the Wycliffe P 6 A. society was in so many senses flourishing, Their brotherhood was growing week by week, and he trusted thg members would do everything they could to make it a great success. When he looked about on Sundays and saw the boats of men who never entered a place of worship he felt deeply that there was a large field for ' Christian usefulness and they ought to make it a matter of earnest resolution to get held of these men and bring them in. Since the Wycliffe P.S.A. was started a great number of Sun- day afternoon meetings for men had been opened and were being conducted with success. The P 8 A move- ment was doing a great deal of good throughout the country and la every case where a P.S.A. had been con- ducted on gospel lines it had been a great success and the means of much good. He was pleased to notice that in -connection with a Blackburn Congregational Church where there was a P.S.A. with a membership of eomething like 1,200, the speaker at a recons inertiug was the Rector of Blackburn—Bishop rhortit in, a colonial bishop, who honoured himself and helped the Congregational P.S.A. by going to the Cangregational Chinch to address a meeting of men. (Hear,hear.) He (the chairman) would be glad to see a elergyn.an of the Church of England at one of the meetings of the Wycliffe Society and be was certain the men would give him a hearty reception. (Applause). A violin solo entitled "Sarabande " (Bohn) was given with much skill by Mr T Wallington, whom the Chair- man congratulated upon obtaining his majority that day. Miss Beswick favoured the audience with a recitation " Briar Rose." A section of the Warrington Male Voice Choir then sang " Comrades song of lisps," and in complying with a recall, the audience was still further pleased by the sympathetic and delicate treat- ment of " In absence." Mr W 11 Payton! Daus. Bac. con- ducted.</p>
        <p>" The Rev E C TINKER then delivered an address, and said he was glad to convey the greetings of the Lanca- shire and Northern Clountlisa P.S.A. Federation and lso of the Wigan P.S.d. Sxfiety. He pleaded with the women present to encourage their husbands In the P.S.A. movement. The advancement of the work rested up3n every mecabn of the 13r3ther- hood, a•d in an intereethag way be showed the import- ance and far reaching effect of an endeavour on the part el each member to bring in one member each year. Be vroutd ask them to us their influencein the work- shop in the factory and in the office t bring those wbo were indifferent and careless to the feet of Christ. It was for that work that the P.S.A. movement existed.</p>
        <p>thom tty to rescue some brother who had tallen into the oozy depths of despair and degradation. If ever there was needed a great crusade against the forces of evil that crusade was needed to-day, and ia the r.s. A movement they had the materials ready to , fight for this great crusade. The P.S.A. brotherhood I was a great army on the side of purity, righteousness, sobriety, and Godliness, and he would ask all present to throw in their lot with the forces of goOd against the forces of evil. (Applause). Mr Millington efftctively sang "The Curfew" and the I their gave another selection, for which they ware heartily applauded. • The services of all who had taken part in the meeting were fitting acknowledged on the proposition of Mr B FARRAR, seconded by Mr ATKINSON.—Mr BARNES, of Wigan, acknowledged the compliment, and an enjoyable gathering terminated.</p>
        <p>The opium:Lana of public opinion is that</p>
      </div>
