      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>" WARRINSTON LIMITED."</head>
        <p>" WARRINSTON LIMITED."</p>
        <p>TO TER EDITOR OF TAR " EXA.EINER,"</p>
        <p>Sir,—A writer in that admirable local periodical "The Dawn "—a paper delightfully original but unfortunately not always to be taken seriously— has been sanguine enough to predict that when the persons holding bonds "in Warringt,on Limited have been paid off (in anything from turee to sixty years), our children and our children's children will possess the whole of the great property quite un- encumbered•and may possibly not need to levy any rates at all, but even actually receive a bonus." Now, I do not wish to be unduly pessimistic, nor do I desire to be placed in tha same ca egory as those persons (if there are any) who, we are told, have recently vilified the town's fair name so much, but Ido most earnestly desire to know whether, and how, such an ideal state of affairs is possible. Calumny has bean so prevalent of late tout one hardly dares to suggest that he is not satisfied with the questionable management of the town's finances without a dread of for ever being branded as having a personal grudge against some one er other of our " Directors." Hard things have been said on both sides of this lamentable agitation, and I am one who believes that it would have been more seemly if there had ' been no necessity for such an agitation. A fall and fair incotiry would have satisfied everybody, but by some unfortunate ruling such an linquiry would not be tolerated. However, the eaders of the " opposition" will now have access to the books they have so longed for and we shall follow keenly the result of their researches. Cer- tain questions were to be asked of the retiring Councillors at the last election and as they were not, apparently, answered correctly, we may look to the members vvho were so unanimously elected by the " vox mull " for a satisfactory statement as to what was actually spent under the headings mentioned.</p>
        <p>To return to our friend "The Dawn," I venture to think that the person who is responsible for that article has allowed his desires to distort his reason. What we, as ratepayers, re- quire is not an exaggsrated article either for or against the town's finances, but a plain statement showing whether we are or are not in a satisfactory position. Portiaps, the writer referred to, will tell us whether he intended his readers to take his re- marks seriously or whether he, after all, was "only dreaming." We cannot write of sixty years hence with any degree of accuracy —like some folks,—but we can refer a little to see what has already been done for us. I would advise all those who are interested in figures to take the first opportunity of perusing the 'Year Book "published by our Council. I can assure them a very pleasant and instructive evening. Of all the accounts that I have ever come across commend me—for simplicity—to that book. You can get just what you are in search of with so little trouble and mental exertion. All you need to do, as you turn page over page, is to knock the ash off your cigar and go on quietly smoking. The information you require will come intuitively— at least, I should imagine that has been the impression of the compilers. In 1857 the total rates levied in the borough amounted to 4s lid in the X. In this year of en- lightenment, 1902, the total rates levied amount to 6s 63 in the Z —an increase of Is 7d in the X. So that up to now the "children " have not derived very tuuch benefit from their "parents" manage- ment—and the bonus is as far off as ever.</p>
        <p>If we are to have improvements we must psy for them, and that is where our friend the "Dawn" goes sadly astray. He has quite overlooked the fact that, having no capital, we have to borrow money to pay our way. That money, I take it, has to be paid back doom* day. go that, whilst we are paying off some bondholders we are issuing new bonds to others.</p>
        <p>Let up. for a moment, refer to the Gas Depart- ment, The loans taken up for the redemption of annuities, according to the year book, amounted to £62,632. If that amount was the only sum we had to account for we should be alright. Bat, unfortu- nately, extension was necessary and so we had to borrow more money, viz :</p>
        <p>.£ a. d. Extension No. 1 ..... 52,350 0 0 „ 2 ........... 17,250 0 0 „ 3 2,064 0 0</p>
        <p>The repayment of those loans is also pread over a term of years. We thus find that before we have liquidated an old loan a new one is necessary. In the nature of things therefore " a bonne "is impossible. The writer also states that " we have a hest of assets which have much appreciated in wiles—the Town Hall and Park, for instance, which stand at £31,000, and are worth perhaps twine that earn." " A host of assets which have much appreciated in value !" Let us look for a few of them. Turn to the Borough Rate Fund Balance Statement, 31st March, 1902. .</p>
        <p>First of all we come to the Cemetery, .£26,186 I9s 9d. I think, we may safely say that the Cemetery, at least,has not appreciated in vain* and is evidently not one of the host of assets referred to.</p>
        <p>The Baths £6725 7s. Have they increased in 'value? Wait until the extensions are completed. The Bridewell and Sessions House .£4lOO. That building, at any rate, has net increased in value, portion of that amount should be debited te the New Police Buildings which already stands at— £24041 21. The demolition of VIM old building points out to us very forcibly the urgent necessity of providing for depreciation. Someday, I am afraid other premises now considered valuable assets will have to be sacrificed to make room for larger and more up-to-date buildings. May be—who knows? —the Town Hall itself will become too small for the ambitions of " our children's children." The gymnasium, School of Art,gusenrn and Library, Technical Institute, the Water Works, the Gas Works—these cannot be said to have im- proved in value, and se we might go on. The Town Ball and Park £31,062 12s 10d. How is that item made up? Town Hall and 5 acres of land... .£9OOO 0 0 Additions and alterations.. 2406 13 2 Furniture and portraits ....—....... 1200 0 0 Furniture Bank 10u5e.......... 400 0 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>£13006 13 2</head>
        <p>£13006 13 2</p>
        <p>recreation Ground ....—.— ....... —1712500 0 0 Laying out the Park and Recrea- tion Ground, &amp;c., &amp;c Fountain ...... ........ 1229 15 11</p>
        <p>£18055 19 8</p>
        <p>I take it that so long as Warrington is,so will be the Town Ball, and Park. The Town Hall as a building will never increase in value but, we may safely say that it will depreciate. Now, what of the Park ? It is net a proßtable asset by any means, taken as a Park. If,some day,it could be sold for building purposes, say for shops and dwellings,semetbing might be made ont of it, but 1 that is altogether out of the question. How then has its value increased so w onderfully ? I hope the writer will give us some further enlightenment on a subject whieh has up to now been kept very much in the dark. There are other questions raised in the article, but I have already trespassed upon your space.—Yours, SINE Pft..E.JUDICIO.</p>
        <p>Warrington, 21st Nov. 1902.</p>
      </div>
