      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NOVEMBIIR 21st, 1902.</head>
        <p>NOVEMBIIR 21st, 1902.</p>
        <p>IN the character of the Birmingham and Mr. Chamberlain. honours paid to Mr. CHAMBERLAIN on Monday evening, the people of Birmingham approached perilously near to the step which leads from the sublime to the ridiculous. Thousands of torch- bearers, guns of the Royal Field Artillery, a detachment of "handy men," an escort of Yeomanry, and a long tail of University students, 1 with torches, were among the features of a gorge- ous procession following upon a sumptuous banquet. , , Men much less liable to" tete m ont6e "than the _ . COLONIAL SECRETARY would be in great danger of a bad attack of that malady under such exhilar- ating and exciting conditions. A CAESAR coming home from one of his conquests can hardly have been so applauded and feted by the populace of Rome. Even the official "Triumph" decreed by the Roman-Sanate must often have fallen short of the spectacle presented in Birmingham. " The 'Right Hon. JOSEPH CHAMBERLAIN and Mrs. CHAMBERLAIN, preceded by a detachment of the United Fleet Reserve and followed by au escort of 1 Warwickshire Imperial Yeornany "—remembering ' many other episodes in Mr CHAMBERLAIN'S career, lit is difficult to repress a smile. But public demonstrations of this kind are no laughing , matter. The extreme personal attachment which Mr. CHAMBERLAIN has been able for so many years to command iii Birmingham, and the large area which may be called " the Birmingham sphere of influence," is in itself a phenomenon that demands to be regarded seriously. Whatever change he may have made in his attitude to parties he has always carried the balk of the people of Birmingham with him as well as many of their leaders. He has always been the centre oE wild public dernonstra- ' Cons, not less in the days of LARRY MACH than in those in which we are now living. In the chief midland city he has exerted a conspicuously greater influence oven than JoHN BRIGHT; and this is not because he is a Midlander, " one ' of ourselves," as the Birmingham people might say, for he -WAS born in London, and entered Birmingham, a prospective conqueror only, at the age of eighteen. Part of his secret it may be is • that he speaks of "my city" and "my people,' , just as he said" my policy," when he meant the policy of the Government in South Africa. With -. I , what facility a man so situated and so idolised 1</p>
        <p>might have developed into a dangerous demagogue may be guessed from a not yet forgotten threat of a march to London at the head of a band of Bir:n- ingham artisans. But the fierce energy exhibited in the response to Lord SALISBURY'S rather ill- considered challenge of less than twenty years ago has found other and larger outlets. Whatsoever may be the views entertained concerning Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S influence on British policy, its good or its evil effect has unquestionably been tremendous. For the present there can be no final judgment as to how far he has deservod so well of his country as many of his countrymen be- lieve. We are too near to recent events to be able to view them in the true perspective. It may be doubted, indeed, whether such a right judgment on the facts as will meet with anything approach- ing to a common assent will be possible in our time. For the present it cannot be denied that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN is a heroic figure to large num- bers of the people of Great Brita;n, and that in other places than Birmingham there are those who will not consider Monday night's extraordin- ary exixibition to be either grandiose or overdone. Perhaps we ought to recognise in the fact a de monstration of the popular respect for efficiency. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN is far from being "the one strong man in a blatant land." Bu</p>
        <p>he is undoubtedly regarded in Birming ham and elsewhere as tile one strong man in the Ministry. From that point of vie n the excessive adulation which has fallen to his share, and which he obviously likes, is the clearest possible contemnation of his colleagues. To the average Englishman he seems to be a man of busi- ness, one who " sees things through" ; and though he is no doubt quite as far responsible as any other member of the Ministry for the disas- trous mistakes made in the early portion of the war, he picked himself up sooner than the others, so to speak, and almost all that was afterwards done well outside the purely military sphere was done on his initiative. At this moment the greatest chance of :his life presents itself. He has the opportunity of justifying everything that has beenK said in his praise as "the practical Lman "On British politics. Should he really act as the reconciler in South Africa, those pertions of his past which are perhaps too much insisted upon in the present will be forgotten or forgiven, and he will take his place] permanently among great Englishmen. But the character of Monday night's demonstration does not encourage hope. There was too much of the Eastern satrap in it. From DO point of view was that a good pre.- liminary to a mission which is likely to be all the more successful the more simply and unpreten- tiously it is conducted. However, it is now possible that we have had the last of the farewells; and that instead of being spoiled by the demon- strations at Birmingham Mr. CHAMBERLAIN may have sobered himself by comparisons between the fugitive character of such events and the enduring public approval which may be won by good work another continent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRAMWAYS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRAMWAYS</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Extension. Proposed ,Double Line Opposite the Church.</p>
        <p>The proposed tramway extension to Stockton Heath was under discussion at a special meeting of the Parish Council whima was held in the National Schools on Tuesday evening. Mr Clarke presided and there were also present :—Mesers Pemberton, Bouchier, Whitley, PearsomMelntyre,and Bradshaw (clerk). The Chairman said the C3uncil would remember that at a recent meeting they passed a resolution recommending toe Corporation to lay a single line corresponding with the length of the churchyard wall, because of the narrowness of that part of the road. The matter was very fully considered at the time and they came to the conclusion that it would be for the benefit of the inhabitants to have a single line. The resolution was sent to the Corporation, and the Town Clerk forwarded it on tei the engineers, Messrs Preece and Cardew, who replied stating that they agreed to a single lineoppokite the Church, -but pointing out that in their opinion a single line across the narrow roadway 22 feet Sin wide at the swing bridge, would cause much obstruction to traffic and would probably be olaected to by the Board of Trade. When that letter was received the Council took it for granted that their wishes would be carried out. On Wednesday last it came to his knowledge that the Tramways Committee had decided upon laying two pairs of rails opposite the Cnurch. He immediately wrote to the Town Clerk, drawing his attention to the resolution of the Par,sh Council. The Town Clerk asked him to go and have an interview wikth him in order that he might explain matters. He did so, and Mr Whittle explained the reasons why the Tramways Commit- tee had adopted the double line. Therefore the pre- sent meeting had been called. Mr Whittle read him a letter trom the engineers in which they said it would be very much better for the working of the traffic to have two lines. One point the Town Clerk urged was that there would be less wear and tear with a double line, and that it would be safer for the public. If there was a single line there would be two points, and this would cause jolting. It was not a question of expense, the Town Clerk explained, because a single line would cost several hundred pounds leas than a double one. The Clerk then read the following letter from the Town Clerk :—" I send you herewith two plans of the Light Railway No I. One shows a double line of rails throughout and the other a short length of single line opposite St. Thomas's Church. While the second plan would be the cheapest for the Cor- poration to carry ont, they are advised on the ground of safety and of affording the greatest facility for traffic to adopt the double line through- out. It will avoid the double set of points, which can only be passed over at a slow rate of speed, and wtiich are always objectionable on account of the jolting they cause. The swing bridge will at times cause delay and inconvenience,and it is desir- able on that account to have double lines of rails.al there will have to be fixed stopping places on each side of the bridge, It is an axiom of the Beard of Trade that the narrower the road the greater the necessity for double lines. You will see from the plan that there will be a clear Oft, Gin, on each side between the outer rail and the kerb. This is the space fixed by the Bard of Trade rego- lations. The road opposite St Thomas's Church, when widened, will be nearly loft wider than linutsford-road near Warrington Bridge, and double lines of rails are worked there without in- convenience or accident, although there is not room in that length of road for a vehicle to pass between the tram lines and the kerbs. You will observe that it is proposed to put in a short length of railway No 2soas to avoid any interference with railway No I when railway No 2 is proceeded with. It is desirable that these lines should be carried by a .lean sweep into the middle of Grappen - hall.road. This may necessitate the removal of the tree planted in Victoria-square, as the branches of the tree will interfere with the cars. The Corpora- tion will be prepared to remove the tree to any position your Council may select. Please return the plans, and let me know your decision as soon as possible as instructions have already been given for the preparation of specifications and for ob- taining of tenders—Yours faithfully, J. LYON WIC/m.3c. Edwin Bradshaw, Eeq , clerk of the Parish Council." The Chairman said the matter required very serious consideration. He proceeded to deal with Mr whittess arguments and contended that aecorcline to the pl in the footpath alongside the church-yard wall woull not be wide enough. Mr Pearson : Will our decision be respected ? The Chairman : I think that whatever we deciie to- night, from what the Town Clerksa.dtome. will be carefull9 considered. He (Mr Whittle) impressed his own views noon ma very strong!y te thousht that if we decided upon a stogieline we should be ;eking a rnistake. Mr Pearson I don'c think the arguinent is a etrong, one for the double line. The Chairman : We have been consid wing this all along as a tramway, but we must not forg-ct the fact that it is not a tramway, it is a railway, and ic is cuing to be used as a railway. The C-rooration are making the connection and they are under a covenant with the proposed Northwich Light Railway to make the con- nection and take over the traffic as provided r in the (he'.er. That is a very serious thiog. We shall have a railway passing through this very parr. A single car is one thing,but a train load of goods—the number of waggons we have never been able to gather, bet I think it would he according to the power they can get from the electricity suPSl9—is a different matter. The Clerk said he took it that the Corporation might enter into an agreement with the Warrington and North- wieh Light Railway promoters, and oot that they must do so. Mr Pemberton said the Corporation had already made a private agreement with the Warrington and Nerthwicb Light Rail wav promoters. The Chairman said he did not think the Council would be justified in inflicting upon the inhabitants of Stockton Death for all time a throe feet footpath. Mr Bouchier considered that it was ratLer ignoring the Council to make the alteration without first approaching them. It was very questionable whether they would have been approached at all if the Chair- man's attention had net been drawn to the matter and he waited upon the Town Clerk. He Proposed that the Clerk be instructed to write to the Town Clerk of War- riaton giving the reasons why the Parish Council thought a single line would be better opposite the church than a double line. If the Corporation did not fall in with their sueeesticrt he moved that a similar Letter he sent tu the Board of Trade. Mr Pearson seconded, and the motion was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>BRUNSW/CII M UTUAL :</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of the above society Mr W 11 Leathwood delivered a lecture on" Some of tie doctrines of the Uhurch of England." There was a moderate attendance, Mr G A Hayes occupying the chair. Messrs W Millington, T Saunders, and A Hayes afterwards addressed the meeting, a vote of thanks being accorded to Mr Leathwood o the motion of Mr Alfred Dodd. POOLE'S MYRIORAMA</p>
        <p>The above company has been at the Public Hall during the past week. We are pleased to note that the show still maintains its aeservedly high reputa- tion for being thoroughly up-to-date. In spite of other local attraction there ha* been goad audiences each evening. Those who wish to spend an instruc- tive and entertaining evening should certainly visit the show before it leaves the town. TEDIPERANCH AT HALTON.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Sons of Temperance—Rose of Halton Division, was held on Saturday evening in the Village Hall, under the presidency of Mr F Hankey. There was a good attendance. Three new members were initiated. Short addresses were given by Messrs T II Whyte, Thomas Heeson and D Bisbrown, of Run- corn, and by Messrs C P Bradbury and William Looker, of Holton. Mr Looker referred to the pro- gress the Order was making in Halton. The division was formed 12 months ago and from twelve mem- bers they had increased to 45 They also had a sucessful cadet section with 31 members and two more had been proposed for membership. BILLIARDS—A Bo Y CHAMPION.</p>
        <p>Last Friday exhibition games of billiards were played at Wilson's Hotel, Runcorn, by Master To hill, 12 years of age, who lays claim to the title of the boy champion. In the afternoon he was met by Mr Wildgcose and an exciting game resulted in a win for Mr Wildgoose by the narrow margin of eight. His best breaks were 37, 28. 24, 22 and 21, and the boy's best efforts were 31, 21:1, 23,22 and 21. In the evening Master Tothill played with Mr W Brimelow a 500 game and came out an easy winner, having completed the-total when Mr Brimelow was 191. The lad's best breaks were 55. 31, 29, 24 and 21. He afterwards engaged Mr Wildgoose in a 200 game and won by 129, his highest break being 58. There was a large attendance and the exhibition aroused the keenest interest. The boy's playing was little short of wonderfal. Both games were completed in less than two hours,including a couple of five minute's intervals. DEATH AND FUNERAL OP Miss BARCLAY, The death took place at Liverpool-road, Chester, on Thursday last week,of Miss Rdith Barclay,second daughter of the late Canon Barclay, who was for 40 years vicar of Runcorn. The deceased lady was 53 years of age. She was in Runcorn so recently as upon the occasion of the National School bazaar,and was. deeply respected throughout the district. During her father's life Miss Barclay took a great and a practical interest in church matters. She was for many years superintendent of the Girls' Sunday School, and during the latter part of her father's life she superinteoded a great many of the parochial organizations. She gave to the Parish Church the beautiful reredos, the altar rails, and the altar frontal, and to St Michael's Chuut the splen- did east window in memory of her parents— The remains of the deceased lady were convey d from Chester by road on Monday, and were interred at noon in the Runcorn Parish Churchyard. The funeral was largely attended. The service in the church was conducted by the Vicar (thr. Rev Canon Wood), and that at the graveside by the Revs J Howson and 11 B Firth.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER POULTRY AND I'IGEIN SHOW.—The schedule of the Manche iter great show ef priz multry, pigeons, cats,rabbits, cavies, sod mica, to be held in St James's Hail, Oy.ford-streec, Manchester, on the 16th, 17th, and 18;h December, privides tor 400 clastes. Thircy-two judges have been appointed, all of them well-known authorities on the points of the respective breeds upon whose merits they have to speak. Sir H F de Trafford, Bart., is pr.sident, and the snow in east years has bees honoured with entries from tier Msjosty the Queen. Manchester show is now in the first flight, and ranks as one of the; classical events of toe season. Entries close on the 29th November, end prizs lisle can be obtained from Mr John W Hirst, 2, Ridgefield, Manchester. Z"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The 168th quarterly meeting in connection with this society was held in the Co operative Hall, Itarlestown on Monday evening. Mr D Forrester presided and there was a large attendance of members. The report and balance sheet for the past quarter showed the cash receipts to be £12,911 Sc 8d being an increase on the corresponding quarter of £2030 68 511. The net profits realised were £1929 7s 24 whine it was proposed to dispose of by paying a dividend of 28101 in the £ to mem- bers, absorbing .ClB5l 18s 91 and £1 Os 21d on non- members checks, £lO to Worktueu's Compensation fund, and the balance £66 2s 20 to dividend re- serve fund. Daring the quarter 02 new mem- bers had been admitted and 29 had with- drawn leaving 1870 on the register, 1413 of whom had brought in checks. The report and statement of accounts were adopted. Messrs Owen and Mullins were re-elected to the committee. Mr D Forrester was nominated as President. Mr Jones was appointed delegate to the Wholesale Society's quarterly meeting. After some little discussion upon the question of the agitation of private traders against the Co-operative movement. it was decided to guarantee £lOO to the defence fund. A dona- tion of £3 3s was voted to the Manchester -Royal Infirmary. A member was expelled from the society for refusing to give to the committee information in accordance with the rules, which they con- sidered would be to the best interests of the society. The meeting was adjourned until M.onday next in order that the proposed new cneck system might be dealt with.</p>
