      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDA V: NOVEMBER 21, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDA V: NOVEMBER 21, 1902</p>
        <p>BETHESDA P.S.A. SOCIETY, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The ordinary meeting of this Brotherhood was very largely attenued last Sunday afternoon, Mr Samuel Coventry in the chair. The speaker was Mr Frank Littler, one of the joint secretaries, who gave a very able address, based on the society's motto, " One is your Master, even Christ, and all ye are brethren." Space will not permit more than a brief summary. Among the enemies of the PS A movement, which are not few, said the speaker, the only one that is likely to be dangerous is the one from within,and the object of addressing the society on the subject of the motto of the Brotherhood was with the idea of placing the proper ideal before them that there might be truth in the inward parts. The motive from which the movement sprang by the inspira- tion of Ged, was, in the speaker's opinion, but the expression of a divine unrest in the hearts of a vast number of men, who chafed at the strife, divi- sion, clique, creeds, and dogmas which so separated men who should be joined together in good deeds, and was the outcome of a belief that it was possible for men to meet together on a platform wide and tolerant enough to enable them to agree on the grand fendamentals of belief, and sink all these petty difficulties and jealousies in the grand recogni- tion of a universal brotherhood. Such an idea was expressed by the motto of the movement " For One is your Master, even Christ ; and all ye are breth- ren. He proposed to speak on this occasion only ' with reference to the first part of the subject Our Master, Christ " and at once stated teat, because of His unique position, because of His sublime teaching and perfect life, Christ de- manded as His right, that all who believe in Gad and seek after truth should acknowledge Him as their Master. His (the speaker's) idea was not, however, to attempt to correct anybody's opinions with regard to His personality ; what he wanted to do was to call forth that living faith in them which should find its only expression in action. Opinions which are merely the expression of a man's intel- lectual conclusions are of very little use in com- parison with a deep strong faith which comes out of the heart and impels him to obey its dictates. The dominant note in the life and teaching of this One whom we acknowledge as Master is obedience; it is the first word we hear from His lips, it is the note which is heard in the hour of darkest trial in Gethsemana,it is tha voice heard on Calvary, "I am come not to do my own will, but the will of Him that sent Me," and our service to Him most also be one of obedience to the will of Goa, which perfect will he came to reveal to us. Confessions of faith, creeds, dogmas, reliance in anything another may have done—all these are worthless except as they give birth to this spirit of obedience, thus prompting to the following after of the Master. The severe words of condemnation are not spoken to those who did not accept this or that form of belief, but to those who knowing the will of God refused to do it. " Not every one that eaith unto me Lord, Lord ! shall enter the kingdom, but he that doeth the will of my Father whish is in heaven." Such teaching may be hard and difficult to follow, but the voice that calls is the voice of love, calling men from the swine-troughs of sin into the Father's house, away from the gulf of degradation into which they have fallen, into the glorious perfection of the children of God. Such, persisted the speaker, appeared to Him to be the spirit, might He say the foundation on which this P.S.A. was built, and he appealed to those who composed its membership,to lift up their eyes to this glorious ideal, and join hands and hearts in obedient love ani service to Him who is their Master, even Christ. The soloist was Miss Harrop, who sang with taste and expression the solos "To the sound of the Bells " and " The Holy City."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>MR. SAM WOODS CANDIDATUAZ FOR I HE NEWTON DIV!SION.</head>
        <p>MR. SAM WOODS CANDIDATUAZ FOR I HE NEWTON DIV!SION.</p>
        <p>Oe S iturday evening Mr Sam We ode addressed a social gathering of his worlarrs at the Coemerative Hall, Eerteetown. After tea cal le.len partaken of, an enter- tainment teking the form of singing, reciting and gramophone selections was held. Mr Woods seed teat all alone the line he had met with unfailing and unetiated kindness. He bad not yet been all round the c auvetuency but tie hoped to do so before long. The district was au unusually large one and took time to traverse, but if the pr.•sent outlook could be considered, the-e did not seem to be any likelihood of an election for three or four years, so that he had plenty of time. He did not want to talk to them about education nor did he want Le bother teem with a speech on the War. The War was not finished yet, and although they had seen, the great loss of life there had been in connec- tion with the nefer, there was one aspect they had not yet gee°. What was to be done with the thousands of men who had been torn from their country to fight the battles of England, and bad come home again, meined. What was to be done with the widows of those who had been kided. He thought that if the present Government allowed these people to exist on the alms they received from the people of England, it wee a disgrace to the country' He wanted to speak a few words on the question of Labour Representation. Since he decided to accept the candidature of the Newton Division as s pure labour candidate, he had been requested to stand in a similar capacity for Cleveland, but as he had previously accepted Newton, be thought it was his tnenden duty to decline the honour of repre- senting Cleveland and he had done thie. (Hear, hear.) , In doing their utmost for him and WS candidature, they I were not fighting fer him, they were fighting for the principles they all held dear. He thouget it was the duty of every Labour Representative and every Member , of Parliament to consult .his constituents. He always believed in keeping in touch with the people represented, and obtaining the'r opinion on any matter before voting either for or against it. lie was told that they had 40 millions of people in the , United Kingdom. He took it that at the very leeet 35 millions of these people belonged to the working classes. They believed in representation and popelation going together, The Home of Commons Was composed of 670 membere, 356 of whom blouged to the capitalistic class. The balance wee on the wrong side, and be thought the time had come when a change was neve- sary. He was glad to see from the signs of the times there were indications in the pur-ly industrial classes and the middle class populetion that there would be a ehange. The speaker te'erred at length to the question of the payment of labour members of the House of Common!. In his opinion this ought to be done. There was no disgrace in receiving such pevment. This was a vital question affecting Labour that they had t coneider. It was a disgrace that our legislature email I depend on the depth of a man's pocket, and not on the strength of his intellect. Respecting his emponents statement at a previous meeting thst if they returned him (the speaker) to Parliament they would return a Home Rule member, he would iuferm his audience that, he was in the House of Corernens when the " Home Rule " Bill was first introduced, and he voted for it. He voted for it conscientiously because he thought it would bring Macey to the Irish people. He did not for a moment consider that it would mean separation from the Imperial Parliamrnt. He believed in Horne. Rule for Scotland and also for Wales and England. He believe I that these countries should be bound together under the Imperial Parliament. No one knew tho business of Scotland better than the Scotch, and the same remark applied to Wales, Ireland and England. Referring to " Procer?ure Reform " the speak,)r said that the pro- cedure of the House of Crommees was not what it should be by a long way. If they got Labour members in the House they could depend upon it that this course would be altered. He asked them not to be disheartened because they could not revolutionise the world in a day. He asked them to respect their opponents. They did not rely on any pelitical party, these took help from any. They would pursue an independent course on behalf of Labour. (Applz.usl.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>EARLICSTOWN WARGIIATE CHURCH SOCIAL</head>
        <p>EARLICSTOWN WARGIIATE CHURCH SOCIAL</p>
        <p>A social and dance in connection with this church, was held in t'aist Wargrava schoolroom on Friday evening The roomwas tastefully decorated by several of the lady members of the congregation. About 35 couples were present and dancing was com- menced, to the strain of Mr Peplow's band, at 8 o'clock and kept up till about midnight. Songs were rendered at interval 4 by Miss 14 a Jones. The duties of M.C's were ably carried out by Messrs W II Sweetlove and R Carson. THEFT 07 A WATCH AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Harry Corbett was charged with steal- ing a silver watch and guard value .£2, the property of William B Linaker. 3, Chester-road, Newton, on December 20th, 1901.—Presecutor istated that on the date named he saw the watch and guard safe in a box in his bedroom. Later in the day he missed them, and prisoner, who lodged at the house, had also disappeared. It was subsequently discovered that the articles had been pawned by the prisoner at Ashton.— PC Lennie said he received the prisoner into custody and charged him, and he replied " That's right."—lt was stated tliat, prisoner was also " wanted "for absconding from the militia.— He was fined 20s and costs, with the alternative of a month's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>PILES CURMl).—Oxien Pile Cure. Safe, speedy, and guaranteed cure for all forma of hrmorrholds,blind or itching piles. Of all Chemists, or from The Giant Oxie Co., 27, shot lane, London, B.C. (3),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>The ayor and Corporation at the</p>
        <p>Parish Church,</p>
        <p>In accordance with a well-established custom in War- rington, the Mayor invited the arembers of the Corpora- tion, the officials, local magistrates, membas of the Board of Guardians, and townsmen generally Co accom- pany him to the Parish Church on Sunday morning, this being the first Sunday since his re-election as Mayor of the Borough. The invitation was not as generally responded to as on former occasions and the procession to and from the Parish Church was smaller and less imoosing than usual. The new Councillors were Orksent and formed perhaps the only new feature of the procession. As usual, the Volunteer Band headed the procession, and immediately behind walked sections of the mounted infantry, cyclists and the Volunteer force under the command of ; Colonel Ridgway. Members of the St. John Am- bulance Brigade with Captain Waring and Councillor Hulse at their head followed next and they were suc- ceeded by the Fire Brigade, under the direction of their veteran superintendent, Mc Frank Turner. Tne pictur- esque figures of the sword and mace bearers preceded the Mayor's carriage in which sat his Worship (Mr. J. C. Parr), the Deputy Mayor (Alderman James clotvh), the Town Clerk (Mr J Lyon Whittle) and Alder- , man Francomb. Then followed the Aldermen and Councillors in their order of precedence according to seniority of service. rhere were present : Alderman Sutton and Hutchinson, Council Les Sm ithurat,Bennet't, ' Pierpoint, Rogers, Tinnion, Joha Roberts, James Evans, J Baird, J Wilkins-ma Sairio3oo,T Barton, J Brophy, J Upson, James Dodds and John Pemberton. The Borough magistrates present were :—Mr F Monks, Mr T Birchall, 51r R F Fairclough, Mr J W Hayward, Mr S Wright, and Mr H Thornton. The Board of Guardians was represented by Messrs A Jollev, T H Ashcroft, George Slather, James Payton, and F 11 Aukland (relieving officer). The Corporation officials present were :—Mr 8-Anley Wilson (deputy Town Clerk),Mr J Fairhurst (oorough treasurer), Mr T Longdin (borouzh surveyor), Dr Guest Gorman (Medical Officer of Health), Mr W 8 Hadi.3ck (gas engineer), Mr F Taylor (secretary), Mr G F F Waite (borough magistrates' clerk), Mr F Tiinperley (borough treasurer's department), Mr Wiliam Jones (chief school attendance officer), Mr II V L Mathias (assiatant electrical engineer), Mr Fletcher (cicrk of work 3 for the naw Technical Institute). The general public included Mr Holbrook, Mr Shacklady, Mr It.bby Mr Ferguson, and Mr Hesketh.</p>
        <p>Promptly at ten o'clock, the procession started from the Town Hall and procee.ied along the main streets to the Parish Church. The precession was met at the gates by the churchwardens and the church was entered by the West door. Many of those in the procession had not attended the church since the renovation and by enter- ing at the West doer they had an excellent opportunity of noticing the marked improvement which has been made in the interior of the church. The removal of the organ to a more suitable position near the choir stalls and the exposure of the stonework of the Church by the removal of the plaster has effected a wonderful improvement and elves the building a Cathedral-like appearance. The pews in the centre of the church were reserved for those in the procession, and as they took their seats the I ,rge congregation sto rho sorvico wis conducted by the Rector (Rev Canon Will's), aorist ,d by the Revs E J Willco3ks, A S Caincrm and, A F White The musical portion of the service was im- pressive and beautiful, the singing of the choir reflecting credit upon the organist and choirmaster, Mr Darby. The To Denrn was rendered to the setting of Smart in F, and was sung by the choir. The anthem was " Draw near, all ye people," &amp;o. Mr James Poole sang the recitative and air with good taste and the choir gave an excellent interpretation of the chorus.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SERMON ON PATRIOTISM</head>
        <p>SERMON ON PATRIOTISM</p>
        <p>The Rev A S CAMERON preached from the words "For he loveth our nation,and he bath built us a synagogue" (St Luke vii verse 5). He wanted to say a few words, the preacher said, on the great subject of patriotism. Any study of the Old Testament, however superficial, would convince one that God dealt with nations, lead- ing, guiding, teaching and perfecting them, for their destiny. God dealt with the individual for the selco of the nation. It wes always intensely interesting to notice how much there was in Holy Scripture that prompted one to believe that nations were reeognized as entities in the eight of Almighty God. It was as if not only every individual bad his or her guardian angel, but every nation had its angelic representative in the pres- ence of God. Nations were represented in the audience chamber of God, and it was a common-place among those who had studied history to observe that nations had functions, vocatiens, and ministries to fulfil. They knew, for example, that ancient Rome, had bequeathed to humanity her legacy of civilize- tion, organizetion, orderly government and law ; they knew that the Greeks had bequeathed to humanity her legacy of art, poetry, and all the beauty of the in- tellectual life, and the Hebrew nation had bequeathed to U 9 all the twenty of the spiritual life, the reality of God's presence in man's soul, and the reality of the closer walk with God. It wes the vocation and minis- try of these nations to b.queach that legacy to the human race, and if nations had functions and ministries to fulfil in the sight of God the true patriot was the man who recognizsd that fect,and because he did to he sought to co-operate with the purposes of God for his country. The true patriot was he who realised that God had a work for his country to do, and, therefore, it was his bounden duty to work with God ; to set himdelf on the side of the things of God and to work for the fulfilment. the desthey and purpose God had in view. The true patriot was net the man who adopted the attitude of indiscriminate praise. The true patriot did not say " I am an Englishman, and, tnerefore, everything English is good, and is managed in the best posible way," but bad his eyes open to the defects of his country. True love could not really he blind. The tree patriot would be loyal, and they know what that meant. It meant legality, lawfulness, obedience. The true patriot would never be an agitator, a seditious person, or a "stirrer up of strife," but would always seek to remend to the claims which his country made upon him. Be would b. a lover of his country, understanding that God had a purpose in view for his race and nation, and would endeavour to fall in with that purpose and work for it, to further the building no of the character of the people on the lines Gad would have it built up, and he would, as the centurion of old, build the synagogue. But to look at some of the influ- entsets which tended to build up the character of.nations. The thought of the people was built up gr ideally into the character of the people. What we believed in and thought about, that we gradually tended to become. As an instance of this, the preacher referred to the opinion of an eminent German who pointed out that in order to understand English politics, Eiglish literature and English journalism it was uecevsary to first understand the language of the Eiglish Bible. Let them,continued the preacher,look at France. They all had in their mind theterrible upheaval at the end of the 18;b century. To what cause was the French Revolution due? They knew the ordinary reasons alleged, the stock reasans--the unequal balance of power between Crown and people, fuerlaliarn maintaining its rights and privileges long after its duties had lapsed. But there wsre other reasons. Voltaire and Rousseau were addressing the thinkers of the French, and the thinkers passed on the Opinions to the man in the Street, and gradually the thoughts and opinions of Voltaire aod Rousseau became the opinions of the man in tne street, the opinions of the gutter. and blood, murder, and rapine wore the result. What was it saved Eagland at that time ? Why , had we no otehreale ? Was it not because Bishop Butler with his wonderful writing and preaching and John Wesley and George Whitfield, with the love and sweetness of the Gospel prevented men's thoughts and convictions being led in the evil direction in which they were so rapidly led across the channel ? Walter Bosant started on his good work in East London, in direct hostility and antipathy to the work of the Eng- lish Church, and yet he had left this saying on record : " That if it had not been for the work of the Church in East London they would have had a bloodier revolution than the French." It was the national conviction, and the though-3 of the people that tended to produce char- aeter, and if that were so—and he believed it was diffi- cnlt to contradict the plain and simple state- ment that thought and conviction built up char, der —could they be unmoved and rest satisfied with the d'rection in which modern English thought was moving? Were they entir,ly satisfied with the various opinions that went to shape iind mould the thoughts of the people They would all agree, he supposed ,that the most powerful influence amongst us for mating men think was journalism. There was in modern jeurneliean'much to admire. They admired the enterprise,skileaud marvellous organization and they were deeply thankful for the healthy moral tone of the leading journals, hut there was much was there not, that was irresponsible and haphazard, ill-educated and anooymou,. There woe much in modern journalism that unbalanced men's minds. In the worst kind of journalism there were recerde of the extraordinary ab- norrnitice and enorminies of life that unbalanced the mind and fed it on drugs instead of upon solid food. It was like feeding the body on narcotics and inteoricante instead of food and drink. Fiction had a great influence in directing thought. They were thankful for the great authors, aid wished they tfere more widely read but there was in modern fiction a heartless tone of cynicism and peesimismth at were in reality a contradiction of the love of Almighty God. If God Omnipotent reigned vice could not ultim- ately be triumphant and therefore to propagate cynicism and Pessimism was really to propagate a lie. There were other thinge,too,that were much to be dreaded. If they neglected the laws of chastity and peTity, if sexual love was unregulated there was an abyss before them into which they Must fall. Of all lessons written clear and large across the page of history none was more plain than this—that where purity was disregarded, where the marriage bond was made light of, there annihilation and destruction must come. In conclusion, let thorn turn to the more positive side and consider not what they must ovoid, but what they</p>
        <p>mast do. Broadly speaking, there were three great influences in their lives laying on oue side the influence of heredity—the example of those they admired, the influence of the literature they read, and the influence of the society, the class, the rank in which they moved and lived and had their being. In the spiritual world et was the same, and God influenced them on precisely the same lines. There was the personal example ef Christ their Saviour, the great literary influence of the Bible, and the great social influenoe of the Church of whien he had made them members. They ehould seek to build up toe synagogue, to mould the character of the people of the nation they loved, first by themselves cepying the example of (Jurist, who was the heal ef every man. Let them aim at Him, and cmy Him in life. He had heard it said that there were fewer real men in Eagland now than there were in the days of Elize.beth. Surely the war in South Africa proved this, if nothing else, that when it was understood there was a direct call for men they would be forth- coming, that when Englishmen understmoi there was a direct Claim upon them for the service of Geer country they would nobly respond. Tney must endeaveur to bring home to the minds of the peeple that there was a claim upon them in the way of patriotism at borne. There was not only need for heroes on foreign service but for heroes at homeen the ordinary humdrum beetle of life where were co medals to reward and no ribbms to decorate. It needed a hero te stand up for while, lie knew ti be honest and beyond reproach. Thera was not only the copying of the esample of Christ, but the influence of the greet book. the Bible. There was much , thee Was said about the Bible now that tended uojke,tly to upset the faith of seine. ,dany said that the authors had changed, that the dates were wrong, and that they could not think of the Bible as they used to do; but let them read what those qualified to speak thought, and they would find that the discoveries of the critics enrich- ed one's sense of what one owed to God's Almighty Word. There was nothing which coul I interfere with the message of God toe Holy Spirit pat was iii the Bible. Tee preacher proceeded ta @peak of the great social influences of the Church. If tasey worked for the go, dof the Cnurch the work weul I be for toe good a the nation. The true patriot was the man who real tel that God had a purpose for his nation, a great destiny in store for the future of his race, and because he recog- nized that, and sought to set himself on the side of the things of God, and bmause he loved the natlon, he bude up the synagogue. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ The service c)acluied with the singing of the Natini al Anthem. The offernries were on tieb%lf of the choir fund and tealisied £25.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council was held on Tuesdky at Dutton. Present J Knowln. (in the chair), Messrs C Roylance, J Bolshaw, A fl ralbot, S Clark-, J Stoke", J Whitlow, T Bazt(r. C E Linaker, T Lei, T Wright, H Mawdslev, G Pilln, P E Bretherton, J W Bsguley, J Spruce, A E ktoo, R Billington, A Wallicgtoi, Admiral Jones-Parry, W Turner, J B Chad wick,wiqi Mnsars,G F A ihton (clerk), WE Hough iggistant cleck),Dr Adams(tnedical officer), J Swinton, W Digfe (sarvevors),Farrington (inipect)r).</p>
        <p>THEL WALL NUESINCE. MR. NAYLOR WRITES AGAIN.</p>
        <p>Under date November 17th Mr Naylor wrote from London enetesing cuttings from " Truth " and the " Lancet " which severely conderned the negligence of te R :acorn Rural District Council in dealing with the nuisance. In the course of his let er Mr Naylor stated that Mr Mclntyre made U 56 of the statement attributed to him by Mr Naylor and he and his witnesses were prepared to prove it Why should the Coune,l be forcing ou Teel- wall a sewagescheme when the effi sent from the one foot sewer has been cu.tified by their recent analysis as v ry good ? With reference to the diphtheria outbreak what was the medical officer doing C In the one fatal CRS, the HimeOffice will be likely to in,ecvene. The publicity given to the nuisance by the press had made it of such national importance that the legislature would nave to take notice of it? Toe Clerk said he hai replied to the letter pointing out that so far as the report was concerned it had been. taken as read, all the Councillors having received a copy Mr Baxter : I think we ought to take exception that part of the r•port from Mr Naylor which contends that this Council has done nothing. I certainly think that is a lie even though it be printed in a paper like " Truth."</p>
        <p>The ChaTrman said nobody cared for Naylor or LonT:. Toy had tried to deal fairly with thetu both. Mr Baxter : It is perfectly ridiculous the amount of prin"ers ink thrown away .n the alled nuisanco. The Cl&lt;srir : Flag anybody anythicxto siy wi-Jo rei- pect to the question. The Chairman : Any gentleman any remarks to make ?</p>
        <p>, ft Member : I think it in time 991lieshin was said in reply to the attacks made by the press. kir Spruce thought it was somsthmr, alarming th., ex- tent to which the thing had gone.flo felt persuaded that the rnembers of that Council were prepared to do the right thing if it could be pointed out to them Re thought that the thing to go so far without any protest fro n gentlemen who gave their ticne for the benefit of the district was s refl'ction on the Council. Their local papers were not behind in the matter. There had been remarks at times under different head. inge pointing out the remissness of the Council. Pio, for one, 974.4 prepared to vote in Rupport of any measure to abate ths nuisance. They only wanted justice and fair play and wished to do jus ice to Thelwali and the whole distrier.</p>
        <p>Mr Wright : Mr Naylor will not be satisfied until we have got an injunction:to chse the tannery. Admiral Jones-Parry then drew at'ention to certain letters received from Messrs Macdoer. ftylands and Naylor, with respect to the treatment of sewage from their respective properties in Waste-lane, Thal wall, and mooed that copies of such letters be sent to each mem- ber of the Connell for their consideration. LI the houre. of a very lengthy speech, Admiral Party said that. during tie lest week he had visited the place where the alleged nuisance existed. He had spent six days in his investigation putting his head, so some people had remarked, where his tail ought to be, During that time he had smelt a distinct odour and so had his wife and friends. The smell seemed to ad.() near Hewitt's farm. He had not been able to really locate it thought it undoubtedly came from the sewer. The Chairman : I propose you (turning to the admiral) and 51lr Wallington write the letters. Mr Stokes said that if the letters would throw any light on the matter they certainly ought to have copie.i. The proposition was unanimously passed. The Medical Officer said that Admiral Partv'a rL marks proved to his satisfaction that the smell arose from the one foot sewer' which opened out near Hewitt's farm about 253 yards fr rn the tannery and crossed a point where the sewage of Mr Narlor's cottages came down. Toe sewer got its smell from that point. As to it being prevalent when Admiral Parry was they, it was caused by the heavy rains. Mr Lang had com- plained ablut the aline thing. It was the one foot sewer'sewage that smel,ed and not a tannery effldent and which caused the sickness. Admiral Party's statement brought iv-me the fact that it was their one foot sewer which was the cause of the Melt The tanner effluent could no more get into the one foot sewer than it could get into that Board room. --------</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>PRoPOSED ALTERATION OF THE COUNOIL MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>PRoPOSED ALTERATION OF THE COUNOIL MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>„... . Mr Linaker said that, as he intimated at the close of the Guardians meeting, he begged to move the appoint- ment of a committee to consider the advisability of holding the Di.trict Council meetings monthly instead of fortniphtly. It was very diffic.alt for mernhers at the far end of the parish to avoid spending the whole day. They would have to have their Guardians se usual, but this in no wise necessitated them holding the Council fortniebtlY. He did not know of any Rural Council, Town Council or Urban Council holding their meetings everc f ortnight. The most im- portant Council business gencrallv come on laet when I members had to hurry away. He hoped the committee would provide time anfaci,nt to devote to all thebusinesa He moved that the Chairman and Vic.-chairman oi the Guard'ans and of the Council wi't the chairmen of the various committees be appointed the committee to inquire into the matter. - Mr Spruce seconded and the proposition was passed. This was all the business of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Will of the Late Mr. E. W. T. Richmond.</head>
        <p>Will of the Late Mr. E. W. T. Richmond.</p>
        <p>Probate of the will, dated 25'h February. 1900. of Mr. Edmond Walter Tyrrell Richmond (38), of the firm of Richnaoni and Co., Limited, London and Warrington, gas stove and meter manufacturers, who died on the 4h September last as the result of a bicych accident, has been granted his widow (Mrs. Laura Richmond, of Darley Dale, Matlock Bath), and Mr John Freeman Atkins, of 13, Bootle- street, llandsworth, Birmingham, surgeon' and Mr William Cassell. of 90, Cannon-street, London, accountant. The testator bequeathed to Mr Atkins £lOO and to his wife .£163, his household effects, [ the benefit of any policies of insurance on his life [ registered in her name, and the income during her I widowhood of his residuary estate, or in the event of her re-rnarriacte an annuity during the rsmain- [der of her HUI of .£lOO. and subject to 11-u- int-,rest he left his residuary es!-ate in tot for hie chtldron, The estate ha q been valued at £27.283 15 t 61 gross, including .£24 263 123 61 in net personalty.</p>
