      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By "OvaUsti</p>
        <p>If the Warrington team is not ashamed of itself it ought to be, seeing that it failed on Saturday to defeat what was practically the Broughton Rangers "A" team. It is true that the points were divided and Warrington thus did better than on the occasion of their last visit to Broughton, but they had a glorious opportunity of taking the full maximum of points, and they failed. Somehow or other, Warrington generally fail on such occasions as these. If they have a big match on they can be relied upon to do well, but give them what is known as "a soft thing" in football parlance and the opportunity is wasted. How many times have Warrington met a weak team of the Reds at Salford and lost ? On nearly every occasion in recent years ; while even at home the worse plight the visitors are in aud the worse Warrington play. It is just as well that they do sometimes atone for this annoying Unit by an excellent display against their most formidable opponents.</p>
        <p>Though nobody in Warrirgtm seemed confident of the local team's success at Broughton it is curious that at Broughton the Rangers supporters had no hope of escaping defeat. They were quite resigned to their fate and only wondered morosely what sort of a score Warrington would put up No doubt they argued that, as Warrington and the Rangers at full strength are well-matched, then it must be an'easy matter for War- rington, with wily two absentees, to beat the Rangers with no less than six of the regular team missing. This is logical reasoning, but then a Broughtonian is hardly likely to know that the absence of two of the Warring- ton threequarters disorganises the team about as much as the absence of five backs from the Rangers. The latter's reserves are more capable for one thing, but principally Warrington appear to enter the field beaten instead of confident in their own 'ability to win. Con- fidence goes a long way in football, and a bit of it dis- played at Broughton would have won the match.</p>
        <p>There are other reasons why Warrington failed to win. One is that the homestets made a plucky and determined fight. Primarily, however, in my opinion —for I resisted the temptations of the County match and have ever since regretted it—Warrington did not beat the Rangers because the half-backs played shock- ingly and Isherwood and Harris were on opposite sides. Whoever is to blame for this change from the team list took a very serious responsibility and merits severe condemnation. There is no doubt that if Harr;s had been on Edmunds' wing he must have scored, for Isherwood more than once made an opening which was absolutely wasted because he was too fast for his wing man.' The three-quarter line was at sixes and sevens throughout, and as an attacking force was just about as effective as paper in a gale. Cohesion was all that was required, and with Isherwood and Harris together Warrington were bound to have scored, and to score meant to win on Saturday. I think Harris had two passes given him during the game, and even outsiders like the Manchester reporters were amused at Warrington so completely starving the only back they had capable of scoring. Experience is said to teach, but it takes a mighty lot of it sometimes to have the desired effect.</p>
        <p>Broughton were guilty of the same bad policy at first for up to half-time they had Widdeson in the centre and Hampson on the wing, neither man being anymse. After the interval, ho wever, they changed places, and Warrington had plenty to do to keep the speedy Widde- son out. This was a case of profiting promptly by experience, ;.nd the home team deserved to win as a reward for superior judgment and field-cr .ft. Though they had a slight advantage forward they could not get through the Warrington defence, which was certainly good enough for anything. The tackling on both sides was the only redeeming feature of a most miser- able display f football. Broughton attacked most fre- quently, but they had only one clear chance of scoring which y. ,ckersall muffed, and Duckworth did the same for Warrington when he could have walked in. I should think Broughton had quite ten shots at goal, which alll missed the mark, whereas the referee never gave Warrington a single penalty anywhere within range. This may have been fair, but it looked rather one-sided. Anyhow Warrington were not ill-plemed to get a point, and the Rangers were delighted at having escaped which seemed to them a certain defeat.</p>
        <p>Naturally there is not much to be said about the individual merits of the contestants in such a poor game. Findlow was smart at half, and Fielding and Barrett reliable, on the home side, while for Warrington Isherwood was the only man to do himself justice. Tie worked like a horse, and it is only to be regretted the more that his erorgies were so obviously wasted,except on the defence. Hubang and Hockenhall appeared un- able to do the right thing and both played far below form. So far this season, Bur.ess has shown none of the brilliancy which • frequently distinguished his play last season. Let us hope that, like, Christmas, it is coming.</p>
        <p>There were not many League matches of importance on Saturday. Halifax kept the lead by defeating Batley, though they sustained a misfortune in losing Morley, who got his arm broken. I am not surprised at this player's accident, for he is one of those men who won't be "held," and tome day retribution was bound to come. St. Helens did splendidly to win at Hull, but how is it the Saints succeeded where Wairington failed P Leigh and Runcorn drew, which was a fair result of a keen game. The result up-to-date is as follows:</p>
        <p>For. Agit. P. W. L. n. G. T. P. G. T. P.Pts walifax ll 10 0 1 13 17 77 6 3 19 El Broughton R Leigh ll 7 2 216 15 75 513 49 16 Swinton Runoorn Salford Warrington.— ....... Bra tford Oldham Wigan Hull Batley Nucl3ersll did fit Helms Widnes I' Runsiet .. ...... Brighouse Rgs Hull K Rovers ll 2 90 3830 16 21 95 4</p>
        <p>A big crowd of Waxringtonians attended the County engagement at Weaste, where Lancishire trounced Yorkshire by 13 points to nil. I ant told that Fish was easily the best man on the field. an I prayed in his finest form.He opened even the eyes of his friends with the grim way in which he tackled,but that crack on the head may have got his dander up. The Lancashire backs I generally were below par, but the forwards did splen- didly, and altogether the team was too powerful for the Tykes. Last week there was a mistake in the West Lane a- shire table as printed in this column, and judging by the number of times I was informed of the fact last Saturday I ,had better correct it at once. Leigh have lost a match. The table, to•date, is as follows. Points _ _</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Agat Pte. Leigh Warrin.gtoa Runcorn ........ .... 4 ... 1 ... 0 —. 3 ... 10 ... 0 ... 5 Wigan.. .......... ... 2 .. 1 ... 0 ... 1 ... 5 ... 3 ... 3 St. Helene 3 . 0 ... 2 ... 1 ... 3t 1 Widnes</p>
        <p>As a result of the bother at Runcorn after the Halifax match, the Runcorn ground has been suspended up to and including December 6th, the Linnets being mean- while ordered to play their matches on the ground of- the Birkenhead Club, to which alO per cent commis- sion on the gate-money has to be paid. Two of the three matches are home" matches, and Runcorn will, therefore, have to receive Hull at Birkenhead to-day, and Broughton Rangers there on December 6th. They play Oldham at Watersheddings on November 29th.</p>
        <p>I think myself, that the Runcorn Club has come very well out of an awkward matter. The order to play at Birkenhead may be a blessing in disguise, for it is quite on the cards that the Linnets will draw a bigger crowd at Birkenhead than they, usually have at Irwell-lane- In thaecase they will make money out of the trouble, and the only sufferers will be the Runcorn public, who will have to journey to Birkenhead to watch their pets play football. I should not have been so-prised at a much longer sentence of suspension, for were not Swinton driven from home for two months once ? The Runcorn Club is more to be congratulated than com- miserated.</p>
        <p>Birkeahead Wanderers play at Lancaster and ml. beck on the days on which Runcorn occupy their ground, while Leeds are the visitors on the intermedi- ate Saturday. For the next three weeks the river- siders should be treated to some superior football.</p>
        <p>-- -- Warrington malks the long journey to full again to- day to engage Kingston Rovers, who are second from the bottom of the Competition ladder. In all proba- bility Warrington will have a very hard game and need to show smart fortn to win. The Rovers are very strong forward and it will take all the energy of the Warring- ton eight to keep the backs supplied with opportunities. There is an important change in the composition of the team to-day, Brooks being given his first trial in the senior team, where he will play right centre to Harris. Roberts has also been dropped from the forwards, A Naylor, the returned Guardsman, filling the vacancy. If he plays in his old form Naylor should be a valuable acquisition to the team,which will be as follows : Knight, full back; Fish, Isherwood, Brooks, and Harris, threequarters ; Burgess and Hockenhall, half- backs •, Beardman, Taylor, Morrison, Naylor, J Swift, AW swift, Forster and Edmondson, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>CLAIM BY A _BURTONWOOD</head>
        <p>CLAIM BY A _BURTONWOOD</p>
        <p>, ,</p>
        <p>„</p>
