      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>FARMER.</head>
        <p>FARMER.</p>
        <p>A cow Run Over.</p>
        <p>Before His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands K.C., at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, Ralph Fora haw, farmer, Burtonwood, claimed com- pensation for damages to a cow alleged to have been inflicted by Thomas Fyrna,egg dealer Colborne —Mr Swift, solicitor, Sc. Helens, represented the plaintiff and Mr a L Riley defended. In stating the case, Mr Swift informed His Honour that the plaintiff was a farmer at Yew Tree Farm, Burtonwood, and on the 18th of July last, the plaintiff's nephew was driving cows out of the farm-yard back to pas- ture, after they had been milked. There were seven cows and as he got in the lane he saw the defend- ant driving a shandry in the opposite direction. The aefendant passed six of the cows safely but on coming to the seventh the defendant's vehicle caught it on the hip and knocked it down. The artjmal was run over and the hip bone broken. If the defendant had drawn up or gone slower, the accident would not have happened. John Abbott, nepnew of the plaintiff, gave evi- dence in support of Mr Swift's statement. He shouted to the defendant to stop but he did net do so. The cow was knocked down and the vehicle passed over it. Ralph Forshaw, the plaintiff, said he heard the defendant shout to the last witness Beep your cows out of the road." The defendant called AG the farm on the following Monday and said be was not going to do anything in the way of paying com- pensation. The cow cost £l6 103.—Richard ESamond- son, veterinary surgeon, St Helens' stated that on examining the cow he found the hip-bone broken. The vehicle must have struck the caw with con- siderable force to break the bane. The cow had depreciated in value about £3 owing to the acci- dent. There had also ben a loss in milk. _ _ _</p>
        <p>For the defence Ur Riley contended that the defendant had used all reasonable care and had acted cireumepectly7.</p>
        <p>Defendant was called and stated that just as he was passing the cows, one of the cows " cupped " the other and knocked it against the trap. The animal was knocked down and a wheel passed over it. At the time he did not think any damage had been done as the cow got up and walked away_ In answer to bis Honour witness said be was driving en his right side. William Burke, carter, Golborne, corroborated. As far as he could see Nynn acted as carefully as possible under the circumstances. Police Constable Bateman stated that defendant was driving on the proper side of the road at the rate of about four miles an hour. Witness did not see the accident..</p>
        <p>His Honour said the plaintiff was to be pitied for the injuries sustained to his cow but he (his Honour) was unable to find in his favour on the evidence submitted. Judgment would be given to the defendant with ca sts.</p>
        <p>• WIDNES MEN SMARTLY FINED FOR TRESPASSING. William Sdward Dys no and Bdward Dyson, father and son, of Wfdoes, were charged at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday with trespaasing in search of game on land in the possession and occupation of James Wright. Cuerdley,ort Novemier M.—Mr Ii A White prose- tutted and stated that on the Sunday in question air Wright and his son were in the farmyard when they heard shots fired from the direction of a pasture field which contained game. They went towards the field and saw three men Including the defendants in the field. Each carried a double-barrel breach-loading gun, and thy had two spaniel dogs with them. When they saw Mr Wright and his son they ran away. bat Mr Wright and his son gave chase. Mr Wright caught the senior defendant who said" did not know I was on your land. I have permission from your neighbour, Mr Johnson, to shoot over his land." He said he did not know who the third man was. Mr Wright took the senior defendant to air Johnson to see if he had given him permission as stated, acid Mr Johnson, in defendant's presence, denied that ho bad. Mr Wright's son pursued the seocnii defendant. who got on the high road. At Wright's request, the man was stopped by a person who was coming along the road and taken to the farm. Mr Wright obtainet defendants' names and address. The senior defendant had a cartridge bag with hina.—Hvidence having bqen given, defendants stated that Dyson, junior, shot at a bird whilst on some waste land, and it dropped in Mr Wright's field. They went into the field to get the bird.—A fine oi 20s and costs was inflicted upon each defendant, and an advozate's fee was allowed.</p>
