      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A HORSE Yon ELEVEN SHILLINGS.—PROSFCUTION FOR ILLTREATMFNT AT WABRINGToN. At the Warrington Pollee Court on Monday before the Mayor and other magistrates, William Green,scotland-road was summoned for travelling a horse whilst in an unfit state on November Stn. James Daley, Scotland-road, was summoned for causing th• animal to be travelled and Thomas Chamberlain for aiding and abetting.—P S Evans stated that on the date In question he was on duty at Bridge Foot, and in consequence of information received, he pro- ceeded to the pin mill in Knutsford-road. In a passage near the mill he saw the horse In a very emaciated condition and alniost failing. It was in a very weak state and Its oyes were closed. There was no one in charge of the animal but after witness had waited about an hour, the defendant Green came upon the scene in company with a man from a knacker's yard. with tle intention of slaughtering the horse. Green said he had travelled the horse from Mr Chamberlain's farm at Grappenhall and it had taken ft an hour and a half to get to the pin mill. HS knew the animal was in a very bad state and when he got to the mill be thought the best thing to do was to have It slaughtered. The horse was taken to the Bridge Inn. a distance of about 300 yards and it took it about a quarter of an hour to walk there. On the way It groaned very heavily and appeared to be in great pain. Witness afterwards saw the defendent Daley and told him what he hod done. Daley said he bought the horse from Mr Chamberlain who told him that the horse was all right and would be able to walk to Warring- ton. Green Bald that Daley told him to take the horse to a knacker's 3ard. On the following afternoon witness and Inspector Print visited the defendant Chamberlain and told him what had occurred. Chamberlain said he sold the horse to Daley for lie, and added " What could he expect for its ? Be is a horse dealer and should know better" Be also stated that he worked the horse on the day before he sold it, and it assisted in drawing 25ewt of tornips out of a field. lie knew it had been ill for several days and iatd refused to drink' P S Barnett gave evidence.—An electric car inspector named Carter sal I he saw the defendant Green beating the horse with a stick In Enutsford-road.—lnspectar Print said he examined the horse' It was a bay gelding, aged, and in an extremely poor condition. It was so weak that it could , scarcely walk and was suffering from a violent chest disease. Daley said he knew the horse was in a bad state Sad would not have travelled it but Chamberlain said it was fit to walk to Warrington or even to Manchester.—Mr Dixon,veterinary surgeon, said he examined the horse after it had been slaughtered. It had suffered from catarrh and inflammation of the right lung.—The defendant Chamberlain said Daley bargained for the horse on the understanding that it was to be slaughtered. Defendant thought it would have been fit to travel to Warrington•—lareen said Chanbertain was not present when the bargain was made.—The ease against Chamberlain was dismissed and the other defendants were ordered to p ty the costs. VIOLE,T ASSAULT ON A WARRINGTON WOMAN. Arthur Caveny, lodging-house keeper, Ship-yard, appeared at the Warrington Police Court en Tuesday to answer to the charge of assaulting Mary Rogerson, wife of William Roger- son, alae a lodging-house keeper in Ship Yard.—Mr Browne appeared for the complainant and stated that about a quarter to ten on Saturday night, Mr and Mrs Regerson,aecompanied by a Miss Maria Smith, who lived with them, went out te do some sbopping. Mr Egerton went on in front, and when Mrs Rogerson and Miss ,Smith passed the prisoner, who was under the Influence of drink, he made use of a foal expression. Complainant asked if he was alluding to her, when he struck her in the face and knocked her dew.. Miss Weak cried out " Auntie Is being murdered," and Mr Rogerson came bark and found Caveney palling his wile's hair and pummelling her face In consequence of his treat- ment her face was bruised and badly cut and her head was also sore. Mr Rogerson got his wife into the house and Cave- ney wita a running kick, broke in the door and kneaked the staple off. Mr Rogerson went for the police, and while be was away Mrs Rogerson foolishly opened the door, when Caveney again hit her on the forehead and knocked her down. The police came on the scene and took Caveney up the yard. Sergeant Barnett in his report stated that Caveney's hands were smeared with blood, and Mrs Rogerson was bleeding from a wound near the right eye and appeared to have been badly assaulted. It was not the first time Caveney had been before the magistrates ter grievous assault, and though some of the witnesses present had been seriously threatened he asked them to take no notice of that as they "Mid have police proteetion.—Oaveney said complainant struck him first. —Superintendent Monaghan said Caveney was fined 20s and costs in MS for assault.—The Bench imposed a line of 42e and costs, or ene menth's imprisonment. They also allowed 21 Is adveaate's fee.—Mr Browne said that to show there was nos ill-feeling towards Caveney complainant would give the Court fees. if paid, to the poor box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>ivarrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Before Mr H Roberts (in the chair), Councillor Smethurst, John Roberts, and Mr Thornton. ALt.tovm VALVE PRETENCES.—Franais James Oliver. of no settled abode, was charged with having on the 22th inst obtained ba from Richard Spann, wheelwrie.ht, Friars' Green. by false pretenees.—Snperintendent Monaghan said that on Wednesday afternoon prisoner went to Mr Spann's premises and represented himself to be a farm bailiff. He said he had come from Lytham Hall, Ormskirk, and Intended to give Mr Spann an order for a float ; and would pay as muca as £5O for it. Mr Spann showed him several boats and eventually prisoner gave an order for one. As they were going along Friars' Green together, prisoner borrowed 5s from Mr Spann on the strength of having bought the float. Subsequently Mr Spann made inquiries arid found prisoner had given a wrong name and address. The man was arrested by P.S. Barnett.—Prisoner was re- mended for a week. DOMESTIC DIFFERENCES. -John Jackson, 3 New Springs, Wigan-road, Aspull, was summoned for persistent cruelty to his wife Ann Jackson, whose address was given as 35 Birchall-street, Warrington.—Mr Browne appeared for the omplainant and defendant was represented by Mr W dr, Shawcross, barrister,Manehester (instructed by Mr J Wilson, solicitor, Wigan).—Mr Browns said defendant was a man of substantial means and resided at New Springs, Aspull. He had been married twenty-five yeSts, and, unfortunately gave :way to drink eleven years ago, and ever since then he had illtreated his wile. She load had to run out of the house a all hours of the night to get out of his way. She had stayed away from him for a week at a time on numerms occasions owing to his treatment and had stayed with her sister-in- law at Warrington. The last time she stayed there was at the end of September. After she had been there a week defendant Came over and began crying and saying how sorry be was for all that had occurred. Ee undertook not to take drink In future end lived with her at Warrington for a week, tie behaved well for six days but on the seventh be went out in the morning and at night returned 'mad drunk." He made Use of most swill threats, telling her that she most go home with in and they wadi,' die together. Complainant believcd he would</p>
        <p>murder her and told him she would not live with him again. —A large number of witnesses were called and It was denied that complainant was of drunken habits—Tile defence was that the parties lived together In comparative happiness until two years ago. Defendant's wife then commenced to go out drinking with neighbonrs and came home under the inflo• enca of drink. Defect tent complained and they got to words ani defendant. when in drink had perhaps used abusive language. He had never treated his wife cruelly, and denied making use of the threat at Warrington.—Evidence was given bearing out this version of the case.— The Henan can- suited in private and on their return into court the Chairman said they did not think persistent cruelty had been proved sufficiently to justify them making a separation order. They however felt that defendant's conduct had been Buell as to justify them in reprimanding him very severely. They hoped he would pay the costs. TROLNICALITY PROCURES DlettleelL.—Mary Rinsella, 4 Halirax•street, brought an action against John Stephens, 13 Legh-street, on behalf of the British Natural Premium Life Association Limited, London, to recover ten guineas due on a policy payable on the death of her son—Mr Steel repre- sented plaintiff and Mr Riley, barrister (instructed by Mr Lootson, of Leigh) appeared for the defendant company— Mr Steel said this matter was before the Court about three weeks ago, and it was stated by Counsel for the defendants that the company was in liquidatiou. In view of that fact it was decided to withdraw the case. It came to his know. ledge that the present defendants the British Natural Pre- mium Life Association,Limited, told taken over the liabilities of the National Guardian assurance Co., and consequeutly. summons was issued against them—Mr Riley asked to be allowed to take a preliminary objection. The summons was issued against John Steevens, on behalf of the British National Premium Life Association, Limited. His objection , was that this insurance company was a limited company and was not registered under the Friendly Societies Act. It was therefore impossible to sue a man on behalf of the company • They could only sue a limited company by serving a process at the registered effioe of the company.— Mr Steel submitted that defendants waived their objection 40 any irregularit.7 by appearing at the Court that day. That was the first Intimation he had received that the company was not registered under the Friendly Societies I Act. It was quite evident that the company wished to avoid liability by teahnisalities.—The magistrates clerk said that as the company was not regissered under the Friendly Societies act, the Court had no jurisdiction • The case would hate to go to to the County Coart.—Mr Steel said the pro- ceedings were no credit to the Company.—Mr Riley said the claim would have been paid long since lilt was a good one. The company had a good defence apart from the teehnical objection Übe policy having lapsed months ago.—Mr Steel said his friend might take It that the matter would Rat rest, The case would be taken in the County Court. DF.FRAUDING THE RAILWAY COMPANY.—SMART PENALTY Patrick Conroy, 16, Milton-street, Leigh, Was summoned to travelling on the London and North-Western Railway without paying his fare on September 6th, and also for giving a false name and address. —Ueteadaat did not appear.—Mr Fenna prosecuted and said the defendant was a plasterer and sub-constractor. Some little time ago he worked in Warrington but was now working and lodging in Leigh. On the date in question he arrived at Bank Quay dtation and on passing out said " Contract" to the ticket collector. There happened to be another aollector in the office who knew the defendant had not got a contract and he told the other collector to stop him. Defeadent was stopped and he then said he had not got the contract with him. Ile was told that he would hive to produaa the contract before he left the station and he then admitted that he had not got one and that he had travelled from Hariestowe without a tieket, He offered to pay the fare but the collector refused to receive it. he was asked for his name and address and gave a false one, and considerable difficulty was experienced in finding out where he lived.—Defendant was fined 20e and costs in the first case and 10s and costs in the second. An advocate's fee was also allowed.</p>
        <p>MONO s.y.—Before the Mayor fin the 'hair), Aldermen Fran- comb, Heaton, kiistohinson, and Sutton, Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs F Monks, Birotiall, Hayward, R T Fairoleugh, Wright and Thornton. ' OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—On the evidence of P.C. Hayes, Alfred Beekett. who made his eighth appearanoe, was fined 10s and costs for making use of obscene language in the Market ace at 7.10 on Saturday night. lISPICIOUS tIONDUCV—Joha Shields, of no settled abode, was charged with frequenting an outbuilding in Buckley- street with intent to commit a felony at three o'clock that morning.—Superintendent Monaghan said he hal known the prisoner for about twenty years • He was an h.,bitual thief and had served five years penal servitude, and been flogged .for felony—Detective lideacock said that at three o'clock that morning be was on special duty in the vicinity of Buckley. street and 4e-street owing to reports having been r,oeivel of robberies in that district. He heard a noise which led him to the conclusion that there was someone at the hack of the cottages in Backiey-street. He proceeded there and found the prisoner in an out-building, Knowing him to be a bad character, he bronght him to the Foie&amp; Station.—P.C. Ciongh corroborated and the prisoner was sent te gaol for a month. VAGRAZICY.—Thomas Johnson, of no fixed abode, was on the evidence of P.U. Yf yes fined he and costs tor seeping In an empty hones in Fercival's Fold.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT. A DOCTOIt'S TESTIMONY. .</head>
        <p>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT. A DOCTOIt'S TESTIMONY. .</p>
