      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Late Mr. Samuel Davenport..</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. Samuel Davenport..</p>
        <p>. - The grave closed over the remains of the late Mr Samuel Davenport, ironmonger and general dealer, of Green-street, and who also fore long number of years was a stallbolder in the Market Place, on Thursday of last week, the interment taking place in the Warrington Cemetery. Mr Davenport who was 48 years of age died with startling suddenness from an apoplectic seizure. The coffin was borne to the grave by eight market stallholders,and many others who do business at the market attended the funeral as a mark of respect, including Mr Place, the market inspector. A large number of wreaths bad been forwarded, including one from the stall- solders and another from " William Hickman and Sons Monday sales." Mr Thomas Dale conducted , the service</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>TORTURE OF SCIATICA.</head>
        <p>THE TORTURE OF SCIATICA.</p>
        <p>Dodd's Kidney Pills cure Sciatica, no matter of how bug standing. They have saved thousands of sufferers from days and nights of dull, aching torture. Mr James Flood, of Rumleigh, Bete Ala 100, H. 5.0., Devonshire, wrote on Mw 20th, 1902, stating that he suffered two years from sciatica in hips and legs. Doctors told him he would never get better, and the meaicine he took only gave temporary relief. Tue pain always came back. It was only after taking Dodd's Kidney Pills that the Sciatica went, and did not come back. "I am quite tree from pain," Mr Flood says; "my cure is permaaent,as I have never had a return of the trouble." Mr Flood's cure is permanent because at last ho tried the direct treatment—the kidneys. His kidneys were failing to take out the uric acid in the blood, and no treatment can set right sciatica unless it drives uric acid from the system. This is what Dodd's Kidney Pills will do,promptly and permanently. They cure every one of the uric acid family of human troubles. Dadd's Kidney Pills are 2s 91 per box, 6 boxes 13s 9d; TI all Chemists, or post free on receipt of price from ohe Dodd's Medicine Co., 23 Farringdon-avenue, Lon- don, E.C. Remember the name D-o-d-d-'s.</p>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A. Society. A meeting of this society was held in the Co operative Hall, San key•street, on Sunday. M Clegg presiding. " Mi es Aliee Bridge sang " Eutrea me not to leve thee " and " Children asleep," with feeling, and was warmly applauded. Mr R Pearce Williams delivered as address on "Workers' present day duties."</p>
        <p>Ebenezer Chapel Band of Hope. Mr 11 Thornton presided over a meeting of this ' band of Hope which Was held on Monday evening. In the course of his speech he expressed his pleasure at the increasing prosperity of the society and the number present at the meeting. Mr Henry Taylor, Police Court missionary, gave a lecture en- titled "British to the Backbone." It was well received, and was illustrated by a magnificent set of slides, kindly lent by Bovril Ltd., and showing scenes in America, India, the Artie regions, South Africa, and also Holbein's Channel swim and Jeffer- son's ride to Kiva. These were followed by a num- of South African war slides, and the photos of Warrington officers and men and views of tha graves of those who had fallen there. A hearty i vote of thanks was accorded to Mr Taylor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY NOVEMBER 21 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY NOVEMBER 21 1902</p>
        <p>LIBERAL MEETING AT STOCKTON HEATH. Speech by Mr omhnson, M.P.</p>
        <p>At the Church Institute, S;ockton Heath, on Satur- day evening, a meeting undiT the auspices of the Kniesford Division Liberal Association was held. Mr A H Crosfield presided and he was supported on the plat- form by Mr James TomliaEon, M.P., for Crewe, Mr A J Ring (president of the Koutsford Association and the prospective candinat6 at the next election), Mr R Win- field (vice-chairman of the executive), Mr J Smith (sec- retary), Mr C E Parker (president of the Warrington Association), Councillor F R Roberts, Couceillor James Evans,Mr W Furness,Mr E Lloyd Jones,Rev J Sflughes, Mr J Mounfield, Mr W Jenkios,Mr J Tinder and others.</p>
        <p>The Cnairmen called upon the secreiary to read several letters of apology for non-attendance from Mr Ziba Armitage, J.P., Councillor Win Pierpoint, Mr C Jackson Holmes, and the Ceairman of Executive (Mr H B Clreel. Councillor Pierpoint wrote as follow- : Marlfield, Latchford, Warrington, November 15, 1902. "Dear MrCrosfield,—l am very sorry that a prior engage mint will prevent my being with you te-oight. I hope that you may have a good meeting and that your efforts to further toe cause of Liberalime in the Bautsford Division will be crowned with Success. Toe Govern- ment by its poliey of doles, abject servility to vested haterests,and unparalleled extravagance, ought to stand condemned in the minds of all who are wishful to further the best interests of the people, and put down that spirit of unthinking militarism which is now so rampant in our country, and to which the majority of the Government is uedoubtedly due.—With kind regards, yours truly, W Pierpoint." The Chairman, on rising to speak, had a very cordial reception. There wasan old understanding, he said, as regarded Chairmen, to the effect that he was not under- stood to oceupy too much of the time at a meeting like that. He was afraid he broke that rule very badly when he took the chair last Monday eveniog at the meeting of the Warriagten Women's Liberal Association —(taugnter)—and it would not do for him to trespass twice in one week. (Laughter-) It gave him great pleasure to meet their friend Mr Tonskineon, and to pie- side over a meeting which was intended to farther the I cause of Liberalism in the Knutsford Division, in which his dear father took a very groat interest for many years. (Applause.) Perhaps it was for that latter reason that he had ventured to accept the invitation which he received from their very energe'ic and inde- fatigable secretary (Mr Smith) to occupy the chair that evening. He was not going to venture tie anticipate what they would hear from Mr Tomkiesoa on the Education Bill. Us did uot profess to be an expert on the intricate question of education, but on the broad facts, which appealed to all of them, whether txperts or not, perhaps he might be allowed to submit one or two considerations They heard many people say that at all eveate the Bill was half the loaf ; and why net accent that ball loaf ? Well, he thought because they had a pretty shrewd notion that it would be rather difficult for them to get the other hilt. (Pear, hear and laughter) If they considered their constitution and the manner in which the Liberal measures were whittled down and emasculated by the House of Lords they might have a good idea how difficult it would ho to carry an amended ' Education Bill after the present one had been rimmed. That it would be any settlement of the education ques- tion he did not believe any Liberal imagined for a moment. The Nonconformists of England were thor- oughly aroused upon the question and they bad every right to be. It was argued that the Bill was some improvement on the present sestem ; out what he objected to was the way in which it was calculated to I entrench sectarianism ; in fact, the authors of toe Bill had put the interests arid the privileges of ecclesiasticism in front of the true , interests of national education. (Hear, hear.) He did not think at this time of the day and at this stage of the world's history they had any right to be ceramic with a second best in the matter of education. (Hear, hear.) They had,on the contrary, a right to demand that the host education should be given to all the children of the country equally in their interests as a commercial nation, recog- nizing the competition—the world-wide competition— they had to face, and also for another reason, because they knew too,to use a very familiar metaphor, educa- tion was the ladder by which the children of thel working classes, the poorer chases, had the chance given to them to climb in the social scale. (Hear, , hear, and applause.) The Bill, it was true, had been I very much altered in Parliament, and the important modifications which had been made there in themselves justified absolutely the amount of time that bad been spent by their Liberal chiefs and repreaentatives in fight- ing the measure in the House. (Applause.) When the measure first came out, at the first blush, he thought Liberals and a good many Liberal Unionists in the country, were fairly staggered at the Government ven- turing to propose a measure of that kind on the top of I their re-actionary policy in the direction of protection. It certainly looked like riding for a fall. (Hear, hear.) The Government had been reactionary in its home policy, extravagant in its foreign police and reckless in diplomacy, aud if it was bent on self destruction he thought all Liberals would be willing to let it have its own way in that respect. (Hear, hear.) INTERESTING SPEECH BY THE PROSPECTIVE CANDIDATE.</p>
        <p>Mr A J KING moved the follewing resolution : "That in the opinion of this meeting tlao contieuance in office of the present Government is in mattes% of foreign, colonial, and domestio policy detrimental to the best I interests of the people of this country and of the Eine pire. That it condemns tho Government Education Bill because (1) it substitutes for the present direct I control, committees not directly elected by nor respoe-, sible to the ratepayers ; (2) because while transferring the cost-of sectarian schools to the ratepayers it leaves the controlling voirea in their management in private hands ; (3) besause in the majority ef the schools of the country it makes the appointineut of teachers, paid wholly from public funds, to depend not on personal character or teaching ability but upon the professions of particular creeds. Further this meeting declares that no such reactionary meager° shogld be passed without a mandate from the eountry, and it calls upon his Majesty's Government to withdraw the Bill or apply to the people." He amid that although he had net had the pleasure •f being in Stockton Heeth before, he had attended many Liberal meetings in various parts of the Enutsford division during last winter and it was very gratifying te find that although perhaps la point of numbers tie Liberals were Oct as strong as they would like te be, such Liberalism as existed was of a sturdy, robust, and uneompromising charaater. Unfortunately daring last winter, end during three long years,there was only one possible or practicable eubjeot of discussion at a pullie political rneetiog,sed that was the terrible war in Soot' Africa. He was not going to say much about the war that night. They were all &amp;aekful it was ever--(hear, hear)—but they must not for that reason con- clude that there was no longer any coca- sion for the people of this country to take an interest in the affairs of that unhappy land. (Hear, hear). He would only remind them in a few weeds ef the extraordinary blunders whicda were made by the Government at the 60M11281116IIISCt of the war, of the statement that 20,000 men and ten millions et money would bring the war to an end by Cerietanas, of the announooment that infantry and not mounted soldiers were required, of the declaration that the Boers would not and could not fight and that they had lest all that spirit of independence which they formerly p d, and of the statement that the Orange Free State won Id not join the Trenevaal in fighting against this country. They all recollected how thous statements were made, and how they all proved to be false. He would only recall to their minds, lest they forgot, a few of the more painful incidents of the eon- duct of the war. He would remind them thac 20,000 brave English soldiers lost their lives either from wounds or disease; of the hospital scandals at Bloem- fontein ; of the farm burning and destruction and devastation of the whole country ; of the committee:ion eanepe which cost the lives oi seine 13,000 women and children ; of the futile proclamations ; of what Mr John Morley, he thought, rightly called the infernal atrocity of shooting men like Letters and Seheepers in the enforced presence of their friends ; of the hysterical shrieks of unconditional surrender and no shred of independence. The only comfort they could have in recollecting these incidents. was that it was some satisfaction to think that through those weary years there was to be found in this couutry a small band of resolute men, Liberals who protested and effectually protested in most of those eases, against what was being done. (Applause.) Even the" uncon- ditional surrender " ciy had to be given up, and in the erml p aCe W-44 m ide on tArms 'cis toe acr Itmdov; io</p>
        <p>the field, largely, he belieeed, through thristatesmaaship of Lord Kitchener. (Applatue.) Wnat was the state of things at the present time ? Lerd Milner said twelve months agothat practically me wilole of tar agrieul- tural property of the country hal been des- troyed. Oily a few weeks age a report was published of his recent tour through the richest part:of the Transvaal, and he stated again that in that reglia the farmers had last praatically everything except their lama. He welt on to state that fortunately the land had somewhat improved to value. Teat was the condition of the country from the paint of view of the means of eubsisteaae. If they looked at the country ' politically, they could not forget that throughout Sow' Afriaa, even ircluding Cape Coloay and Netal,aa regard- ed the white pepelatioa the Dutch were, and were likely to remain, ire a vastly preponderating proportion. It seemed to him that the cieedition of tniugs would tax to the utmost the resources of the Governmeut and woald need the moat careful watching of the people of this country as to the nature of legislation and administra- tion in south Africa • He was glad to see that the Colonial &amp;watery Was going out there to see for himself what was the real state of affairs. (Hear, hear.) Hs was sure they all 'tithed Mr Chamberlain "God-speed." they wisbed him a fair aya and a cool head that he would be able to briug his undoubted abilities to bear to do something to reconcile the many and diverse interests which existed in Soute Africa, and to bring balm and healing to the wound of that country with thas thought ha had done so mush to Imam (Applause.) Mr. King weal: oa to speak of the geueral pence/ of the Government, and pointed out that since it came into offime 150 millions had been added to the national debt. Referring to tho corn tat, he said that in plain English it simply meant a revereal of our time-honoured free trade policy, won for us by the efforts of our ancestors. and the tax idea of the bare necessities of life of the very potrest of the people. The fact was that the ener- gies of the Liberal party at the present due required to be directed, not so much to the promulgating and the earrying out of Gloss many and numerous social and domestic reforms whiale they all kaaw were so urgent, as to endeavour to stem the tide of reaction he every phase ,of our public life. Every privilege they had been acmes- ' tomed to regard as established, every liberty they had hitherto hold deer had been attacked and threatened by the present reactionary Government. (Applause.) Alluding to the Irish qaestiou Mr King said they were tole roma years ago that all that Ireland wanted was twenty yeere of resolute government combined with I something which was described as " killieg Home Rule by kindness." It was now about sixteen years since 'Mr Gladstone' first Hanle Rule Bill was thrown out, and these proceedings tad hail a pretty fair trial. The result Wee that there was at the nrcosait time an agrariaa revolution in Ireland, almost unpreeedentel eveu in ths history of Ireland. The Coeroiea Lot had been applied to whole districts including some which were declared to be remarkably free from ordinary crime. Elected re- presentatives of the Irish ha the House of Commons were being incarcerated in prison with the addition of a sea- tow:to of hard labour, and tine deprived them of the privelege of serving on a local authority in Ireland for a number of years. Yet, when the 'rise mere/writ milted I Mr Balfour to be allowed for one evening to discuss the state of things in Ireland he denied them that privilege. He (tee speaker) was a con- vinced Hones Ruler even before Mr Gledstone took rip the questioo. He moved a reeolutioa its a de- bating society in London to the effect that no solution if the Irish question would be permaaently m etisfaiotery which did not embody the principle of Home Rule. Ho had seen no reason to alter that opinion. He wait con- fess that he had always seen one very great diffioulty in granting Home Rule te Ireland elem. Tart difficulty was intensified by the fact that even a man of Mr Glad- stone's commanding genius was unable to devise any satisfactory working scheme by whale ualer those cir- cumstances the representation of tin Irish in the laiper- la! Parliament could be carried 'O. It seemed to nixie (the apeaker) that when the Irish question erne before them again it would come befors meat in smother form whereby that difficulty would be reoalved. It was aboolutely impossible under the preseet circunattanoes for Parliament to do its work. It woe entirely over- weiehted, the reason being that at the present time it was doing some work two or three times over. Re .be- lieved he was right in saying that soneethieg like 75 per cent, of the Bills brought before the House of Common were Bills dealing with only ore part of the United Kingdom. For instance the Eameation Bill dealt only with edueation in flogland and Wales. In seemed to him that if by seine process of devolution they could create legislative bodies in Eaglandafreland, Walea, and Scdtbsnd to deal with affairs peculiarly effeatiog the interests of each country and leave matters of Imperial Interest to Imperial Parliament, it would not only solve the Irish problem but very mach increase the efficiency ef our imperial Government. With regard to the Ede- eation question the present was tee third Bill the Government had brought in, and the two former ones were received with scant reapers% This third effort was a much more serious matter, and as they were aware, it had roused the feelings of the country in a way which nethina of recent data had ecatalled. How Was it there was all this turmoil? What was all the clamour about? New was it that they had the edifying opectacle of Christian ministers of verieue deanmiaatioas engagiog in wordy warfare in anything but a Christian spirit ? le did not wish to eay anything disrespectful about any denomination, still less the Established Cauroh aad as they wore meeting that night in a Church room he would like to thank the Vicar for his courtesy. It had been stated that before 1870 ao one took any care of the ecluoation of the people except the Ghana' of Eneland. On referring to history they found that in 1807 the education of tbe country was in a terrible con- dition, The only, schools whist' existed ware private schools, and no respectable or decent mar would teach in them. A Bill Was introduoed and passed in the aOl.llll of gammons in 1807 autherieing the establishment of a scatted under public oontrol in every village in the country. That Bill was thrown out of the House of Lords at the instigation of the Bishops because they said it would infringe the establiahed right if the &amp;unroll to control the education of the people. The frinads of education were not die eouraged by the failure to get the Bill passed, and determined at the instigation of Ong John Laneester,a Quaker—(applauee)--.Wila had already made a name in educational matters, to form a volun- tary aesieciation, wbica was first of all known as the Lancastrian Association and afterwards by the name of the British and Foreign Sollool Society. For several years this association, which wag entirely underioneinational, with the help of the Chinch party' did a great work in building cahoots and training teachers. But a certain extreme scales of the Chureh party said this work was undermining their exclusive privileges as regarded aduoltion. Tao alarm they raised was successful, and Ohurclopeople formed a society commonly °ailed the National Society. It was very interesting to and that the real name of the society, which they did not often hear. was the " National Society for the Klee tion of the children of the poor in the principles of the Established Church." The history of education between that time and 1870 was the history of a struggle bet wean these two parties —the ecclesiastical party who considered that the educe- tie, of the young was the exalueive privilege of the Church, and that the basis of education most be the national religion based, as they said, upon, " our most excellent Ceteohistn,* and on the other hand the party who believed that a national system of education must conoern Roma with secular matters, and with the funda- mental principids of Chriatiauity common to ' all nations. Did the general bray of the sloe- tare want the Education Bill P Be thought that bad been answered at Bury, deven Oaks, East Tasteth, and Leeds. Be pointed out that the electors y,ero reit- ponsible for the return of the Government which had introduced the Bill but the last election was fought on the question of the war. Ii the Keutsford division they had made up their minds not to be in the humiliat- ing position they had been in at the last two election+. They had nothing to say against Mr Egertsu, who Wee a most excellent into and it distinguished creamed t of society, but be voted on the wrong aide, and they were determined at the next election that he would at any rate hero to fight for his teat. (Applause.)</p>
