        <head>TUE CHURCII AND EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>TUE CHURCII AND EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>_ Mr Jenkins seconded the sesolation. He said there was no getting away from the fact that they were ii a Liberal meeting. It might be something nnvel in that part of the Parliamenttry division of Icsatsford, but he thought for all teat it wag perfectly right sai proper, and he believed that if th,y had such meting more frequently it would be for the vol of the CAUIa and for the welfare of the public at large. (fear, hear.) Thay bad beea rather mired of late in that locality ; one bad hardly known who was who or what was what (laughter)—but he trusted that that state of affairs bad come to an end, that they had entered upon a new and better era, and the.t the old and familiar motto of Li bera!s " retreneneue,o 4;0 rtiform "j would 001</p>
        <p>their motto. (Applause). He thought from past experience, that they need not expect much attention to these neetters from the present Adminis- tration, and the 111993 the Governmene had already landed them into was Calp4ble in the extreme. Daring the past eight or nine years they had tied Little else but class legislation. This party and that party had been ' pandered to witheut rhyme or reason, and he feared that the higher interests of the nation had been sadly neglected in the inceseant desire to meet the wishes of party supporters. What was the Education Bill but a I subsidy to the Established Cnurch—(hear. hear)—and a response to toe cry of Convocatioa ? Did the nation ask for such a Bill, and had the present Government any mandate for Enaoh a thing ? (" No.") He noeld I only arrive at the conclusion that the Government's !meaning was the supremacy of the Courele and the overthriew of everything that might stand injher way. He had no animus against the Church, but he would not make a pet of her. He would accord her the powers and privileges which he would give any end every other religious community, and then let her live by her merits. (Applause.) If the Established Church wiehed to monopolize the educatien of the nation and :iteculeate her doctrines and dogmas, then let her pay for it. (Applause.) But hands off the pub- lic purse, if they pleaeed ! What was the Cauroh doing previous to 18T0 ? Was not failure written indelibly on her efforts ? He believed education had made treat and rapid strides since the passing of the ,eict in 1870. Board schools had deservedly flourished. Their Free Churches given feir play—and they asked for no favour —would hold their own anywhere and in any sphere, and he was sure the success which had attended their voluntary effort spoke loudle and in no uncertain tae to the nation, and he treered the day Was soon corning when the voice of the nation mewld be heard and when a righteous and coosidiirav3 Parliament would libarate the Church from the thraldom whiten bound her to the State and would hose her and let her go. (Loud applause.) TOMIKINSON SPEA.Ei ON Tim EDUCATION BILL'</p>
        <p>Mr James TenVeinson M.P.,supp ortel the resolution. In a vigorous speech on the Eiucation Bill,he remarked that the Lerd'Cbaneellor had said that the closuring of the Education Bill by eempartments and passing clause after clause without discussion Was absolutely necessary to save the House of Commons from degenerating into a mere talking machine. He might reply that it was better to be a mere talking machine than a mere walking machine. (Laughter.) Al- , though it went against the grain to divide upon every clause, proposal, and amendment, irrespee- tiva of their exact nature, nevertheless they felt bound to mike their protest against the system of closeting, I without discussion, and adding to the Zaw of the land a number of clauses which, to judge by the experienee of the clauses that had gone before,were everyone of them capable of large amendment. (Hear, hear). He was glad to hear from their excellent President, that they fully intended to fight theHnutsford Division at the next election. There was se division that ought not to be fought. Die division could be hopeless now after the experiences of Seven Oaks, where a 4,000 reduetion took place in the Conservative maj irity ; Cleveland, North Leeds and East fexteth. All those, elections showed a wenderful swine of the pendulum and nbenee in public opinion. They wanted a large majority to out the Liberal party really in power, and make it %tile to deal not only with legislation in the House of Commons, but ;lo with the House of Lords. Speaking in the House on Tuesday night he rather chaffed the Government. He said their reason for fight- ing the closure to the last was that they net only con- tended that the Bill was never submitted to the cenntry before—a proposal was never made, and there was no mandate for it—but they contended the country was absolutely against the Government. He asked could anyone look at the list of birthday honours and net be , struck with the fact that there Was net a single peerage given. (Laughter). Were they to believe that among I all the faithful followers and distineuished members I who sat on the Government side of the [loose there was I no one either fit to receive or willing to receive a peer- age ? He said they dared not make a peerage. They 1 had lost seats before, and they had nearly lest Seven Oaks, and he challeegei them to mikia peer in any constituency. (Leughter). He also quoted a message of Mr Chemherlain's te the elec- tors of Dewsbury io support ref his cententioe that the Government had no mandate for rite Education I Bill. In that message Nfr Chamberlain pointed out that patriotism came before partizanshio, and that it was not a contest between Liberals and Conservatives, but between those whe were for their cnuntrydani those who were feethe Beers.tie(Mr Tomkinson) was preelucled from diseessine the good taste of that message. (Laughter.) Whilst he (Mr Temkinson) and his friends had sees fight- ing in the betties, Mr Chamberlain's family were gain- ing meney by their enaneetion with aompenies manufacturing munitions of war. (fi, as, hear.) , ' Proceeding to refer to the Education Bill, Mr Theikinsei said he wee a Liberal first and a Churchman afterwards. 1 Hie Church principles were en broad that they embraeed all Christian denominations.lie maintained entirely him- self a school which his father built and which he doubled in size, Aril therefore ho Rundle' I from a pecuniary veldt of view this Eineaties Bill would put something in his picket, but nevertheless he Wan a strong opponent of it. (Hear, hear.) NI one could listen to tie debates in the 1101183 OF (letrrnons witheat coming to the rienelo- sion that the Goverment had undertaken a most difficult and thorny task which they did not fully understand. They had get into a great noses over it, they had no popular mandate behind them, and there was no doubt ,isat the Bill had been na.lertaken at the billing of the Established Church and the Bishops. When they under- took a diffizult task they did not understend thee were not likely to make a suneess of it. There were few members on the Government side of the Helves who really uaierstooe and not enemy who took a great inter- est in edmaation. They kstow there was a party in the Reuse, represented by the Bight Hon James Lowther, who said Board Schools were expensive nuisances—(laughter)--and there were not a few who thought with him. Mr Balfour WI a benevolent sort of neutrality towards adulation, and he had picked up his Bill—as counsel sometime did with a brief—AS lee went along and knew more about it now than he did at first. (Lenghter.) Sir William Hart Dyke was an earnest educationaliet, Sir John Gorst was a very good man and had been doing better below the gangway than above it, hut when they compared them with Dr ilifacnamtre,t4r Lloyd George, Mir Whitley (a Member of the School Board of Halifax) Mr Hutton, Mr Corns Grant and others, there was no comparisin between the knowledge, experience and enthusiasm for education of those gentlemen sitting on the Opposition side with similar qualities, or abienoe of qualities of gentlemen upon 'the Goverment side. (Applause.) W hat did this Bill do /I Without any mandate what. ever the Bill abolished the SotOol Boards, although they hail done a most admirable work arid had been respon- sible for the education of millions of children during the past 30 years, and handed over their powers to a distant body not elected for the purpose, and in many eases unwilling to undertake the duties, even if they were competent to do so.—Mr Toaskineen pro- ceeded to show that in many cases it would be impos- sible for workingmen to sit on tire County Council as they had done on local School Boards, owing to the dis- lance ref the county town from their home. The Governmeet oven refused to *ceept an amendment I allowing expenses to the Conoty Council meetings. The ' Bill proposal for the first time C plaoe upon the rates to some extent the Church of England denominational ' Scheele. Mr Tomkinson traced the history of the atti- tude of the Church of England to yards national educe- tien during the past century, and showed how the Government grants to the voluntary schools bad been increased from one-third of the total I expenditure to almost the whole of it. Ten- pence out of every shilling spent by the denomi- national schools was provided rout of the public purse. and this twopence had now become an " intolerable strain." (Laughter.) Under the Bill the public found the money end another set spent it. It was a total divorce of taxation from representation. But there was an even stronger objection to this Bill than that. Tilers were 3,033 villages in which the Anglican soilel was the only eettoel to which children could go, and yet the Bill gave to the trustees four managers out of the six with ills express object of keeping up the Ohurch of England "atmosphere," and of course estorating the retinal with it. If the parents of the children by a large majority were so anxious that their children should receive the 810(4%1 instruction of the Church of England why need the C lurch fiesr to accept two oat of the, six and sehrnit the eleotien of the other foar managers to people ? (Applause.) He thought the clergy of tire the Church of England were making a great inietake in not showing their trust in the people. Hs lid not think they wild eerhiu! in</p>
        <p>fear whatever. (Hear' hear.) With regard to the teachers he thought that was the worst part of the whole Bill. They were goin4 to establish a great public service—as much a public service as the army, navy, or civil service—in anyone of which the mere idea of a re- ligious test would be scouted by everybody. By the Bill they were going to iesoeute and attach to that sec- vice a religious test. Tiley knew perfectly well that if the object was to proselytise, to saturate the atmosphere of the school, with the principles and teaching of the Esti blished Caurch both the head teacher and the secend teacher would have to be of the Auglican com- munion. In other words they excludel thousands and thousands from the only learuF3l profession which was open to them. Could anything be mere unjett thin that? If it realls were a question of either doing away with denotninatioaal teaceing as a acate matter or passing the Bill in its present form he should say derminiaa- ' tional teaching must go. tie wee alining to the oori- j clusioa that the less the State had to do with religious teaching in the schools the better. It might very well hire all these so-called Voluntary schools fer six hours a day, five and a half days in the week, for tea menthe and a half in the year, and let the religieue training be given perfectly voluntarily half an hour after opening the school ennui an hour before it closed (Applause.) The present coascience clause did not act.lt csst a stigma upon many of the poor children, and the fever they were the more they felt it. ("Hesr,irear,"and applause.) He thought they had ovary reason for opposing tee Bill. It isolated almost every principle teat a Liberal held dear—the principle of equality, especially religious equality ; the absence of religious tests ; the abiolute freedom of all the public services to every deserving citizen apart from all religious formulas. (Haar, hear.) The Bill would be a bribe to a vast number of people to become professedly members of one particular sect in order to ethjoy its emoluments. It was a great fresh endowment of the Eetablished Cherole ; it abolished school boards without a mandate ; and it violated the principle of taxation and representation going together. He thought with all these reasons they would be un- worthy to call themselves Liberals if they did not offer to it an uncompromising opposition, and if passed, not to rest until its most objectionable features had been repealed. (Applause.) The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried, there being only four dissentients. THE FORTHCOMING FIGHT. SPEECH BY MR. C. E. PARKER. TO PREPARE FOR A GOOD FIGHT.</p>
        <p>Mr Winfield movecl—"That this nueetiag pledga3 itself to use every legitimate MUCH that lies in its prAver to prepare far a good fight in the Knutsford Parliamentary Division at the nest election, and urges all Liberals to unite in an active campaign against the reactionary financial and legislative schemes of the present Govern- ment." (Applause.) Mr Mounfield seconded the motion, and said he thought the time had come when every constitueney should be cinteeted.</p>
        <p>MrO E Parker, in supoortini.aid there had bean such a distinct reversion to Toryism in the crovernment during ths last year or two tat it was high time the Liberal party was pulling itself Nether and getting ready tor a fight.</p>
        <p>There was no doubt that the Liberal ITaionists during the earlier part of the present Government undoubtedly influenced thc Government in the right direction, but there had been going on during late years a sort of ab- sorption of tho better elements of the Liberal tfaionists, and sernehew they veine i to have lost th de power and influence a good deal, and dialer the pant year at least the measures that had been passed and things that had been done brought them back to some of the worst feat- ures of the Tory legislation for many years. (Hear, hear). His interest in education and the bringing in of the Mutilation Bill had really been the thing which had induced him te take perhaps a little more active part in political matters then he had been doing for a number of years. He certainly should not have occupied the official position which he now held, had it not been that the principles which underlay the Education Bill were re- pugnant to him sae a true Liberal. There was no doubt the members of the ENIS3 of Commons, Conservatives. Liberals and Unionists, had boson doing what they could to make the Bill a reasonable measure, and he thought they had done a good many things which had very much improved the measure, bat the foundation was wrong and they!could not build a proper edifice upon it As long as they had compulsory and State-paid educa- tion there could never be a concordant and proper writing system when they tried to fit into it an entirely di#erent system which wee based upon an entirely different idea. The two things would never work to- gether well. For this and other reasons, it gave him pleasure to see any awakening of Liberal sentiment and Liberal ideas in connection with that division, and he treated they weld study these questions and do what thee eould to %oilier° their ambition in the direction of a Liberal member to represent theta. kilear, hear.) Some pre* had bs.-in cruel enough to say the Education Bill was not au E Incation Bill at all. If it was riot edu- (rational as far as the children were concerned, it was I certainly edunational as far as the adults were con- cerned, and some of them knew a very great deal more about the edanation question now than they did a Few Months ago. For his own part, he perhaps had had grecial advantairea in the way of studying the voluntary system during the last twenty years, because he bad oecupied the position of manager in two or three ae.hools. He had seem that although the managers bad tried to do their best for education in connection with those schools, they bad not been able to enlist the en- thusiasm of the piseole in the edueation of their children. It was quite impossible to enlist the eathasialm and co- operation of the neople in the education of their children if they shut them out from all management in the echeole. They must stand firm on the bed rock that if the people paid, the people should manage the schools. (Applause). The Chairman remarked that he ventured to say that no one in his part of the country .107fle better qualified to eneak with authority on the educational question than Mr Parker. After referring to Mr Parker's letter to the press on the subject, he said that Liberals had net only to argue out Uwe questions, but when they bad made tin their minds, they should be prepared as good British citizens to not their shoulders to the wheel and help to move the Parliamentary machine a little bit forward. Reference had been made to some of the remarkable instances in which there had been the heavy swing of the pendulum in other constituencies, and that should encourage those who were concerned in bring that division to a better way of thinking. He thought they must all appreciate the pluck of the eandidate who was prepared to tackle the division. The fi.ht would be an uphill one,but the harder the fight the more honour there would be in winning it. He hoped those of them who lived in the constituency would do their best when the fight came, so that the Liberals could give a really good account of themselves (A1,1)1%125(9. The resolution was carried unanimously. VOTE OF 'MINKS TO MR. TOMKINSON.</p>
        <p>Mr Tinaler moved :—" That this meeting desires to thank Mr Tornkiason for his presence and able address, and desires to place on record its keen apprecia,tioa of his earnest and persistent work for the principles of Liberalism, and congratulates him on his victory at the last election in a neighbouring constituency. (Applause.) He felt that the Goyernment could have done no better service to the Liberal party than to have introduced the Elucation Bill. The candidate in that division had a barl and acriooi task before him, and he (the speake(s thought that ho better weapon could havebeen placed in his hands than the Education Bill which had been forced on the country, and was a good illustration of the 11 tab- version of Liberal principles when the other side got into power. He had been intensely interested in the speech of Mr Tonokinson, especially as it came from one who acknowledged himself to be a Churchman. If a man of his temparament hated the Bill how much more should a horrid heretic and Dissenter hate it ? (Laugh- ter.) It was not the office of the State to teach religion at all, and be protested against the idea of admitting ministers of any denominatioe into national schools.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Lloyd Jones seconded, and in speaking of the religions education in Board schools, said that the School Board managers of Liverpool recently offered twenty prizes for the best essays on religious questions. Nine- teen of these prizes went to children attending Board Sohools, and only one to a scholar at a yoluutary school. (Applause). The Chairman xpressed the hooe that the facts so lucidly, graphically and psrtinently driven home by Mr Tonskinson would have their weight with the electorate in that constituency and in the neighbouring constitu- ency of Warrington. The motion was heartily agreed to. In responding Mr Totakinson said he was delighted to find that the Liberals were awake in that division, and that there was a gentlem a reedy to step info the arena</p>
        <p>as the Liberal candidate. Referring to the corn tax he said it made all the difference between having free trade and not having free trade. It was the opening of the door. These was a party in the house and in the coun- try which was avowedly in favour of protection, which meant having things scarce and dear instead of plentiful and ahem). Be mould watch the contest in that division wite great interest. THE CHAIRMAN THANKED. SPEECH BY COUNCILLOR F. R. ROBERTS. Mr Tonskinsou proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman. Councillor F R Roberts seconded. He said he had the pleasure of Mr Crosheld's election agent whea he fought at Warrington eighteen months agoond oeuld testify to • the splendid zest and vigour witn which Mr Crostield fought that apperently and indeed hopeless campaign. He thought he might say that Mr Crosfield had been for VVarriogton what lair Tonakinson had been for Cheshire—the leader of many oomparitively ferlorn hopes, yet full of spirit, hope, zeal and earnestness, and he (tee speaker) booed, a winner in the long run. If they considered the progress which had marked thought upon the education question, and if they bore in mind how different the attitude of people WAS now from what it was a few months ago,it was not by any means hopMes3 problem that they rnight win Knutsford division. A few months the Chairman of the Museum Committee of the Warriaeton Town Council insisted Upon a resolution being passed in favour of the Educa- tion B,D,that resolution was passed on to the Council for confiroaation and everybody seemed astonished when he (Councillor Roberts) got up and moved an amendment. He got no seconder, and ho was th 3 only person to vote against the Bill. So overcome was tha Town Clark at this fact that he onaitte Ito put in his printed copy of the minutes of the Council meeting, that anyone bad voted against the Bill and be stated there that the reso- lution was carried unanimously. (He) Councillor Roberts) would undertake to say that it the Warrington Town Council were asked to vote upon such a resolution as that to-day, it was very doubtful indeed whether It would be carried. Certainly he would not have the honour of being the solitary opponent of it. There was no such thing as a hopeless fight especially at the pre- sent time. With snob a.candidate as Mr King, who had had proved himself to be an admirable and lucid speaker, he theught it was by no means hopeless that if not at the next election, at a very early Mention at all events, they might have the honour of being represented by him He (Couucillor Roberts) was sorry to say he had never given a winning vote in his life. He had voted, be thought,four times at Warrington and had never had the pleasure of getting his candidate in, but etiil ha did not think it had affected him physinally at all events, and if they kept up their peckers he thought they might rely upon people seeing through the unfairness and son- righteousness of that abominable Bill. There was not a single good point in it, except perhaps the co-ordina- tion of authorities and teaching. That was the only good principle there was in it. (Applause).</p>
        <p>- This vote was cordially agreed o and 'the Chairman briefly replied. A QUEiTION RI sr. JAMES'S SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>member of the audience asked Mr Tomkinsen if there was anything in the Bill to compel Nonconiormist children to walk two by two to the Church. This was clone in connection with St. James's school. Whether the children were Nonconformists or not they had to go to the Church and learn things that Nouconfoi-mists did not want their children to be taught. Mr. Tomkinson said that if his interrogator would write him a letter stating these facts to the House of Commons, he would put %he goestione in the Howie to the Secretary of State, whe would write to Warrington, ace if be fonnd the facts to be as alleged, the Education Department would step in and say the children had no right to be taken in that manner. The mee tin then concluded.</p>
        <p>The Rev. F. F. Tay 13r, Saldloharn via Manchester, writes :—"I like Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Coona very much, and have used it every day. t baliev2 it is all you represen it to be. It is fir away t, be preferred to tea, coffee and other cocoas." The proprietors of Vi-Cocoa are still sending out thousands of dainty sample tins of VI-Cocoa, daily, from 60, Bluth ill Row, London, B.C. and we should,on the strength of this letter alone, advise our readers to drop a postcard to their address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above authority was held on Tuesday at Dutton. Present : fdessrs J Knowles in the elate) A. It. Norossci (vice-ohairmao) T Lea, T (Wood,J Douglas,the mei:shore of the R.D.C. withiblesre F Ashton (clerk) W E Hough (assistant clerk), J Cunnah (master), Messrs I Cooke, Riley and DePrez (relieving other).</p>
        <p>A QUESTION OF ARMY PENSIONS. communioatioa vase removed tram tise Clerk to the Swansea Union askieg the Bonrcl to palg e ra,olujoa sitniiar to the following :—" 'Nat the Clerk be instruct_ ed to commucicate with the becretary of State for W“ urging upon tuna the importance of Arranging,l in cue interests of the pensioners, for the payment of any pensions weekly instead of quarterly." It was resolved Co support the resolution on the pro- position of Mr Pilling.</p>
        <p>THE FIRE ESCAPE. Mr Wood proposed tuts the master receive 5 per cent. for the superintendance of the erection of the fire escapes. Mr Douglas seconded. Mr Knight moved that the master receive £5 ss. Mr Lea seconded,and the amendment was carried. The financial sZatetnent. snowed the cut-payments to be as follows :--ituccorn, £35 ; FrJdeaam, £25 ; B ud worth, £3l. Mr Wright proposed and Mr Chadwick seconded the report, which was adopted.</p>
        <p>THS STATE OF THE HOUSE. It was stated by the Master that there were 19 fewer inmates of the house than last year at this time. Mr Linaker : That seems to bo the condition of things in more unions than one. In Chester there are 39 less. The Chairman : Considering the cold wi ather that is very encouraging. The Master: The number ot old men is same as usual but the females and the children number less.</p>
        <p>CIIIESTION NOR ME RUNCORN DISTRICT C3UNCIL. Mr Linaker said that he intended to move at the District Council meeting that the meeting be held once a month instead of 01:1C0 a fortnight. As the Guardians were involved in the matter be suggested that the Chairman and vice-chairman shculd be put on the coin- mitts he would move to consider the matter.</p>
