      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PROPOSED NEW THEATRE.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Plans have been prepared, and will be submitted to the Corporation for approval at an early date,of the proposed Grand New Theatre, which is to be erected on the site immediately behind the Feathers Hotel and contiguous property. The estimatel cost of the new theatre is £25,000. There will be an entrance to the theatre from Bridge-street along an arcade which it is proposed to construct, with small shops on either side, on the site lying between the Feathers Hotel and Messrs Weaver's shop. The entrance to the pit will be from Friars Green and access to the gallery will be gained by an entrance from the new street which has been named Barbauld-street. The capital of the company will be £30,000 and this sum has already been subscribed so that there will be no appeal to the public for share capital. The Chairman of the company is Mr Frank Curz3n manager of the Prince or Wales', Avenue, Strand and Criterion theatres, Loncon, and associated with him is Mr Frederick Mouillot, managing director of the Theatre Royal, Dublin,and the Grand Theatre, Swansea. Mr Louis Calvert. actor and manager, of Slough, will undertake the management. The Lon- don solicitors for the company are Messrs Winter and Rowbotbam, and Mr F R Roberts is the War- rington solicitor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>BAZAAR AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>BAZAAR AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Some Historical Associations.</p>
        <p>A bazaar in aid of Providence Chapel building fund I was opened in the schoolroom on Thursday atterncon 1 by Mr ft A Naylor. Mr A. Mouefield presided and having referred to Mr I Naylor' willingness to aesist in the furtherance of all good objects, said there was one association of the building in which they were assembled, which he was sure would be of interest co them all, and particularly to We Naylor. because he knew there was no cause in which Mr Naylor took a deeper interest than the cause of temperence. In that building the first total abstin- ence society which was formed in this country was founded, tram commencing the cause which had achieved such glorious success. It was in that building that the first total abstinence pledge in connection with any total abstinence society was taken. He expressed the hope that all present wopld assist in continuing the good work in connection with the chapel by making the bszsar a succese. (Applause) Mr It A Naylor referred to the historical facts men- tioned by Mr Mounfield and said that in his opinion there was no Cause more closely associated with Chris- tianit- then the cause of temperance. No matter what they did fr Christianity, strong drink reared its head and stood in the way of the accomplishment ef every good object. Be thought it was a curse and a disgrace not only to our civilization' but to our Christianity, to think that tbe people of England were squandering about 160 millions of money every year upon drink ; and doing that whioh was referred to by the prophet when he said " Wnerefore do ye spend money for that Which is not bread and labour for that which satisfieth not ?" Mr Naylor urged his hearers not to be dis- couraged by small failures but to continue their work in the cause of temneraace and concluded by express- ing the pleasure which it afforded him to declare the bazaar open. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr John Derbyshire seconded by Mr Whitley, Mr Naylor was heartily thanked for his presence. Thursday's proceeds amounted to about £5O. The bazaar was re-opened last evening, but there was no formal ceremony. The following are the names of stall-holders :—Work stalls : Mesdames G Shaw, Grounds, T Houghton, W Shaw, Hardy, Sutton, Pierce, McDermit, and Brayshaw. Refreshment stall : Mesdames J Houghton and Benton. Flower stall : Misses Kenworthy and Mounfield. Entertainments were given at intervals, songs being contributed by Mr E Sumner. and recitations by Miss Fairbrether. Soloe, duets, quartettes, Icc., were ren- dered by tnembere of the choir. Mr Williams gave gra m aphone selections.</p>
        <p>Concert in Aid of the N S.P.C.C. Avery successful concert was held in the Conser- vative Club Assembly Room on Thursday evening in aid of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to children. There was a large audience and the various items of a capital programme were listened to with the greatest pleasure. The vocalists were Miss Mabel Henshall, Miss N Anderton and Mr L Aukland. Miss Henshall possesses a veil sweet voice and her first contribution "The rose,', was well received. She was heard to greater advantage in "0 peaceful England " which was given with marked expression and elicited a hearty encore. Miss Andetton's singing delighted the audience and she was recalled for a feeling interpretation of -bye." giving in response " The sweetest flower that blows" She had the honour of a second encore for a creditable rendition of" The valley by the sea." Mr A.ukland's powerful bass eoice had ample scope in " Toe young royalist "and s The Yeocaan i's Wedding," and he was cordially encored for the last-named song. In response he sang "Rocked in the cradle of the deep." Mrs W B Roberts, whose abilities as a violinist are well known, contributed two solos in admirable style. Miss Gertrude Henshall also gave a violin 'solo with taste and skill. Miss Agatha Jones clever- y danced a hoz npipe which so pleased the audience that a repetition was demanded. Later on she gave a graceful" fan dance" with equal success. Mies Madge Davies's humorous recitation " How I Married Aunt Annie" was one of the most enjoy- able items of the evening and a hearty encore was accorded- Mr H Mosley gave several humorous 0 ngs and was eminently successful in his efforts so amuse the audience. The "living marionettes," ty Mr Frank R Bickford were a decidedly novel ad entertaining feature. Mr T D Amos was Use ampulla.</p>
        <p>Good.</p>
