        <p>„ POTATOES AT £1,120 A TON.—Potatoes do not usually occur to tne specnlator as a litfttlig of operation, but the history of the variety known as "The Northern Star ” is one of the romances of finance. It was brought out last year by a grower at Markincla in Fifeshire, who parted with a few tons at the extraordinary price of £1,120 a ton. To this rate, which works out at about ten -billings a pound, he religiousy adhered. At this rate two pounds were purchased by a firm of dealers belonging to Bardne,, near Lincoln. From these two pounds they have this year grown 1301 kt., and are so pleased with the result that they have bought a quantity more at the rate of £5OO a ton. Two other growers, Mr Kime of Manbamle-uen and Mr Blades of Epworth, purchased a ton at the tame figure. and have paid more for subseluent consignments. After this the story of the King Edward VII. potaton, which began to sell at £l2 104 a ton, and rose to £4O. seems quite tame, though it would have been sufficiently remarkable under any other circumstances. Who would have thought such potentialities of fortune making lay COTICP•II7rI in the homely ratkv P—Cortntry Life.</p>
      </div>
