      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Smart Fine at Warrington.</head>
        <p>Smart Fine at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Before Mr Henry Roberts and other magi striate at the Warrington Felice Court yesterday, Thomas Parkinson. 442, Knutsford-road, was summoned for having on November 9th used water supplied by the Corporatkm for other purposes than those agreed upon. Mr Steel represented the defendant and said that in compliance with an arrangement with Mr Stanley Wilson, deputy-Town Clerk, who prosecuted, he would plead guilty, subject to making an explanation.—Mr Wiison geld the defendant wascharged with two offences but in consequence of an arrangement be had arrived at with Mr Steel he (Mr Wilson) had decided to withdraw one of the charges. Although the charge against the defendant might appear on, the face of it somewhat trivial it was really a very serious charge indeed, for it amounted to one of larceny, and be (Mr Wilson) sub- mitted that it was rendered all the more serious when brought against a person in defendant's position. Defendant resided at 442, Knutsford-road, and at the rear of his house there were a number of outbuildings which he used as a stable, cow sheds and piggeries. He believed defendant was also engaged in another business of a commercial Deuce. On Sunday last Mr Lloyd, a water inspector, visited the premises in conaequence of certain statements which had been made to him by one of the workmen in the employ of the Corporation. The Inspect-ir arrived there about 10,45 in the morning and found attached to the kitchen tap a hose pipe, some sixty feet in length, delivering water to a barrel stand- ing near a steam boiler in the outbuildings. On making further inquiries he found that defendant kept on the premises two cows, one horse, twenty sows, and about a hundred suckliogs. A large boiler wai used for the pur- pose of cooking food for the pigs. Defendant had been keeping cattle for some time and was well acquainted with the water regulations. He knew perfectly well that it WB9 his duty to call at the water office and arrange for any supply of water other than for domestic purposes. As a matter of fact in July he called at the office and told them that he had two cows and one horse, but:said nothing about the boiler or the pigs. He must have known that it was an unlawful act to connect his private house with outbuildings and he (Mr Wilson) submittod that defendant's object was to avoid payment of an extra sum for water. He was liable to a penalty of £s.—Mr Steel said defendant did not admit that his object was to obtain water without paying for it. He committed the offence entirely out of misapprebension,and had no intention of defrauding the Corporation. He was new willing to take steps to ask the Corporation to fix a pine in the stable.— The Chairman said he believed defendant' conduct bad been a deliberate and complete fraud on the Corporation which bad been carried on for a considerable time. If be had been a poor, illiterate man, who did not know better, the magistrates might have taken this into con- sideration. but a man in the defendant's pesition. who was the secretary of a limited company and had bad considerable business experience to his (Mr Roberts's) knowledge, was not a man to make a mistake of the kind stated.—Defendant would be fined £3 and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Thomas Morris Charity.</head>
        <p>Thomas Morris Charity.</p>
        <p>The many friends of the late Mr Thomas Morris will be glad to hear that the trustees of this charity selected 86 respectable old women for a pair of blankets and 34 for a red flannel petticoat. These seasonable articles were cUstributed last week at the office of Messrs Arthur Browne and Crompton, Sankey-street, Warrington, the blankets having been supplied by Mr Paulson, draper, of Sankey- street. Warrington, and the petticoats by the War- rington Charity Investigation Society.</p>
