      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RUNCORN CYCLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>Annual Dinner.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner, concert and distribution of priz ,s of Runcorn C.C., took place at Wilson's Hotel on Wednesday evening. The function was a great success, about 80 members partaking of a first-class repast, prepared by Mr and Mrs Wildgoose. Unfor- tunately indisposition prevented the president, Mr Taylor. J.P., taking his position at the head of the table, and the chair was occupied by Mr Fred J Norman, J P. who was suppc,rted by Messrs. H Con- greve' CE, J J Shaw, R Bowden, B. Chadwick, A Abel, R Briscoe, W Booth (hon secretary), &amp;C. Prior to proposing the loyal toast, the Chairman vaid an eloquent tribute to the absent president. No man he said, deserved such universal respect as did Mr Taylor. At all times and under all circum- stances Mr Taylor was prepared to help any good movement forward. Ha was his (Mr Norman's) beat ideal of a citizen. Be never allowed his private interests, his party feeling or his religious creed to enter into anything that affected the pub- lic good. Mr Taylor always tried to put everything on the broadest possible lines and they all sincerely hoped to-morrow would see him all right again. (Applause.) Mr Congreve in an oppropriate speech, referred to the fact that the membership had increased dur- ing the year and that the club had an enduring memorial of good work done in the Cottage Hospital He then distributed the prizes as follows: Medals for attendances: Messrs G Whinyat‘s J Waterworth, J McKay, J W Jackson, J Faulkner and J Green. Five miles scratch (gold medal pre- sented by Mr Taylor) : W Rae. Five miles handi- cap the (gold medal presented by Mr A Abel) : W J Barrop, 2, W Rae; 3, A Charlesworth. 25 mile handicap 1, (gold medal presented by Mr Con- greve) : A Charlesworth. 2, (medal presented by Mr Abel) : W J Harrop, 3, W Rae (club medal). Slow Race 1, W J Herron. (copper kettle presented by the club) : 2, W Rae, (cycle lamp presented by the hen secretary. The Chairman gave the toast of the evening "The Runcorn Cycle club" in an eloquent speech,in which he referred to the past athletics played and ought to play in our national life, and alluding to the Cycle Club's noble efforts as in the cause of the Cottage Hospital. He advocated the Committee of Management being constituted on the broadest possible lines and there there would be no difficulty in raising the money necessary to carry on the magnificent institution ' Mr W Booth acknowledged the toast in appropri- ate terms,</p>
        <p>The pleasure of the Company was much enhanced by the musical and elocutionary efforts of a party of Liverpool amateurs consisting of Messrs Sid Baste, B V Sayers, D 0 Parry, T Edwards, J Mullen and Leach, Messrs Bunion and Waterworth also assisted.</p>
        <p>Lecture on " Macauley." Continuing his course of lectures on "Prose Writers of the Ninetienth Century " at the 00-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Thursday evening, Mr J C Powys. M.A.. took as the subject of his fourth lecture " Macauley." No author, he said, was better known to the English people than Macauley. In explaining the reason of Macauley's popularity, the Lecturer said Macau- ley wasla writer, the texture of whose mind was peculiarly adapted to the English nation. Speaking in a nobly heightened etylesthe language of plain men, dealing with moral and intellectual questions from the point of view of plain men, Macauley never shocked conventional susceptibili- ties by flashes of terrifying insight, nor did be advance beyond the circle of average intelligence into obscure regions. There was nothing mystic or transcendental in Macauley. He was an orator first and a writer afterwards. After pointing out Macauley's limitations due to his Philistinism and Whigism, the lecturer referred to Macauley's great common-sense, which he possessed in a more marked degree than even Dr Johnson or Will'am Shakespeare. In analysing Macauley's style, the Lecturer said in the true sense of the word, Macauley's style was rhetoric. He used every , argument that would serve the purposes of his 1 special pleading and this led him into extravagance of language. His essays were really treatises and covered the whole history of the world. Ilia mind was capaciously receptive of facts and the scope of his reading was immense. He was a great critic of literature. The best period of English prose was undoubtedly the eighteenth century and !Macaulay must be given the distinction of being one of the greatest of English prose writers.</p>
