      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>f_tttero to the E'bitor.</head>
        <p>f_tttero to the E'bitor.</p>
        <p>TO CRICKET' CLUB SECRETARIES.</p>
        <p>Sir.—Whilst we ate in the middle of the football sea son, Cticket Club secretaries will be busy arranging fi tures tor their respective clubs for the ensuing seaso However, before such fixtures are completed, may suggest, throngb the medium of your paper, that a attempt should be made to try and form a Cricket League. There are many clubs in Wsrrington and dis- trict who I am certain would join if a start were only made, and i someone would take the iedtiativo step in the matte', I am sure the ides would go with a swine. I should be glad to see what ocher Secretaries thill of the que-tion.—Yours A SECRETARY. fThe writer of " Notes on Games and Athletics " in the" Examiner " has aim a word for Cricket Citit4 Secre- taries: He would be much obliged if they would regu- larly send reports of their matches with a few comments on the play..—En. 't EL"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Alderman Roberts' Resignation.</head>
        <p>Alderman Roberts' Resignation.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I bn permitted as a fairly-large ratep tyer of the town to express my very sincere regret at thq re- signation of Alderman Roberts. I hold TIO brief for this gentleman and have no irrerests to serve other than those ot every other ratepayer. But Ido most sine, rely deplore the fat that he has been hound-ci out of public lde by what I consider to have been a wanton and un- scrupulous agitation. That his firm have had deslirws with the Corporation has been well-known for rats— but if this is undesirable wby not agitate to alter the law—or get a Micu,e passed in toe Town Council prohibitin thi ? Ris letter of resig- nation was to inc Elludc pAthetic. How well I remember his first "at home Mayer. Surely no , Mayor ever had such a fisttering honour as he had then —judging from the crowds who assembled to pay him homage. No such demonstration has ever been wit- nessed in Warrington before. But, alas t. for the futility, the fleetness of popular favour—in less than two years, the once-while public favourite is put in the public pillory and reviled and execrated before all men, with the result that he is, as I have before stated, h.unded out of public life. However, it may be some satisfaction to Mr Roberts to know that he has some friends in the town after all that has been said. I said some friends—l may say hosts cf Lie/ids. I had a very striking instance of this only list night. I was in a certain company and remarks were made by one person derogatory to Mr Roberts, when a working man got up and striking the table with his fist said be would never stand by and bear Mr Roberts' name sullied. He said •he had never been introduced to, nor had he ever spoken to Mr Roberts, but he was prepared to defend Mr Roberta' character in a court of law to the tune of a hundred pounds—and this, he said, was his own hsrd earned money. The man's manner sufficiently indicated his thorough earnestness in what he said and I know personally that he had the money to stake. I wish to state this as my public sympathy with, and confidence in, our late alderman: Yours</p>
        <p>JOHN GANDY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>State Church and some of its Pretensions.</head>
        <p>The State Church and some of its Pretensions.</p>
        <p>Er,—The agitation and bitterness that are now dis- turbing the peace of all sections of the ragious world are, I believe, mainly due ti the arrogrant action of the State Church. Already in possession of a monopoly of State patronage and assistance she is trying to wlvance her interests by a still further increase of the power of her priests over the religious eduction of the young This undisguised violation of " the golden rule', is probably the outcome of an idea or belief that the Church by law established is really the Church of Christ, and its priests the successors of the Apost:es,and therefore worthy of all acceptance. I propose in this sad a future letter, to set forth two strong reasons for rejecting both of these propositions. And in doing so I shall not meddle with the creeds or ceremonies of the Church as her outward and visible frsmework f arnishes ample material for my purpose. SS ben Jesus Christ came into the world the Jewish Church was about to disappear. For fifteen centuries it had served the high and noble purpose of pnserving the knowle dee of God in the midst of heathen clarkasee ; and by its types and shadows had poiated,however dimly to the Lamb of God, who, in due time, was to be mani- fested as the Saviour of the world. When that Lamb appeared, there was, of course, no further need for the types and shadows. Aud some fcrty years after His crucifixion the Romene destroyed the temple where alone they could be legally performed. On the site of that sacred structure there steeds to-day the great Mosque of Omar built twelve hundred years ago. And the New Testament, fully and clearly reveals the origin and nature of the religion which Jesus set up in the place of the Hebrew one. It was cot a system of forms and ceremonies, but a purely spiritual revelation based upon the love of God for mankind, and in no way de- pended upon human patronage or support for its establishment and extension. Its disciples were not to be made by priesily manipulations, but by the opera- tion of the Divine Spirit upon their hearts, convincing them of sin and enabling them to embrace the heavenly salvation. And, of course, the headehip or supreme authority in such an institution could only be vested in its Divine Founder, as no merely human being could possess the qualifications requisite to fill an office so closely associated with the present and eternal destinies I of men. Christ as God, as well as man,is therefore, the one and only Lord anci,Maater of all believers, and such believers are bound by their love and fealty to him to resist, even unto blood if necessary, the claim of any mortal, whether King or priest. who would dare to assume his prerogative. Now if the foregoing view of Christianity be sound and I believe it is, it will be an easy task to show that the English Church opeely centravenesiits most import- ant principle, the headship of Christ. In doing this she follows the evil example of Constantine who, in the fourth century,set himself up as the head of the Church. Let me prove, from his own oracles, her complicity in this brozr isurpation. In the year 1562, with the con- sent and approval of the Archbishops, Bishops, and the I whole clergy. the sovereign of England made the following declaration.—" Being by God's Ordinance. according to our just title, defender of the faith, and supreme governor of the Church, :within these our deminione, we bold it most agreeabielto this our kingly office, and our own religious zeal, to conserve and maintain the Church committed to our charge, in the unity of true religion, and in the bonds of peace." (Book e' roman Prayer : Preface, to the articles of religion). This language holds good in the Church to-day, and its true,obvions meaning is that Causr, and not Christ, is the supreme governor of the Church,fer, of course, there cannot be two supreme rulers in any ore- vines whether spiritual Cr material. But the Church has a salve for any tender consciences that may be wounded by this dishonouring of their Lord and Master In her xxxvii article she tells them that, "We give not to our Princes the ministering either of God's word, or of the sacraments ; but that only prerogative, which we see to have been given always to all godly princes in holy scripture by God himself." In the first part of this quotation we see another bold attempt of "The close Corporation of priests" to curtail religions liberty. If the King is a Christian, not in name ealy but also in spiritale shares,in common with all believers, not only the privilege but the duty of ministering in holy things according to the grace given unto him. The Church is equally unfortunate in her reference to the godly princes of Scripture. I have read • good deal about these . good men, but I have yet to learn when God appointed them to be "supreme governors " of his Church, originally the Jewish people lived under a Theocracy—direct govern- ment by God himself, acting through divinely in- spired seers and judges. And it was only, when they revolted mainst that government that kings tame upon the scene, not as supreme rulers of thines ecclesiastical, but as instruments to punish them for the's' rebelliou. Moreover, it certainly is not true that the church gives her besdehip only to godly princes, for one learns from history that soros of them were the reverse of eodly,and yet they were not deposed on that account. But after all, the dicta of kings and priests must not he allowed to influence our juagment on the grave subject under discussion. The Lord himself is our only teacher and exemplar in the matter. Had it been His will and intention that his church ehoulci at any time be under State patronage and control, as the Eeglish Establishment is, He would surely have left us some " light and leading "on each a momentous subject. But He has not dem) so. Indeed, throughout His whole life He appears to hays avoided both bishops and politicians. Neither the Jewish lanhedrim nor the Raman Proconsul could have any part in setting up and establishing His Church. That Church being a heavenly, and not an earthly inatitution,it could only live and prosper under His cwn Almighty and gracieus rule. In the days of His flesh He claimed to be the one and only Lord and Master of His disciples, and from that time until now all true believers have joyfully acknowledged Him as such. When one sees a man or a woman assume the supreme control of the Church of Christ one's thoughts will instinctively go forward to the predicted time when a still greater innovation will be developed in the person of the " Man of Sin," who will sit in the temple of God, and exeit himself above all that iii werehipped. In common with all ether religious bodies, the State Church has much that is true and good, and much to be admired and con- served within her fold. But her dogma of the Royal Supremacy, like her dogma of war is anti-Christian and a atone of stumbling and a rock of offence to all believers who have learned to regard the Church of Christ as a spiritual community far above human jurisdiction. The laws of men may be obeyed, so long as they do not violate the laws of God ; but when such laws, whether enacted by King or priest, invade the province a Christ his followers are under no obligation to observe them. In a future letter I hops to deal with the claim of the State priests to be regarded as the auccessers of the 405tieF,—Yours truly, lOWA.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sam Woods had a kind ot free and easy gathering on Saturday night at Earlestown. He came to meet his workers, who indulged in a cup of tea. Later on they had a kind of social gather- ing, and Mr Woods gave them a good address on the question of representation. It could not be called a fighting meeting, but it was a uoeful one, and brought Mr Woods more into touch with those who will have to work for him than any amount of public meetings.</p>
        <p>THE -EXA iN ER FRI )AY NOVEMBER 21, 1902 NOTES BY THE WAY AT THE MAN WHO MENACED THE I EARLESrowN. PREMIER. LBY A CoURESPONDENTI. Mr. John. O'Donnell, M.P:, at The United Irish League had a capital meeting Earlestown. on Saturday night, on the occasion of the visit of On Saturday night the Earlestown Davin branch of Mr John O'Donnell, M P, of Balfour fame. The hall the United Irish League had a large and enthusiastic was crowded, and the platform was well filled, gathering in the Town Hall when an interesting address amongst the latter being some downright fire,eatere. was given by Mr John O'Donnell, M P ler South Mace. Tee meeting opened very tamely, but when the Dr O'Keefe, et Helens, presided, and he was supported Chairman of the local branch of the League had on the platform by Councillor Tageart,of Liverpool. Mr finished speaking he had worked his audience up to F Tetley of Manchester, Mr E Crilley, organiser, and sucks a pitch that they w'ere fit for anything. He meTmhbeecrsuoifrmthienlsoariadl be had, during the) afternoon, paid were the Issague. said some people called them Feuians, but they a visit to the premises of the local branch of the U were not Fenians. still it did not matter what they lriasohnLersaienue wanhdeftile T thewa s lw a agreeablyees,unripurcits:ig.tol,sewe,,t9hae were cal:ed, and if they could not get their rights in any other way, they would be proud to be Feni- creplorable thing that some Irishmen when they rose an ans. This raised the root, so to speak, and as other inch or two in the SOCiil scale or climbed a rung or two orators proceeded in a somewhat fisry strain, no of the golden ladder thought they were no longer Irish. adjective was too bad for the British Government. men and deserted the old country. Every Irishman owed his country a debt of gratitude and it was the duty of every Irishman to help paying off tiler, debt. Mr O'Donnell is a medium sized man, of a peace- ful aspect, and nobody would Suspect that be could (Hear, hear.) The members of the local branch seemed fir a moment dare to act as he did in regard to Mr to appreciate thoroughly what that debt was, end were Balfour. After listening to him, however, for a thing their best to discharge it. It was something fora short time, as he warmed to his subject one was men to boast of, that he had been born in Ireland, and not surprised that he was carried away by his cfoorutnhtary and he ought to do something t pay off that He was also at times facetioue,especially when talk- debt honour he owed a debt of eratitude to his feelings on the memorable occasion in question, debe of gratitude. (Applause). ing of Mr Wyndham. He said Mr Wyndham, or Mr Mellodee moved the following resolution "That we the Irishmen and women of Earlestown and district George, as he called him, did his best condemn the British Government for the cowardly and to copy Mr Balfour. When Mr Balfour brutal manner in which they are carrying out their smiled, " George " smiled, and when Mr. coercion ant in crimeless Irelandethet we express out Balfour frowned, Mr Wyndham's countenance heartfelt sympathy to our fellow countrymen who hive also underwent a change. Mr Balfour was bran imprisoned for offering the only advice to the notoriously a lazy man, and was in the habit of people of Irel old that will save them from extermin- lolling about on the Treasury benches, and when ation. Wdeamba el esgno tt pao rl eyads partygs tie rce ut rhs :01 them, bby hnl ide tde edvb ecy ro lafirt jtnouchdene i nßtthe edy. thehis lteeglesl ewi re fronttired o fhheimactu:llGlyeoprguet ,his ifteaetteudpelOnan ImriesnhdP,aarnli so far as the lolling went, but nature had not been may take with regard to the Eincation Bill, will have our unsawoe7Diondensruli that his recent action in tee House could not therefore put his feet on the table as his support. Furthermore we be to assure so generous with him when giving out legs, and he D lr eJoh f Commons in defying i yine and despising Messrs Wendhern legs were too short. and Balfour has excited our admiration and approval and that nothing he may do in that respect will ever disturb the confidence and esteem with which he is so justly helenby Irishmen and women in Eirlostown " Mr Hosgrove seconded and said that in appreciation of what they had done, the moral and material support they had rendered to the Irish party, they could always rely upon the attendance of some of the men whose names were well known in all Irish homer. They believed that the Irish party were sent from Ireland to Westminster to look aster the interests of the tenant farmers and the people of England, but they were not sent to advocate the rights of bigots or of any relieious party whatever. The duty of the Irishmen wan to look after the interests of the party in Ireland, and so long as they did that, they could always look to the answering support of the Nationalists of Earlestown and district. 'Mr M Flanaghan supported the resolution, saying they had 82 representatives going from Irihand as repre- sentatives of the people, and they were thrown out of Parliament and sent into prison like dogs and not one of them had a voice in the ruling of their native land. Rulers were sent over to Ireland who were altogether averse to the wants of the people of Ireland. (Hoar, hear.) ra e tenant nlruatniFot resolution. .aFramrE eeery saanioddi IMur ac jnhoc hha nebsuotse,epr was nes oe ti erwou was • spnot urhtpee odsn_, nht of in the House of Commons a few nights ago. They were detereeired to stand shoulder to shoulder by the fighting men of their party. The fight for liberty was their fight and they would make it their fight until victory had blessed the arms of the Irish people in their struggle for liberty. Their movement was a democratic move- ment, a labour movement, and it must succeed. Their duty lay deep down in their hearts. It was their own nation which had been down trodden and persecuted for 700 years and they stood to-day the only unconquered race on the face of God's earth. (Cheer'.) If there was to be loyalty and good feeling, arid a good understand- ing between the two peoples, he maintained that the people who had persecuted them so long, should stop to consider where they were going to, and what they were doing to brine' about this state of things. He appealed to the good feeline of any Englishmen who were present to recollect that they had a Case, that the English system of Govern- ment had been a failure, absolutely cruel and unjust, and he claimed for it that it was their duty as Irishmen and every fair minded Englishmen in that hall, to see that justice was done, Ireland liberated, and that the accursed system of Castle rule was obliterated. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Taggart, of Liverpool also spoke in eupeort of the resolution' which on being put to the meeting was carried with acclamation. Mr John O'Donnell, M P. said it was two years since he had the pleasure of standing on that platform before, and since then the Cause of Ireland had advencei by leaps and bounds, and if they only continued for two years more, as they had in toe past, there was no doubt their success would be assuied. The question of Home Rule was foremost in the naiads of Irishmen, but now it was more advanced in the minds of Englishmen than it was when he spoke to them two years ago. Two years of steady solid fighting work in the House of Commons and on the hill sides of Ireland bad brought the qoestion of Home Rule so much to the front that neither Tory nor Liberal could any longer ignore it. They believed it was utterly impossible for , one man to govern the affslrs of his neighbour's house- hold. They believed in the justice of their cause and in the justice of tbeir demands and were determined to have their old house in College Green once more. (Applause.) It was the aim and ambition of every liberty loving Irishman, and was the first plank in the United Irish League preeramme. The conditions of the system in Ireland were altogether different from the conditions affecting the land santem in this country. There was mores than one victim of landlord oppression in that hall who left Ireland when a boy who knew very little of the Irish question, but felt great bitterness against the English government. People were harried from their homes, not teceuse they could not pay their rent, but I cause of the unfortunate system which was in force in the country. They were fighting against a system which had caused these things, and in doing stelae thought they couldl safely lay claim to:pr otection not only from their own countrymen, but from English- men who were desirous of doing right to human beings. (Hear, hear.) He believed if the Irish land question was settled, and the tenant farmers were the owners of their own holding, Irishmen would be satisfied. It was because they wanted to preserve their nation that they were making euch a fight for the land, in order Zhat the people might first get held of it, and then till it and enjoy the fruits of their labours. (Hear, hear.) The system of landlordism was the cause of all the mis- fortune of Ireland. They must, during the next few years, try to put an end to that system and banish land- lordism, lock seek and barrel out of Ireland. (Cheers.) It was no wonder that young men who left Ireland, because they could not help it, went to other lands with vengeance+ and feelings of hatred agsdast a Govern- ment which forced them to seek a livelihood thousands and thousands of miles from home, The sys• ten of landlordism had been supported by the English Government because they felt it was the best way to rob the people of Ireland, in order to keep them opnreesed and down trodden, robbing liven to the extent of over £lOOO.OOO, in taxes more than they were entitled to pay, and more than £1,000,000 was devoted to the upkeep of the Royal Irish Constabulary. But the Constabulary, who were the police, were different to the police in this country. The latter were not used as a soldiery to brutally illtreat the people who were keep- ing them and supplied them with a means of livelihood. The Constabulary had re:thine to de with the prevention of crime but they had a lot to do with organising crime. If an Englishman was deprived of trial by jury and of free speech he would be up in rebellion in 24 hours. Because he and his colleagues tried to fight for the liberty of the people in Ireland Vika, were aralkod by Mr Wyndham and imprisoned. (Shame). Re had charged the Irish members witb being agitaters for mer- cenary motives. that they would not be agit stars unless they made money out of It. That was not a truthful charge, it was a baseloss charge and he said the man was a liar who made it. Was Mr George Wyndham the great English patriot who worked for norhine For mismanaging Ireland he got a salary of £4,000 a year and he never foront to draw it. In addi,ion to that he received £2 000 for an assistant to help him to carry en the devil's work in Ireland. He had not the common decency to pay his own laundry hill. Last veer he drew from the British treasury £l5 to pay for his laundry bill in Ireland. That was one of the gentlemen of England who taunted the Iri,Jh leaders with making money out of the Irish peenle. He said he was a lose cad. (Cheers). Ha had tri ,c 1 to become a greet man In Englend throush his persecution of the Irish people, but the Irish psoole had proved teueh nuts for him and others to crack. (Leughter closed theandapollsaeuse. meeting. Votes of thanks to the speakers</p>
        <p>EVIDENCE, NO F HgARST-IY,—A careful investiga- tion has been made by a trustworthy reporter into a rP- markable cure at Boenlev, aaorihed to the use of Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. Tim whole affair has been sifted nut, and an interview with the person concerned obtained. with the reau;t that the case is found an astonishing one. The evidence col- lected by the reporter is submitted in another column. From what we can learn. moat people in Lancashire swear by Dr Williams pink pills, and ,b is not to he wondered at, in view o the hundr,ds of cores they have affected in cases of paraly,is, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and Sciatica f , also all diseas-s arising from impoverishment of the; blood, scrofula, rlckess, chronic erysipelas,consumption of the bowels and lung, ansemia, pale and sallow com- plexion, general muactil-r weakness, loss of appetite, palpitations, pains in the hack, nervous headache, and neuralgia, early cl-cay,all forma of female weakness,and hysteria These pills are a tonio, not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name, Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, and are sold by chemists and by Dr Williams' Medi,line Company, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, E C., at 24 91 a buc, or six box.i for 13s 9d. Pink pills sold loose or from glass jars are not Dr Williams' pink pills ; accept them only in the pink coed wrapper as above cribed.</p>
