      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>IHE CLATTER OF THE CANS. A Burnley Dairyman's Surprise Packet.</head>
        <p>IHE CLATTER OF THE CANS. A Burnley Dairyman's Surprise Packet.</p>
        <p>Even a milkman's life is not devoid of adventure, and one experienced by Mr John Towason, of Islr 1, Brown- aide, Burnley, is well worth rec3unting. Mr fownson, who is about thirty-two years of age, is a fine specimen of the robust, fresh-looking, and obligicg dairsman,and has been for some time in the service of Mr George Halstead, of To wneley Lot' ge Farm, Burnley. He has Ilnt always been engaged in farm work, and the circumstances under which he was able to resume the calling .with which he is most familiar has excited a good deal of comment. A representative of the " Burnley. Gazette " sought out Mr. Townson, whe was buoy on his round, but be consented to pause while he told his story. He said that, some time ago, he was prostrated by a severe attack of rheumatii sm from which he obtained no relief until he tried Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, which cured him completely. " I had to give up farm work, to which I had bean accustomed." con- tinued Mr. Townson," and tried work in a cotton mill. This did no better for me, and I was obligel to take to my bed for six or seven weeks. My appetite left me, and I could hardly get any sleep, the pains were so bad. They shot about in all directions—sometimes in my arms, then int-, my hands, and dawn to my thighs. I suffered severely. I was under a doctor for several weeks, but, in spite of his skill and attention, the pains did not abate.</p>
        <p>Eventually, I was persuaded to go to my father's farm, to see if the rat and change would do me any good. This was at Sedgewick Farm, near Blackburn. My parents were shocked to see me looking like an old man, and ho bling about on tRo sticks. I lay in bed at home for a few days, and then my mother strongly recommended me to try a box of Dr Williams's pink 1 piils. She had often read in the papers of peopls they ! bad cured of rheumatism, and other severe illnesgeg,and pressed me to try them. I laughed at the idea of pills curing me, after I had been under a doctor, without getting the slightest benefit. HOWOV6r she brought • box home with her, ana I began taking them. " Before I had finished the first box I felt a decided relief.l could have jumped over a five-barred gate,even after the first few doses. I felt so moon better that I couldn't lie in bed any longer, so I longue.' up. and startled my parents by telling them I was coming down- stairs to milk. They couldn't believe it ; but I got up and milked tne cows alright, and 'AX9 able to move about without any trouble. I have never had any rneumatistn since (although I have been exposed to all sorts ot weather), with the exception of one slight touch I then got another box of Dr Williams' pink pills,and they put rue all right again. I have never had any trouble since, and that is going back some time. Answering further inquiries, Mr Townaon mentioned that he took six or seven boxes of the pills in all, and he attributed his recovery entirely to them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the P.S.A, was held in the Co-cperative Ball, Earle-street, Earlestown, on Sunday last. Mr George Clarke presided over a large attendance. A short address was given by Mr John. Jackson. Mr A Bantom rendered several vocal items at intervals. Recitations were given by Miss Williams. Miss Williamson accompanied at the piano. EARLRBTJWX THURSDAY FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>The fitth annual ball in connection with the above club was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown on Wednesday evening. The room was very prettily decorated for the occasion by Mr A Clarke, and there was a large attendance of dancers. Tho M.G.'s were Messrs H Walton and EL E Pritchard and an excellent programme of dances was :(312e through. The catering was in the hands of Vlr G Ed wards. 1.0. G T. HOPE OF EARLEST OWN LODGE:—</p>
        <p>The usual weekly session was held on tuesdaY evening in the Co-operative Hall. Bro • C Hatton, F CT, presided. After the usual business was finished, the following programme was gone through :—Ode 32 was sung, and solos were ren- dered by Sisters J Hill, Mrs Williams, and Mrs Owen ; Lancashire readings by Bros G Clarke and J Warburton were thoroughly enjoyed. An earnest address was then given by Bro B J George. The best thanks of the Lodge were heartily accorded Bro George for his address A short sketch of the early life of Bro Joseph Matins, GC T being 13ad by BroJ Woods, brought a very pleasant and enjoyable evening to a close. FREE CHURCH COIJNCIL'S CONVERSAZIONE.</p>
        <p>A conversazione in connection with the Free Church Council of Newtou-in-M tkerfield was held in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, Lord-street, on Thursday evening. Tea was served at 630 about 300 partaking thereof, several lady members of the I Free Churches presiding at the tables. After tea a concert was given in the schoolroom. The chair was I occupied by Mr J U PrDflt,the president of the Coun- cil who in a few remarks said be was pleased Le be ' present at this gathering, and he was quite sure that all the members of the Council were just as much gratified. He hoped that in the future more of these meetings would be held. He referred at some length to the formation of the Council, and said that it could only be kept together by constant prayer. Brief addresses on the work of the Coun- cil were also delivered by the Revs S D Thomas, G Cook, and C E Dove. Mu•tical items were contribu- ted by Miss L Barr, Mrs Turner, qiBSeiS J Howard, L Jones, C Sirett, D Roberts, S Watkins, L Stronach, Messrs G Kidd, Edmunds. J Sephtoo, and T A Limond. Mr G Kidd accompanied at the piano.</p>
        <p>INSTALLATION OF A FRBEMASON AT NEWTON. The installation cf Bros. Arthur W Anderron to the Mskerfield Lodge (2155) Newton-le-Willows was held at the Lodge rooms, Pied Bull Hotel, on Tuesday evening. The installation master was Bro J Gooding 1P M. Among those present were Brothers J D Murray JG T Eng., H mtoneg P M, PP G D,(2155) J N Paterson. P 51, P PD,G D C, (484) R H Pearson PP(I C, (143) E L Bent, T Ormand and others, including Brethren from the 'various Lodges in the West and East of Lancashire ; and also Cheshire brindles Having been duly ' installed in the chair of King S)lotnon,the worship- ful rawer appointed the following as his masters IG C Starkey JP M, j Singleton S W, R Corlett J W. Rev J Ryder Chaplain, T Graham, Treasurer, J 11 Lane Sec, J Williamson S D, T Barnes J D, 1H Stone P M, P P 5, G D, DC. J Prescott organist,T Southern I G, FT Bennie, A G Gaunce, H NI Wight- man,W Austin,R Cummings,Tyler. The worshipful master presented to Bro Starkey the past master, jewel in recognition of hie services as master of the lodge during the past year, and complimented Bro Starkie on his able fulfilment of his duties Tie usual St John's banquet followed. A musical enter- tainment was afterwards given and included items y Bro R H Pearson of Warrington and 1)1 Proctor Chester Cathedral.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>EDUCATION BILL. --- Protest meeting at Runcorn. Speeches by Sir J. T. Brunner, Bart., M.P., and mr. Henry Broadhurst, M.P.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION BILL. --- Protest meeting at Runcorn. Speeches by S- ir J. T. Brunner, Bart., M.P., and mr. Henry Broadhurst, M.P.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening at the Primitive Methodist School room, Greenway-road, Runcorn a protest fir e:ing against the Eiucation Bill was held usder the presi- dency of Mr F J Norman. Teen) was a very large attendance. Sapoorting the Chairman beside. Sir J Brunner, thrt., M P., and Mr Henry Broa,i'qur. 4", M P. vro.e Messrs S Taylor, J.P., A R Nortnan,J.P., J Wilson, Rev J 03born.M.A., W A Ere, Bev A W..119,11ry Sidney Morris, W Millngton, J M Frith,A R Frith, W Handley, : R H Posnett and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>RESOLUTION.</head>
        <p>THE RESOLUTION.</p>
        <p>Mr Norm sn,who was received with appl inse,said that he Was not responsible for occupying the chair that evening. Others had placed him there and in return they would have ta hear what ha had to say respsctieg the Education Bill. The resolution he had to propose was " Tnat the Runcorn townspeople protest against the Bill lescauee it abolished School Boards and threw the cost of sectarian schools on tie public without giving popular control ; that it legalized and imposed theo- logical tests on the appointment of teachers ; that the meeting further protested against the closure of neces- sary and legitimate debate in the House of Commons,and was in favour of a national syritem of education free from sectarianism." Cantinuine, Mr Norman said that tna sectarian claim was that every child should rocs edu- cated iu as atmosphere of its own particular faith. Tnis was really impracticable because every child in the country woull have this right and thus the national system would be wane straight away as there would have to be 400 or 500 different dogmatic atmospheres. The claim of citizenship was that for every child an the land the State ought to provide a sound secular education with a religious grounding of those fundamentals on which the majority of the denominations were egread. It was a question of the God-given Bible against toe man-made creed. (Hear, hear.) In 1870 it was religion versus atheism, now it Was religion versus theology. The Board schools taught their scholars a religion that WAS almost utai- vessel in contrast to the sectarian schools which taught sectarianism. In Runcorn the two systems were in vogue. The V. luntary Schools had tPen there many generations, the B oard only two or three. The stroagest argument in favour of the latter system was that the Board schools were cro vded with children of the bsst church people in Runcorn, who were satisfied with the religious instruction given. In the Board school the children were not asked to hold their hands up if they went to chapsl or church. They were taught rattier that they were all children of the great, good God sod citizens of a mighty empire. And the national platform maintained that it was their duty to make cit:z ms and not sectarians, (Applause). The sectarian formula was resolved into getting one man's money to teach another man's creed. The "Times " had admitted that the parson had abused his power in a great many parishes and had pushed his church views in the day school. And the Bill made that condition of things eternally lawful. The citizens claim was that they who paid the piper should choose the tune, and in the Runcorn Board this was the case. But this could not be said of the National Schools. As defined on the sectarian platform three weeks ago—when those present were put on their honour not ta oppose the resolution—the sectarian policy was to teach the children the doctrines of the Church of England. It was all Church and nothing but Church. The citizsns platform was the education of the People, for the people by the people. And this was plainly denied them Ira the hard dry facts of the Bill. Whoever drafted it must have been steeped with Machia- vellian doctrine. It was a measure that would make meta martyrs to their faith or hypocrites to another. The parson was made the predominant partner, and the sacred rights of parentship were trampled under foot. The Bill gave the parson a happy hunting eround over which no one had any real control. The Government had no mandate to introduce such a measure and they had ne moral right to force it on the people. The 1311 even had not the support of Parlia- ment. Over 100 Unionists were opposed to it and had it not been for the Roman Catholic vote the Bill would have been defeated. Tney must insist on popalar con- trol and that 'se long as the people payed the money no religious tests should be enforced. (Applause.) ThE MEMBER FOR THE DIVISION CONDEMNS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>BILL.</head>
        <p>THE BILL.</p>
        <p>Sir John T Brunner rose amid prolenged applause to second the resolution. He believed most devotedly in the resolution. Before touceing on the question he would like to say what a great los3 theenation had se- tamed in the death of the Rev Hugh Price Hughes. He was a gallant leader in every good cause. He had been taken away in his crime, which was a very great loss to Liberalism. Mr Norman had said he did not care a rap for any of the amendments to the Bitl, which wa3 rsth.r a hard knock at himself oad bis friena Mr Broadhurst who had hen fighting week after week to get amendments into the Bill. He strongly believed in making a bad job better. Had it not been for their efforts the Bill would have been a mush greater misfer- tune to the country than it was geing to be. Unstinted praise was due to the splendid band of young Liberals who had fought the fight in the House headed by Lloyd George. (Applause). Toes' had done yeoman service for their country, for freedom, and for true religion. Over forty clays had been spent in ceminittee which meant eight parliamentary weeks. Sir John then pro- ceeded to deal ori`h the various amendment.; intro- duced into tJe Bill, giving a detailed account of the changee. lie said that they had eeoured that the money to be spent on the children was not to be limi,ed which was a great step. The Cheshire County Council had spent their whisky money wisely by devot- ing it all to Technicel instruction. Runcorn not being the education authority would still have the power to spend its own money upon Technical Instruction. As the Bill was framed the property of the Technical Institute passed to the County Council. He himeelf, interviewed Mr Balfour on the subject and he promised to amend the Bill so that all such schools would still be , the property of the ratepayers. The Government Bill as drafted put men into an inferior position on ac3ount of religious belief but the opposition had managed to reverse that. They had also secured one third of the I management of the Voluntary Schools and would -never I give up the fight until they had absolute equality for every man. Again they had provided that if the teacher did not satisfy the clergyman on Saturday night after 1 the school was closed or if he did not go to Church on Sunday he could not he dismissed for ;that as hitherto. I He certainly thought that the school managers should I realize their responsibilities better and take a deeper interest in educational matter.. The Henyon-Slane7 amendment was the only one put in from the Conserve- I tive benches with the exception of one or two put in by the Liberal Unionists. Colonel KeayoneSialon9 was an evangelical Irishman who believed that the parsons' nails wanted cutting. The amendment really meant that the religious instruction would be under the control of the managers instead of under the parer. All the Liberal party voted for the amendment whica carried by an enormous mejority and was only opposed bv:the High Church Party. In' consequence they had had a regular tornado of ;views respecting the amend- ment from the clerical part), and it had been. proposed to emend the amendment in this fashion !—tnat where l the clergymen differed from the rest of the managers or where there were two against four the clergyman should apply to the Bishop who would decide what reli- gious instruction was to be given. It was not;therefore, a question of the sacred right of the parent so much as the divine right of the Bishop. It yet remained to be seen whether this right of appeal would be given to the Billion or whether the Government would stand to their guns. It was the party whip that had canned the Bill. There was a large number of Conservatives with them in the matter. Ile was hope- ful that they would vet get popular control and that the clerical party would be disappointed. His religion was a simple one : It was to; love tie Lord thy God with all thy strength and thy neighbour ari thy e C. He honed in that spirit they would continue to fight the question until the battle was won. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>MR BROADHURST SPEAKS HIS MIND.</head>
        <p>MR BROADHURST SPEAKS HIS MIND.</p>
        <p>IMr Broadhurst, M.P., who received a great ovation in supporting, said that he felt with Sir John Brunner that the country had sustained a great loss in the death of the Rev Hugh Price Hughes. He was one of the most powerful men in the country, and had his health been good during the last two years, his tnagnificegt voice and pewer of intellect would have made the moun- tains and valleys ring against such a monstrous Bill. Of late, it would have been to the honour of the Liberal patty had they refused point blank to legislate in fetters. Not only were they losing power of control over their money, but unless they became better patriots they were going to lose their liberty. Toey were going to have executive power instead of rioreseotative Govern- ment, and were going at a break-neck pace back to the time of the Stuarts. There were evil days in store for old England. He objected to the Bill from top to bottom --a Bill which placed the County Council in supreme control. In Cheshire they had easy communica tion, but in some rural counties it was not- pospible for the small tradesman to loss time and money to attend the meetings. It was an almost wholesale exclusion of the elate most highly in'erested in elemen- tary. education. The Bill struck at the roots of right, reason and Liberalism. The money would be spent by a very small class,ancl any man who loved justioi must o'ci dead against the measure. It created universal payment for a selected Government. The majority of the people wanted religious instruction but there was a vast differ- ence between religion and dogma. It was all very well to say that the management would protect. That the minority of two would safeguard their interests. But there was nothing in the Bill to show that the two would not be two Churchmen. (Appltuse.) If they did not know what the clerical spirit was than the town was a fortunate one. To be in a perpetual minority of two was a degradation to which no honourable man should submit. By an elaborate schemetitlmight even be:made</p>
        <p>Possible for the Liberal party in the 11 mse of Commons to be placed in a perpetual tninerity. Five yesrs ago such a Bill would have bee 3 thought impossible. But it was a foretaste of other re-actionary measures that were coining. They asked above all, for equality. They should have the best teachers irrespective of creed, who should be free to do what they liked after school liqurs. (Hear, hear.) He read in a parish magazine the other day " they seemed to forget he sacrifices and noble work done for nearly 100 years In the Cause of educa- tion by the church of England and their voluntary sys- tem." There was no voluntary system, nothing but sectarianism in it. The church had never lifted her finger in the cause of national education only to save her authority in churco matters. The fact was that the religious instruction of the Board was as superior as the secular education. In some of the sectarian schools they were not only without a- Bible but without a prayerbook. Sir John Goret bad admitted that the Board Schools gave better religious instruction then the voluntary schools. How ridiculous then it was for this county Vicar or Canon to write in his Parish Magazine to belittle the religious in- struction given. Mr Broadhurst concluded with a pass- ionate appeal for all lovers to liberty to stand firm in thi great national crtsi.9.</p>
        <p>The resolution on oeinz put was unanimously cried the usual votes of thanks closiug the tweting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>BRASS 'BAND CONTEST AT RUNCORN. A Warrington Band Secures First Prize.</head>
        <p>BRASS 'BAND CONTEST AT RUNCORN. A Warrington Band Secures First Prize.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the second annual brass bind contest promoted by the Rancarn Crete Cub a am u of the Ran- cor:, Cottage Hospital was hold in the Public Hal'. Mr S Taylor presided, and was accompanied on the plat- form by Mrs R H Poanett, Mr and Mrs FJ Norman, and Mr and Mrs AR Norman. Diem was a crowded atte ad- ance. The following band l competed :—Runcern Volunteer Band, Weston Point Works Band, Helsby Public Band,St Ed wara's Band,Petaketh Tannery Bled, Daveaharn C.O.E,T.S_ B..nd, Elmo Hall Band Crostield'a (Warringtoa) Band, and Frodsbana Volunteer Band. The test piece was " A Casket of Gams " (II Rauod). Tae performances of toe bands indicated careful train- ing, and were much appreciated by a critical audience. The arrang,ernFoit for tne ju:lging was admirable and deserves to be generally adopted. The judge,Mr P Fair- hurst, of Wigan, was locked in a room on the right hand side of the hall during the whole of the performances. The bands did not play io the order of the programme, , and Mr Fairlaurat gave them numbers as they appeared and made his adjudications on the numbers. At the c mclusion of the performances be gave his report. He said the bands had been :judged not upon their reputation but upon play that day. He hoped that whatev.rr the result was the losing bands would take it in a sportsmanlike manner. Be bad gone to some trouble in preparing for the ad- jadication, having had the test piece played by his own baud and was conversant with every note. It was a strange thing that the whole of the hands bad made a mistake in a certain part. The piece, however, had been very well rendered. He awarded the gold centre medal (presented by Messrs Fattorini and Sons, Brad- ford) forithe best cornet woloist to No 1 (the Helsby band). !The first prize (a silver cup and £2) he awarded to No 3 (Messrs Crosfietra band). This band, be ,said, was far ahead of the others. The second prize went to No 7 (Runcorn Volunteer baud), the third to No 1 (Heleby band), and the fourth to No. 5 (Penketh Tannery band), Messrs Crosfield's band was coaducted by Mr R Smith and the other bands, in their order, by Messrs T Outhwaite, C Bate, and J P Harper. The cup was won last year by the Volunteer Band. It has to be won twice in succession before becoming the property of a band. The cup was presented to the winning band by Mrs R Ii Posnett, to whom a hearty vote of thanks was passed on the motion of Mr Taylor,seconded by Mr F J:Norman The Chairman was also thanked, on the motion of Mr Norman, seconded by Mr Posaett.</p>
