      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A.- SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Thsre was an average attendance at the meeting of this society on Sunday. The devosional part of the service was conducted by Me B Farrar. The chair was occupied by Mr Gauld who spoke upon the value of individual effort, and pointed out that iF each rnembor did his part, toe chum] w ,uld son be to sad.—Mr O J Brown dlivered Al address and ref, reed to the d fficulties which were placed in the way of the fallen r •turnin4 to a hettor Itfe The Feher welcomed such but the elder brothor raised objeetions when a man was _ down, It was theo herd to regain confidence-1 slight increase im membership was reported.</p>
        <p>BAPTIST caußca, GOLBORNE STREEL—THE PASTOR ON TaE HISTORICAL JE3US.</p>
        <p>The above church was filled to oveello wing on Sunday evening when the Rev Herbert Duanico gave the first of a series of sermons on the " Divinity and Deity of Christ." The preacher remarked that one of our greatest English philosophers had somewnere said, " that man. kind could not be too often reminded that there was once a man whose name was Socrates " that was no doubt true bat it was more important still that man- kind shouid be ,gaiu and again reminded that there Once lived upon th;s earth a man whose name was Jesue. Jesus Christ,said the preacher,was the most powerful spiritual force that had ever operated for good on and in humanity and who He was, what He was and how and why Re came to be it were questions of petrenial interest. He had been studied by the greatest artists and also by the greatest thinkers. He had been praised by the lover of beauty and lauded by the lover of the commonplace. Eta had been held forta as the representative of the greatest philosophers and He bad been looked up to as a type of childtike simplicity. He had been adm lr d as the embodiment of a ereed and eulogised as the negation of all creeds. Men of all ranks and of all conditions, of all creeds and of no creed at all, were all unanimous in ascribing to Christ, the supreme place in the tertidle of htsmanity. Even the unbeliever who denied Christ's Godhead assured them that Jesus of Nazareth was the great sage the mighty reformer, the wisest and holiest of mankind. Henan, the French sceptic denied Christ's Godhead yet declared Him to be the unatt :linable ideal of moral greatness and. David Strauss, the German sceptic, reserved for Christ the first place in the temple of genius. It had somewhat surprised him (the preacher) to hear that in these days there were people in Warrington who had actually doubted, if they had not denied, that Christ was a genuine lais'orical per mage. No man who wished to associate with any intelligent community of people dare deny the historicity of Jesus. That He lived in the latter half of the eighth century of the Roman period and the first of the Christ ian era, be- longed to the Hebrew race, lived in Palestine, died in Jerusalem under Pilate, were facts beyond dispute and even if it were proved that myths and legends had gathered around His name those things would ever remain because they were as well attested as any facts of history. The preacher then gave evidence from three different sources in support of this viz :—Pagan, Jewish and Christian—and quoted among others such writers as Tacitus and Pliny of the Pagan writers, Josephus from among the Jewish, and the testimony of the gospels and epistles. In the face of such overwhelming evidenee inclusive of Pagans, Jews, Sceptics, as well as Christians, all rational men were bound to admit that such a person as Jesmi of Nazareth did live. The preacher then briefly outlined the line of arguinrot which he inteoded pursuing during the next ten weeks.</p>
        <p>A HUSBAND'S EXTRAORDINARY CONDUCT—HEARTLESS THREATS. At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, before Mr G Comber (presiding) and other magistrates, Walter Hicks,Penketh, was summoned for persistent cruelty to his wife—alr Browne appeared for the Complainant and stated that the defendant was one of the keepers of the locks at Widnes, and lived at Peaketh, near to the Ferry Inn, ad- joining the Golf Links. He earned 25s one week (when on night duty) and 24s when on day duty. He Was not a man of drunken habits,but he had a bad temper end wee horribly jealous of his wife and cruel to her and the Children. In June he picked up a pair of scissore and stabbed his wife in the neck with them but she pushed him back and prevented the infliction of a deep wounthwhile on another occasion he poured some hot tea over her head and face. He was always calling her filthy names, and once he threatened to kill her and the children,and threatened to throw them into the river when the tide was up. He added that it would take their bodies away and they would never be seen again. On another occasion he gave the sausages prepared for tea to the cat,and stuck a knife into the wall, saving that he was going to murder all in the house. He drew the knife across his own throat, and said he would commit suicide but, unfortunately for the complainant, he used the wrong side of the knife. (Laughter.) Defendant had also threatened to follow the example of the Deeming murderer at Rainhill who murdered his wife and children and then put them 'under the hearth- stone, He had kicked and struck her on several occasions, and one day he put some blacking In his son's eyes because the boots were not properly cleaned. Because the boy acci- dentally dropped his school slate on his father's fon, the de- fendant got hold of him by the neck and swung him round —Defendant denied the changes of cruelty. The Bench granted a separation order with 5s a week alimony, and defer dant was ordered to pay the costs.</p>
