        <p>SLAUGHTER-ROUSIC BYELANS. NOTIOR IS 4,BREBY GIFBN that the URBAN DIS- TRICT COUNCIL OF RUNCORN, in the County of Chester, intend to apply to the Local Government Board on January 2nd, 19C3, for confirmation of the Byelaws made and adopted by the said Council for the licensing, registering, and inspection of Slaughter-houses, for preventing cruelty therein, for keeping the same in a cleanly and proper state, for removing filth at least once in every twenty-four hours, and requiring such slaughter-houses to be provided with a sufficient supply of water. A copy of such bye-laws will be kept at the office 61 the said t °anon in Waterloo-road, Run- corn, open for the inspection of persons interested, and a painted copy thereof will be furnished to every person apply- ing for the same on payment of the sum of one shilling for each copy. R. MARSHALL, Secretary. Dated this 20th day of November, 1902.</p>
        <p>COUNTY OF CHESTER. BYE-LAWS AS TO PLACING, DEPOSITING OR LEAVING BROKEN GLASS, Sze ON RIG HW AYS AND OTHER rußLic nacEs.BEDGE CUTTINGS, &amp;c., AND ANNOYANCE ON SUNDAY. NOTICX IS HEREBY GIVEN, that the Cheshire C ,unty Cmucil. at a Meeting held at Chester Castle on Thursday, ail 13. h day of November, 1902. made the following additional BYE-LAWS for the good rule and government of the County, viz. :•—• PLACING OR DEPOSITING BROKEN GLASS, &amp;C., ON HIGHWAYS AND OTHER PUBLIC PLACES. (a) No person shall vice, deposit, or leave, any article cf glass or any broken lass, Of other sharp substance not being rd material, on any high was or public place, in a position in which the some is likely to cause danger or annoyance to passengers or damage to property. HEDGE CUTTINGS, &amp;C. No person shall suffer any cuttings ot trees or hedges to remain upon any highway or public place, in a posi- tion in which the same are likely to cause danger or annoyance to passengers or damage to property, for a B longer period than one hour after sunset on the day upon which they shall have been cut. ANNOYANCE ON SUNDAY. No person on any pubic coach or other public vehicle for the annoyance of passengers, or any coach, brake, waggonette, or other vehicle hired or used for the conveyance of pleasure parties and the like, shall en Sundays blow any horn,or use any other noisy instru- ment, or make, or combine with any other person or persons io make any loud singing or outcry while passing through a.y town, vi'lage or hamlet, or by any Church, Chapcl, or otber Vane of Puhlic Worship, to the annoyance or interruption of residents within any such town, village, or hamlet, or any persons worshlpping in any such Church, Chapel, sr other place ot Public Worship. Nothing in this Bye-Law shall prevent the use of a horn in a reasonable manner by one person on a coach r,r vehicle. PENALTIES. Any person who shall offend against any of the fore- going Bye-Laws shall be liable on summary conviction for each offence to a penalty not exceeding forty shillings. AND NOTICE IS HEREBY FURTHER GIVEN that copies of the sad Bye-Laws have been sealed with the Ccrperate Seal of the said County Counwl. and sent to the Secretlry of State, and otherwiw published as re- quired by the Statutes in that behalf. REGINALD POTTS, Clerk to the Cheshire County Council. Chd''tor, 17th November, 1902.</p>
