        <p>CHRONICITY DOES NOT PREVENT THE CURE OF DEAFNESS. The summing up of the first half of this year's work at the Dronet Institute for the Deg shows some remark- able results from the treatment of apparently hopeless cases of defective hearing and of diseases of the ears. Radical cures are thscribed at the ages of 65, 75, and even 86, and in cases where deafuess had persisted. despite all previous treatment, for 10, 20, and even 40 years. The names and addresses of these oases can be obtained from " The Journal for the Deaf," which de- votes its last issae to a full description of the treatment of deafness by local application—the Dr-met methods. A free copy of this publication (with " Supplement,' describing upwards of 40 cases cured during the past month) can be obtained by addressing the Secretary, Prouet Institute, 72, Regent's Park-road, London, N.W. With the " Journal "is also enclosed a Report Form, by means of which any sufferer can obtain advice free by correspondence and for applying the treatment at home. The case in which boy of four, of Southgate was the subject of an application for a summons for assault- ing a women, came before the Highgate Bench again on Wednesday. As the boy was too young to be charged his brother, aged twelve, was summoned for inciting him to commit the assault. The Bench dismissed the summons on the ground that no one could incite a per- son to commit an act which the law said be could not commit. Just before closing time on Wednesday afternoon a Stock jobber, who is said to have substantial interests in the Kaffir market, shot himself dead in a lavatory under the London Stock Exchange. Fie was identified as Mr Repo!, a gentleman uf about 45 years of &amp;gr.. „scid wer-ko own member of the Stock Exchange.</p>
